WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4570.

2
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.000
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.000
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alright, it's recording.

4
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Okay, so today is April 15th.

5
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.667
It's Friday April 15th, 2016 and I'm here with Ms. Jimmie Lee Williams Riggs.

6
00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:12.667
Lee is her middle name I just found out.

7
00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:18.000
Today we're at the Imperial . . . are interviewing at the Imperial Calcasieu Museum in Lake Charles [Louisiana].

8
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.333
We're so happy you came in from Houston [Texas] today.

9
00:00:21.333 --> 00:00:26.667
Thank you so much for driving and for taking the time to talk to us today.

10
00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:28.333
<v JIMMIE LEE RIGGS>Thank you for asking me.

11
00:00:28.333 --> 00:00:28.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

12
00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:30.000
<v RIGGS>I appreciate it.

13
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.625
<v ARSENEAULT>You been . . . They're like, "Ms. Jimmie Lee.

14
00:00:32.625 --> 00:00:35.625
You got to talk to Ms. Jimmie Lee."

15
00:00:35.625 --> 00:00:40.500
So we're so happy that you could make the time to come out.

16
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:44.625
So for the recording, could you please state your full name?

17
00:00:44.625 --> 00:00:45.000
Just . . .

18
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.500
<v RIGGS>Jimmie Lee Williams Riggs.

19
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:46.875
<v ARSENEAULT>Wonderful.

20
00:00:46.875 --> 00:00:52.125
And so we'll start with when were you born and where were you born?

21
00:00:52.125 --> 00:00:56.625
<v RIGGS>I was born November the 28th, 1936 in Mossville, Louisiana, at home.

22
00:00:56.625 --> 00:01:00.375
<v ARSENEAULT>Can you tell me a little bit about your birth?

23
00:01:00.375 --> 00:01:02.625
Like was there a midwife or?

24
00:01:02.625 --> 00:01:06.000
<v RIGGS>There was a midwife whose name was Aunt [Jannie?].

25
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.500
That I was told.

26
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.500
I think she was the midwife of Mossville.

27
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:19.875
The funniest thing about my birth was I have two brothers, Rudolph and Curtis, and they are eight [and nine] years older than I am.

28
00:01:19.875 --> 00:01:29.625
My mom sent them to get Aunt Jannie, and they stopped at the sugarcane factory [Riggs clarifies this was a mill] where they were making syrup.

29
00:01:29.625 --> 00:01:34.500
Finally they did get Aunt Jannie and I was just delivered in November.

30
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.125
<v ARSENEAULT>So they took a little sugar break?

31
00:01:37.125 --> 00:01:39.000
<v RIGGS>Yeah they detoured. [laughs]

32
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:39.985
<v ARSENEAULT>That's funny.

33
00:01:39.985 --> 00:01:50.815
There's so much I want to ask just about your birth and the sugarcane mill, but where in Mossville were you born?

34
00:01:50.815 --> 00:01:53.277
Do you remember the house?

35
00:01:53.277 --> 00:01:54.262
<v RIGGS>Yes, yes.

36
00:01:54.262 --> 00:01:59.185
You want me to give you a description of where . . .?

37
00:01:59.185 --> 00:01:59.677
<v ARSENEAULT>[agrees]

38
00:01:59.677 --> 00:02:00.169
<v RIGGS>Okay.

39
00:02:00.169 --> 00:02:08.538
I was born west of the Kansas City Southern Railroad Track right across from the masonic hall.

40
00:02:08.538 --> 00:02:14.938
Maybe a half a block from where the school was, Mossville Elementary School.

41
00:02:14.938 --> 00:02:15.923
That's it.

42
00:02:15.923 --> 00:02:19.369
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about your house?

43
00:02:19.369 --> 00:02:22.323
<v RIGGS>I remember that it was white.

44
00:02:22.323 --> 00:02:24.292
My daddy built houses.

45
00:02:24.292 --> 00:02:26.262
That's what he did.

46
00:02:26.262 --> 00:02:28.231
He was a carpenter.

47
00:02:28.231 --> 00:02:33.154
It had . . . You walked in . . . It was a living room,

48
00:02:33.154 --> 00:02:39.062
and then behind that was a kitchen-dining room like, and two bedrooms.

49
00:02:39.062 --> 00:02:43.000
Houses at that time did not have hallways.

50
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:47.923
You just went from one room to the next room.

51
00:02:47.923 --> 00:02:48.908
No hallways.

52
00:02:48.908 --> 00:02:57.769
You walk into the house and then you had a wall, and then you was in the kitchen.

53
00:02:57.769 --> 00:03:10.077
Then to the left was a bedroom with a door, and then a bedroom with a door, and a front porch, and a back porch.

54
00:03:10.077 --> 00:03:15.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did the neighborhood where you lived have a name?

55
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.732
<v RIGGS>No, not really.

56
00:03:16.732 --> 00:03:17.887
Not really.

57
00:03:17.887 --> 00:03:24.239
Mossville was known . . . Where I lived was just known as Mossville.

58
00:03:24.239 --> 00:03:27.127
We lived on old 90.

59
00:03:27.127 --> 00:03:29.437
The old Highway 90.

60
00:03:29.437 --> 00:03:38.099
It was where . . . Well I don't know if you want to go back that far.

61
00:03:38.099 --> 00:03:51.958
It was maybe about half a mile from the Sanctified Church, and maybe a mile from the Methodist Church, which is now Prater Road.

62
00:03:51.958 --> 00:04:00.042
That was like the center of Mossville because eventually the recreation was [built] there.

63
00:04:00.042 --> 00:04:06.972
The swimming pool first, and then the recreation [center], and the school.

64
00:04:06.972 --> 00:04:10.437
The first, original school was there.

65
00:04:10.437 --> 00:04:15.634
Basically that's the part of Mossville I lived in.

66
00:04:15.634 --> 00:04:19.099
<v ARSENEAULT>So you were born in 1936.

67
00:04:19.099 --> 00:04:19.676
<v RIGGS>Yes.

68
00:04:19.676 --> 00:04:20.831
<v ARSENEAULT>In Mossville.

69
00:04:20.831 --> 00:04:21.986
<v RIGGS>In . . .[yes].

70
00:04:21.986 --> 00:04:24.873
<v ARSENEAULT>So you're an original resident.

71
00:04:24.873 --> 00:04:33.535
What was it . . . because I'm sure you've seen a lot of changes throughout the years.

72
00:04:33.535 --> 00:04:37.000
What was it like then?

73
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:42.199
<v RIGGS>Well during . . . At that time many people in Mossville had cars.

74
00:04:42.199 --> 00:04:44.089
We had a car.

75
00:04:44.089 --> 00:04:49.760
I don't know how good it was, but we had one [laughs].

76
00:04:49.760 --> 00:05:00.158
People that did have cars took the other people to Westlake, [Louisiana] or to Lake Charles, or to Sulphur [Louisiana] for shopping.

77
00:05:00.158 --> 00:05:05.356
There were still . . . I remember one family that had a wagon.

78
00:05:05.356 --> 00:05:14.336
He used the wagon to go Westlake to get his groceries, and he did that for a long time.

79
00:05:14.336 --> 00:05:16.699
I remember him doing that.

80
00:05:16.699 --> 00:05:21.425
I was a big girl, and he still doing that.

81
00:05:21.425 --> 00:05:32.295
He was the only person I knew that would hook up his wagon on Fridays and he'd go to Westlake, get his groceries.

82
00:05:32.295 --> 00:05:35.130
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember who it was?

83
00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:37.021
<v RIGGS>Yes, Mr. Earthy Prater.

84
00:05:37.021 --> 00:05:38.438
He did that.

85
00:05:38.438 --> 00:05:40.329
<v ARSENEAULT>That's Ms. Vera's grandfather.

86
00:05:40.329 --> 00:05:42.219
<v RIGGS>That's Ms. Vera's grandfather.

87
00:05:42.219 --> 00:05:42.692
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

88
00:05:42.692 --> 00:05:43.164
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

89
00:05:43.164 --> 00:05:46.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I've heard about Mr. Earthy.

90
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:52.341
<v RIGGS>Mr. Earthy, and he was some person . . . A person that I remember that well.

91
00:05:52.341 --> 00:05:55.511
He didn't stop because people had cars.

92
00:05:55.511 --> 00:06:01.399
I don't know if he ever owned a car, but he did that.

93
00:06:01.399 --> 00:06:05.022
He had children that was my age, too.

94
00:06:05.022 --> 00:06:07.740
Tommy and Wilford was my age.

95
00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:08.646
His children.

96
00:06:08.646 --> 00:06:11.363
That sticks with me of him.

97
00:06:11.363 --> 00:06:15.439
The churches every other Sunday, like the ministers rotated.

98
00:06:15.439 --> 00:06:21.327
Like the Methodists, say the first Sunday, the Methodist church had a minister.

99
00:06:21.327 --> 00:06:29.933
So the people from the other churches would probably come at eleven o'clock church because they didn't have minister.

100
00:06:29.933 --> 00:06:34.009
They would have maybe Bible study in the morning.

101
00:06:34.009 --> 00:06:39.444
Then on a second Sunday maybe the Sanctified church had a minister,

102
00:06:39.444 --> 00:06:47.596
so we would go to that church on that day and we would just have a Bible study.

103
00:06:47.596 --> 00:06:49.861
That made the community close.

104
00:06:49.861 --> 00:06:51.220
We were close.

105
00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:57.561
Even though I was Methodist I went to all those churches back and forth.

106
00:06:57.561 --> 00:07:04.354
I would go from one . . . Whatever was going on we would go to that church.

107
00:07:04.354 --> 00:07:07.978
To me that brought the community close together.

108
00:07:07.978 --> 00:07:15.224
It wasn't that you were Catholic and I was Methodist and he was . . . that didn't happen.

109
00:07:15.224 --> 00:07:18.848
Even though we went to those churches it

110
00:07:18.848 --> 00:07:23.830
<c.p>wasn't that you thought of that church as a different church.

111
00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:27.000
I think that brought Mossville together.

112
00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:28.564
I don't know.

113
00:07:28.564 --> 00:07:31.170
It was a black settlement.

114
00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:41.074
So basically you saw other people, but you left the community or else other people came into the community.

115
00:07:41.074 --> 00:07:44.723
But it was basically a black settlement.

116
00:07:44.723 --> 00:07:49.415
People that had settled . . . People of color settled Mossville.

117
00:07:49.415 --> 00:07:51.500
I found that out.

118
00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:55.149
That they came to Mossville and settled.

119
00:07:55.149 --> 00:07:59.840
I'm sure that's how it became the black community.

120
00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:05.053
Eventually it changed, but it was a complete black community.

121
00:08:05.053 --> 00:08:07.138
<v ARSENEAULT>What about your parents?

122
00:08:07.138 --> 00:08:10.266
Just going back a little bit.

123
00:08:10.266 --> 00:08:16.000
Like do you know how . . . when they came to Mossville?

124
00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:17.722
<v RIGGS>They were born there.

125
00:08:17.722 --> 00:08:19.875
<v ARSENEAULT>Your parents were born there?

126
00:08:19.875 --> 00:08:20.306
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

127
00:08:20.306 --> 00:08:25.042
My daddy was born . . . He was really not born in Mossville.

128
00:08:25.042 --> 00:08:28.486
He was born on the other Highway 90.

129
00:08:28.486 --> 00:08:30.208
<v ARSENEAULT>So more toward Westlake?

130
00:08:30.208 --> 00:08:31.069
Or Sulphur?

131
00:08:31.069 --> 00:08:31.500
Or . . .?

132
00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:32.792
<v RIGGS>Closer to Westlake.

133
00:08:32.792 --> 00:08:33.222
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

134
00:08:33.222 --> 00:08:37.958
<v RIGGS>Near . . . He was . . . They were near the old Dunham Price place.

135
00:08:37.958 --> 00:08:46.139
But they face the old . . . That would be the new 90 because we were on the old, old 90.

136
00:08:46.139 --> 00:08:47.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

137
00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:50.111
<v RIGGS>So that would be the new 90.

138
00:08:50.111 --> 00:08:52.778
So he was in that area.

139
00:08:52.778 --> 00:08:55.000
He was not in Mossville.

140
00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:58.556
I guess he was considered Westlake I think.

141
00:08:58.556 --> 00:09:00.333
I'm not really sure.

142
00:09:00.333 --> 00:09:04.333
I know that was a mailing address for them.

143
00:09:04.333 --> 00:09:07.444
But my mother was born in Mossville.

144
00:09:07.444 --> 00:09:09.222
She was an original.

145
00:09:09.222 --> 00:09:10.556
<v ARSENEAULT>Your parents' names?

146
00:09:10.556 --> 00:09:13.667
<v RIGGS>My mother's name was Anna Garrett McNiel.

147
00:09:13.667 --> 00:09:15.444
<v ARSENEAULT>Can you spell McNiel?

148
00:09:15.444 --> 00:09:15.889
<v RIGGS>M-C-N-I-E-L.

149
00:09:15.889 --> 00:09:16.333
<v ARSENEAULT>N-I-E-L?

150
00:09:16.333 --> 00:09:16.778
<v RIGGS>[agrees]

151
00:09:16.778 --> 00:09:19.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And Garrett's her maiden name?

152
00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:19.444
<v RIGGS>[agrees]

153
00:09:19.444 --> 00:09:21.222
<v ARSENEAULT>And it’s two r's?

154
00:09:21.222 --> 00:09:21.667
<v RIGGS>G-A-R-R-E-T-T.

155
00:09:21.667 --> 00:09:22.556
<v ARSENEAULT>Two t's.

156
00:09:22.556 --> 00:09:24.333
Okay, Anna Garrett McNiel.

157
00:09:24.333 --> 00:09:27.000
She was born in Mossville?

158
00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:29.821
<v RIGGS>She was born in Mossville.

159
00:09:29.821 --> 00:09:39.974
Her father was born State Line, Mississippi on his father's plantation, and he kept him and raised him.

160
00:09:39.974 --> 00:09:40.538
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

161
00:09:40.538 --> 00:09:45.615
<v RIGGS>He went from . . . I couldn't find him from there,

162
00:09:45.615 --> 00:09:57.462
but he went from State Line to the normal college for colored in Baton Rouge [Louisiana] which is now Southern [University].

163
00:09:57.462 --> 00:10:01.410
He was the first teacher in Mossville.

164
00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:01.974
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

165
00:10:01.974 --> 00:10:04.231
What was his name?

166
00:10:04.231 --> 00:10:05.923
<v RIGGS>Berkeley C. Garrett.

167
00:10:05.923 --> 00:10:06.487
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

168
00:10:06.487 --> 00:10:11.000
<v RIGGS>And I got some information off of that.

169
00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:18.897
He was like paid thirty-five dollars a day [Riggs clarifies it was per month].

170
00:10:18.897 --> 00:10:19.462
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

171
00:10:19.462 --> 00:10:21.154
<v RIGGS>And that’s it.

172
00:10:21.154 --> 00:10:32.436
And I got this . . . It came from a thesis someone had written and he was like . . . Let me tell you . . .

173
00:10:32.436 --> 00:10:33.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Sure!

174
00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:33.564
Yeah.

175
00:10:33.564 --> 00:10:37.513
<v RIGGS>I'm going to get it out here.

176
00:10:37.513 --> 00:10:40.897
I'm going to say it wrong.

177
00:10:40.897 --> 00:10:44.846
<v ARSENEAULT>Got some papers with information on it.

178
00:10:44.846 --> 00:10:47.103
First teacher in Mossville.

179
00:10:47.103 --> 00:10:48.231
How wonderful.

180
00:10:48.231 --> 00:10:48.795
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

181
00:10:48.795 --> 00:10:53.308
He gave the land for the first school.

182
00:10:53.308 --> 00:10:54.436
An acre.

183
00:10:54.436 --> 00:10:55.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

184
00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:57.821
<v RIGGS>Let me . . . It said here.

185
00:10:57.821 --> 00:11:06.282
This . . . I'm going to tell you what I . . . I don't want to read it though.

186
00:11:06.282 --> 00:11:17.000
It said that he acquired a teaching position in Shoat’s Prairie and he taught there for many years.

187
00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:19.344
<v ARSENEAULT>Because that's what Mossville was called?

188
00:11:19.344 --> 00:11:21.297
<v RIGGS>That's what it was called.

189
00:11:21.297 --> 00:11:27.547
And he was . . . At that that time he was the leading colored teacher in the parish.

190
00:11:27.547 --> 00:11:27.938
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

191
00:11:27.938 --> 00:11:33.016
<v RIGGS>According to the parish teachers’ payroll he was paid thirty-five dollars a month.

192
00:11:33.016 --> 00:11:38.875
He taught many generations of students in Calcasieu Parish, and he lived to be seventy-five.

193
00:11:38.875 --> 00:11:44.344
But he gave the first acre of land for the first school in Mossville.

194
00:11:44.344 --> 00:11:46.297
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was the first school?

195
00:11:46.297 --> 00:11:54.891
<v RIGGS>It's over where the swimming pool and . . . Well I guess it's between the Kansas City Railroad and Prater Road . . . Do you understand?

196
00:11:54.891 --> 00:11:55.281
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

197
00:11:55.281 --> 00:12:00.359
So I'm thinking where the Rigmaiden Center . . . It was behind the Rigmaiden Center?

198
00:12:00.359 --> 00:12:02.312
<v RIGGS>That's where it . . . Rigmaiden Center.

199
00:12:02.312 --> 00:12:04.266
It was in that area.

200
00:12:04.266 --> 00:12:05.438
That's the area.

201
00:12:05.438 --> 00:12:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That's the area.

202
00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:07.987
<v RIGGS>That's the area.

203
00:12:07.987 --> 00:12:09.961
<v ARSENEAULT>He gave that acre right there?

204
00:12:09.961 --> 00:12:14.566
<v RIGGS>He gave that acre for the school and then from there they bought something.

205
00:12:14.566 --> 00:12:17.855
Then he also owned the land where the recreation is.

206
00:12:17.855 --> 00:12:20.158
They sold that to them [Ward 4].

207
00:12:20.158 --> 00:12:24.763
He was a land owner and sold a lot of land back and forth.

208
00:12:24.763 --> 00:12:25.750
<v ARSENEAULT>I'm so curious.

209
00:12:25.750 --> 00:12:29.368
So he was born on his father's plantation in Mississippi or . . .?

210
00:12:29.368 --> 00:12:30.684
<v RIGGS>In State Line, Mississippi.

211
00:12:30.684 --> 00:12:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>State Line, Mississippi.

212
00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:36.638
<v RIGGS>It keeps coming up . . . Well I was told by my mom that it was State Line.

213
00:12:36.638 --> 00:12:40.406
But you know when you're doing it, it will be State Line, Mississippi.

214
00:12:40.406 --> 00:12:42.145
Then it'll join State Line, Georgia.

215
00:12:42.145 --> 00:12:45.913
But it's Mississippi because on some of the papers that he filled out . . .

216
00:12:45.913 --> 00:12:46.783
<v ARSENEAULT>He puts Mississippi?

217
00:12:46.783 --> 00:12:49.391
<v RIGGS>He put Mississippi because he could write, you know.

218
00:12:49.391 --> 00:12:49.681
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

219
00:12:49.681 --> 00:12:49.971
<v RIGGS>So . . .

220
00:12:49.971 --> 00:12:52.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you know about him?

221
00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:53.244
<v RIGGS>That's all I know.

222
00:12:53.244 --> 00:12:54.489
He died in '35.

223
00:12:54.489 --> 00:12:56.044
<v ARSENEAULT>Right before you were born.

224
00:12:56.044 --> 00:12:56.978
<v RIGGS>At seventy-five [agrees].

225
00:12:56.978 --> 00:12:58.533
Right before I was born.

226
00:12:58.533 --> 00:13:04.133
Did not know he . . . Well my mother didn't know anything about his mother, but he was born after . . .

227
00:13:04.133 --> 00:13:04.444
<v ARSENEAULT>Nothing?

228
00:13:04.444 --> 00:13:07.867
You don't know what she did or what her name was?

229
00:13:07.867 --> 00:13:08.178
<v RIGGS>Nothing.

230
