694
00:34:00.550 --> 00:34:05.060
<v RIGGS>No, I don't because I always remember him having a store.

695
00:34:05.060 --> 00:34:07.110
<v ARSENEAULT>So it was there when . . .?

696
00:34:07.110 --> 00:34:08.750
<v RIGGS>It was there [agrees].

697
00:34:08.750 --> 00:34:09.980
It was there.

698
00:34:09.980 --> 00:34:12.030
<v ARSENEAULT>What did it look like?

699
00:34:12.030 --> 00:34:13.670
Can you describe it?

700
00:34:13.670 --> 00:34:16.130
<v RIGGS>It was . . . His house was there.

701
00:34:16.130 --> 00:34:19.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So he lived behind it or . . .?

702
00:34:19.000 --> 00:34:21.923
<v RIGGS>No, he lived on the side of it.

703
00:34:21.923 --> 00:34:24.114
<v ARSENEAULT>On the side of it, okay.

704
00:34:24.114 --> 00:34:24.480
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

705
00:34:24.480 --> 00:34:28.498
His house was there and the store jetted out the side.

706
00:34:28.498 --> 00:34:36.900
It was a building . . . It was a long building kind of like this, and you could go from the house into the store.

707
00:34:36.900 --> 00:34:38.362
It was pretty large.

708
00:34:38.362 --> 00:34:43.841
It had a meat counter, and then it had . . . I'm trying to think about it.

709
00:34:43.841 --> 00:34:45.668
Everything was behind the counter.

710
00:34:45.668 --> 00:34:51.878
Maybe some potatoes or some stuff was in front of it, but everything was behind the counter.

711
00:34:51.878 --> 00:34:58.089
Like if you needed something then you would get it and they would bring it to you.

712
00:34:58.089 --> 00:35:00.646
Or he would bring it to you.

713
00:35:00.646 --> 00:35:03.568
I know that he . . . What would you say?

714
00:35:03.568 --> 00:35:07.952
He . . . People got their food on credit; that he ran a bill.

715
00:35:07.952 --> 00:35:19.277
Like you would shop whatever you wanted and if you didn't pay for it that day he would write it down and give them a copy and he kept a copy.

716
00:35:19.277 --> 00:35:25.852
Growing up that's where I spent most of my time over there with my uncle in the store.

717
00:35:25.852 --> 00:35:28.410
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things did you do?

718
00:35:28.410 --> 00:35:30.236
<v RIGGS>Basically eat cheese, but I . . .

719
00:35:30.236 --> 00:35:32.793
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, he didn’t put you to work?

720
00:35:32.793 --> 00:35:34.255
<v RIGGS>Sometime I would sweep.

721
00:35:34.255 --> 00:35:37.908
And at that time they had tops on the sodas.

722
00:35:37.908 --> 00:35:41.926
The sodas had a top that you had to open up.

723
00:35:41.926 --> 00:35:43.022
The little top.

724
00:35:43.022 --> 00:35:43.387
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

725
00:35:43.387 --> 00:35:55.077
<v RIGGS>And a lot of times they would be out in front of the little store where people had bought sodas and I'd pick up the tops and work around in the store.

726
00:35:55.077 --> 00:35:55.808
Not much.

727
00:35:55.808 --> 00:35:58.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You were just hanging out.

728
00:35:58.000 --> 00:35:59.811
<v RIGGS>I was just hanging out.

729
00:35:59.811 --> 00:36:01.259
<v ARSENEAULT>[Just into the habit?]

730
00:36:01.259 --> 00:36:03.070
<v RIGGS>I was his oldest niece.

731
00:36:03.070 --> 00:36:05.968
I was the first niece in the family.

732
00:36:05.968 --> 00:36:06.692
<v ARSENEAULT>The favorite.

733
00:36:06.692 --> 00:36:07.054
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

734
00:36:07.054 --> 00:36:11.400
So I just . . . I would go over and hangout at the store.

735
00:36:11.400 --> 00:36:13.935
But I know that he did that,

736
00:36:13.935 --> 00:36:21.903
and I guess once people got their money or whatever then they came back and they paid him because I was there.

737
00:36:21.903 --> 00:36:27.335
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he ever have to go and like, "Hey. . ." remind people or anything like that?

738
00:36:27.335 --> 00:36:28.784
<v RIGGS>I don't think so.

739
00:36:28.784 --> 00:36:30.232
I don't think so.

740
00:36:30.232 --> 00:36:32.405
I think they paid him [laughs].

741
00:36:32.405 --> 00:36:33.854
I don't think so.

742
00:36:33.854 --> 00:36:39.286
Basically I can't say what happened with other people, but I do know that happened.

743
00:36:39.286 --> 00:36:41.097
That's how he did that.

744
00:36:41.097 --> 00:36:45.443
I don't know if everybody had credit because it was little books.

745
00:36:45.443 --> 00:36:48.341
It was little books with a double receipt.

746
00:36:48.341 --> 00:36:54.859
And I . . . don't know this, but I imagine he had where it'll . . . Mine had my name on it.

747
00:36:54.859 --> 00:36:55.946
Those little books.

748
00:36:55.946 --> 00:36:59.205
I can just see them by the cash register.

749
00:36:59.205 --> 00:37:01.016
<v ARSENEAULT>Just kept them all ordered.

750
00:37:01.016 --> 00:37:01.378
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

751
00:37:01.378 --> 00:37:03.189
<v ARSENEAULT>Kept them organized and everything.

752
00:37:03.189 --> 00:37:05.000
What was he like?

753
00:37:05.000 --> 00:37:12.784
<v RIGGS>Well, he might have . . . To other people he might have portrayed being bully.

754
00:37:12.784 --> 00:37:14.581
A rough person.

755
00:37:14.581 --> 00:37:25.359
I guess he was all business, but when I say bully he was like a no frills, maybe.

756
00:37:25.359 --> 00:37:30.150
But he was, when you did know him.

757
00:37:30.150 --> 00:37:36.737
But he . . . All I say was he was a business man.

758
00:37:36.737 --> 00:37:40.928
If it was business, it was business.

759
00:37:40.928 --> 00:37:46.317
And he was a steward in the Methodist church.

760
00:37:46.317 --> 00:37:48.114
His mother was

761
00:37:48.114 --> 00:37:51.108
<c.p>another grandmother that I knew.

762
00:37:51.108 --> 00:37:58.892
Remember I didn't have my mother's and his mother lived . . . I knew her.

763
00:37:58.892 --> 00:38:01.287
He had no children.

764
00:38:01.287 --> 00:38:04.281
He didn't have any children.

765
00:38:04.281 --> 00:38:11.467
So basically he was a . . . I would say maybe a community leader.

766
00:38:11.467 --> 00:38:11.667
He was on committees and did things in the community.

767
00:38:11.467 --> 00:38:17.455
<v UNKNOWN>[. . .?]

768
00:38:17.455 --> 00:38:18.653
<v RIGGS>Oh yeah!

769
00:38:18.653 --> 00:38:28.832
In the back of the property where he lived they had a baseball field. [Coughs] Excuse me.

770
00:38:28.832 --> 00:38:35.419
On Sundays the men in the . . . Saturdays and Sunday played baseball.

771
00:38:35.419 --> 00:38:40.210
That was a thing that black people did.

772
00:38:40.210 --> 00:38:41.407
Then they . . .

773
00:38:41.407 --> 00:38:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did they have a league?

774
00:38:45.000 --> 00:38:48.500
<v RIGGS>Well I was about to tell you . . .

775
00:38:48.500 --> 00:38:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

776
00:38:49.000 --> 00:38:50.500
<v RIGGS>Wasn't a league.

777
00:38:50.500 --> 00:38:54.500
They would . . . My brothers played on that, too.

778
00:38:54.500 --> 00:38:57.500
These young men formed a team.

779
00:38:57.500 --> 00:39:03.500
They played like in Vinton [Louisiana], in Lake Charles . . . Different little towns.

780
00:39:03.500 --> 00:39:13.000
On Sunday like people from Lake Charles would come and the field was behind his house on the property.

781
00:39:13.000 --> 00:39:17.000
So that was . . . And it was just open.

782
00:39:17.000 --> 00:39:20.000
It wasn't no charge or anything.

783
00:39:20.000 --> 00:39:25.500
People would . . . after church people would go to the baseball game.

784
00:39:25.500 --> 00:39:29.000
Other people would like, say, sell sodas.

785
00:39:29.000 --> 00:39:31.500
They would probably sell beer.

786
00:39:31.500 --> 00:39:34.000
I guess they sold beer.

787
00:39:34.000 --> 00:39:36.000
I don't really remember.

788
00:39:36.000 --> 00:39:38.000
I'm sure they did.

789
00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:46.500
Or people had little tents and so people would buy stuff like for instance, I don't know

790
00:39:46.500 --> 00:39:48.500
<c.p>if they had popcorn.

791
00:39:48.500 --> 00:39:50.000
Sodas and stuff.

792
00:39:50.000 --> 00:39:52.500
So he did do that.

793
00:39:52.500 --> 00:39:54.000
He had that.

794
00:39:54.000 --> 00:39:56.500
That was the baseball field.

795
00:39:56.500 --> 00:40:03.000
That lasted up until probably I was in high school in the 50s.

796
00:40:03.000 --> 00:40:06.000
<v ARSENEAULT>When did his store close?

797
00:40:06.000 --> 00:40:09.750
<v RIGGS>His store closed I would say in the 60s.

798
00:40:09.750 --> 00:40:11.417
Don't you think so?

799
00:40:11.417 --> 00:40:13.083
<v JEFFERY WILLIAMS>Probably in the 70s.

800
00:40:13.083 --> 00:40:14.333
<v RIGGS>In the 70s?

801
00:40:14.333 --> 00:40:16.000
Yeah, maybe the 70s.

802
00:40:16.000 --> 00:40:17.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you know why?

803
00:40:17.667 --> 00:40:18.917
<v RIGGS>He was old.

804
00:40:18.917 --> 00:40:19.333
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

805
00:40:19.333 --> 00:40:20.583
<v RIGGS>He was old.

806
00:40:20.583 --> 00:40:26.000
It was probably 70s because we were in Houston when it closed.

807
00:40:26.000 --> 00:40:27.244
<v ARSENEAULT>And what's your name?

808
00:40:27.244 --> 00:40:29.733
We can get your name on the recording.

809
00:40:29.733 --> 00:40:30.356
<v WILLIAMS>Jeffery Williams.

810
00:40:30.356 --> 00:40:30.978
<v ARSENEAULT>Jeffery Williams.

811
00:40:30.978 --> 00:40:33.467
This is Ms. Jimmie Lee's oldest son or . . .?

812
00:40:33.467 --> 00:40:34.089
<v RIGGS>Only son.

813
00:40:34.089 --> 00:40:34.711
Only child.

814
00:40:34.711 --> 00:40:35.333
<v ARSENEAULT>Only son.

815
00:40:35.333 --> 00:40:35.956
Only child.

816
00:40:35.956 --> 00:40:37.200
Her pride and joy.

817
00:40:37.200 --> 00:40:37.511
<v RIGGS>Yes.

818
00:40:37.511 --> 00:40:38.756
So how did we . . .?

819
00:40:38.756 --> 00:40:40.933
We just stumbled up on Ira, huh?

820
00:40:40.933 --> 00:40:41.244
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

821
00:40:41.244 --> 00:40:42.800
I'm so glad we did.

822
00:40:42.800 --> 00:40:47.156
People tell me about Ira and Mr. Rougeau and when I ask about stores.

823
00:40:47.156 --> 00:40:49.022
<v RIGGS>Mr. Rougeau had a store, yeah.

824
00:40:49.022 --> 00:40:51.200
He had a store on Prater Road.

825
00:40:51.200 --> 00:40:51.511
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

826
00:40:51.511 --> 00:40:54.000
Was it the same time Mr. Ira?

827
00:40:54.000 --> 00:40:55.770
<v RIGGS>Yeah, it overlapped a little bit.

828
00:40:55.770 --> 00:40:56.951
<v ARSENEAULT>Around the same . . . Okay.

829
00:40:56.951 --> 00:40:57.246
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

830
00:40:57.246 --> 00:40:59.311
Ira was like one of the first.

831
00:40:59.311 --> 00:41:01.082
<v ARSENEAULT>Was Mr. Rougeau a white man?

832
00:41:01.082 --> 00:41:01.967
<v RIGGS>Yes, he was.

833
00:41:01.967 --> 00:41:05.213
<v ARSENEAULT>Was he one of the only white men in the area?

834
00:41:05.213 --> 00:41:07.279
Or did he live in the area?

835
00:41:07.279 --> 00:41:11.410
<v RIGGS>Mr. Rougeau came in probably . . . Let's just say it was probably in the 50s.

836
00:41:11.410 --> 00:41:12.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

837
00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:14.000
<v RIGGS>Late fifty . . . After probably '54.

838
00:41:14.000 --> 00:41:22.400
I don't want to put time on that, but I think I was in high school or out of high school.

839
00:41:22.400 --> 00:41:24.000
I don't really remember.

840
00:41:24.000 --> 00:41:26.400
But he was white or Italian.

841
00:41:26.400 --> 00:41:28.000
Yeah, he was white.

842
00:41:28.000 --> 00:41:29.200
He was there.

843
00:41:29.200 --> 00:41:30.800
He had his store.

844
00:41:30.800 --> 00:41:33.600
I'm trying to think of who else.

845
00:41:33.600 --> 00:41:35.600
<v ARSENEAULT>This was during the 50s.

846
00:41:35.600 --> 00:41:41.200
Was there any like racial tension or anything at the time in the area?

847
00:41:41.200 --> 00:41:43.600
<v RIGGS>Oh no. No, never had that.

848
00:41:43.600 --> 00:41:44.800
<v ARSENEAULT>Not in Mossville?

849
00:41:44.800 --> 00:41:51.200
What do you remember about that time like in the 60s and the protests and everything?

850
00:41:51.200 --> 00:41:53.600
<v RIGGS>Oh, I integrated McNeese [University], 1955.

851
00:41:53.600 --> 00:41:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You were the first class?

852
00:41:56.000 --> 00:41:56.951
<v RIGGS>In McNeese [agrees].

853
00:41:56.951 --> 00:41:57.268
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

854
00:41:57.268 --> 00:41:59.805
Okay, we're in . . . I want to keep chronological.

855
00:41:59.805 --> 00:42:00.756
<v RIGGS>Let’s go back.

856
00:42:00.756 --> 00:42:01.707
Let's go back.

857
00:42:01.707 --> 00:42:04.244
<v ARSENEAULT>We’re definitely going to go back to that.

858
00:42:04.244 --> 00:42:06.463
So we were talking about Mr. Ira.

859
00:42:06.463 --> 00:42:06.780
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

860
00:42:06.780 --> 00:42:08.366
<v ARSENEAULT>He was a community leader.

861
00:42:08.366 --> 00:42:09.000
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

862
00:42:09.000 --> 00:42:16.901
<v ARSENEAULT>When you were growing up who were some other like, people that were looked up to?

863
00:42:16.901 --> 00:42:19.864
<v RIGGS>Josh Rigmaiden, which was his brother-in-law.

864
00:42:19.864 --> 00:42:24.309
Jake Rigmaiden, Audrey Prater . . . I'm just trying to think.

865
00:42:24.309 --> 00:42:27.765
Maybe Jim Johnson [Riggs adds James Rigmaiden].

866
00:42:27.765 --> 00:42:34.679
<v ARSENEAULT>What were some things they did that people knew that they were the leaders?

867
00:42:34.679 --> 00:42:37.148
<v RIGGS>Let me stop and think.

868
00:42:37.148 --> 00:42:44.062
They . . . One of the things I know that Ira did, and probably Josh did,

869
00:42:44.062 --> 00:42:54.432
was like if people got a ticket or went to jail or something they would bail them out and go see.

870
00:42:54.432 --> 00:43:01.840
Well I guess having the store . . . and I'll have to think of what they did.

871
00:43:01.840 --> 00:43:10.728
It was like if there was something going on and something that need to be done . . . For instance,

872
00:43:10.728 --> 00:43:14.679
let's just say, when they got the recreation.

873
00:43:14.679 --> 00:43:17.148
That was in the 50s.

874
00:43:17.148 --> 00:43:18.630
I'm jumping ahead.

875
00:43:18.630 --> 00:43:29.988
They formed a committee and talked about it and went to Ward 4 and I guess presented that they wanted to do this.

876
00:43:29.988 --> 00:43:32.951
This was Jake and probably Ira.

877
00:43:32.951 --> 00:43:36.407
Josh was probably involved in that one.

878
00:43:36.407 --> 00:43:41.346
And at some point they did the water thing, too.

879
00:43:41.346 --> 00:43:42.827
I don't know.

880
00:43:42.827 --> 00:43:44.309
That was later.

881
00:43:44.309 --> 00:43:51.222
But basically, if something was going on and you needed people to do something,

882
00:43:51.222 --> 00:43:58.630
or go to the courthouse, or . . . Those people would get together and get it done.

883
00:43:58.630 --> 00:44:09.988
Or if they needed something at a church they would all get together and raise the money and do it for the church,

884
00:44:09.988 --> 00:44:11.963
or as a group.

885
00:44:11.963 --> 00:44:22.333
Right now I just can't think of no particular thing that they did do other than those things that I said.

886
00:44:22.333 --> 00:44:25.296
But for instance if . . . this project.

887
00:44:25.296 --> 00:44:29.741
If we . . . they would be . . . Step up to say,

888
00:44:29.741 --> 00:44:36.654
"Okay if you're going to take the town away, let's do something about it.

889
00:44:36.654 --> 00:44:42.086
Let's put something here so we'll now that we were here."

890
00:44:42.086 --> 00:44:45.049
They were that kind of people.

891
00:44:45.049 --> 00:44:49.000
They would step up to the cause.

892
00:44:49.000 --> 00:44:52.276
<v ARSENEAULT>Sounds like the community knew that they could count on . . .

893
00:44:52.276 --> 00:44:52.603
<v RIGGS>Yes.

894
00:44:52.603 --> 00:44:54.569
<v ARSENEAULT>Those certain people to do certain . . .

895
00:44:54.569 --> 00:44:54.897
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

896
00:44:54.897 --> 00:44:56.534
That they could count on.

897
00:44:56.534 --> 00:44:58.500
<v ARSENEAULT>So okay, those are community leaders.

898
00:44:58.500 --> 00:45:00.793
What about some of the gathering places?

899
00:45:00.793 --> 00:45:08.000
You mentioned a couple, but I know people mention certain things when they talk about where the young people hung out.

900
00:45:08.000 --> 00:45:13.194
<v RIGGS>Well I don't . . . To have hung out, that baseball thing was one.

901
00:45:13.194 --> 00:45:17.955
<v ARSENEAULT>So Sundays were a big day where the community kind of . . .

902
00:45:17.955 --> 00:45:18.388
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

903
00:45:18.388 --> 00:45:20.552
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it the whole community?

904
00:45:20.552 --> 00:45:21.851
<v RIGGS>Yeah a lot.

905
00:45:21.851 --> 00:45:26.612
Most of them, yeah, because somebody in their family played ball.

906
00:45:26.612 --> 00:45:27.045
Basically.

907
00:45:27.045 --> 00:45:31.806
<v ARSENEAULT>Were there any like pro . . . Did anyone go pro or anything?

908
00:45:31.806 --> 00:45:34.403
<v RIGGS>No, not that I know of.

909
00:45:34.403 --> 00:45:37.000
No, not that I know of.

910
00:45:37.000 --> 00:45:42.194
Now I don't really know . . . I think the lady's name was Martill.

911
00:45:42.194 --> 00:45:47.821
I can't remember what her last name was, but I remember her family.

912
00:45:47.821 --> 00:45:52.582
She had a little place like I guess a cafe or . . .

913
00:45:52.582 --> 00:45:53.448
<v ARSENEAULT>A canteen?

914
00:45:53.448 --> 00:45:54.313
<v RIGGS>A canteen.

915
00:45:54.313 --> 00:46:01.672
Yeah, and it was between Saprack Road and Mount Zion, or Mount Zion and the Methodist Church.

916
00:46:01.672 --> 00:46:06.866
You would go there and do sodas, and dance, and so forth.

917
00:46:06.866 --> 00:46:09.030
Then they had the rec.

918
00:46:09.030 --> 00:46:10.761
The recreation came early.

919
00:46:10.761 --> 00:46:14.657
And so in fact, I worked at the recreation.

920
00:46:14.657 --> 00:46:20.284
At first it was the old school and it was in the summer.

921
00:46:20.284 --> 00:46:28.507
And then it progressed from that and then they bought some land, which was some of my grandfather's land.

922
00:46:28.507 --> 00:46:33.269
All that land was bought from him where that school was.

923
00:46:33.269 --> 00:46:33.701
<v ARSENEAULT>From . . .?

924
00:46:33.701 --> 00:46:34.134
<v RIGGS>B.C.

925
00:46:34.134 --> 00:46:35.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

926
00:46:35.000 --> 00:46:35.428
<v RIGGS>Okay.

927
00:46:35.428 --> 00:46:42.697
And well that was a place that they could go down there and you could rent it.

928
00:46:42.697 --> 00:46:49.110
I don't know because it . . . after . . . between '52 and '55 I was in and out.

929
00:46:49.110 --> 00:46:52.103
I went to high school in Arkansas.

930
00:46:52.103 --> 00:46:55.952
Basically it wasn't too much that I went to.

931
00:46:55.952 --> 00:46:59.372
Now, maybe some people younger than me did.

932
00:46:59.372 --> 00:47:04.931
There were some clubs, but I didn't get to go to the clubs.

933
00:47:04.931 --> 00:47:09.634
I know the clubs, but I didn't go to the clubs

934
00:47:09.634 --> 00:47:13.483
<v ARSENEAULT>What were the clubs when you were growing up?

935
00:47:13.483 --> 00:47:17.759
<v RIGGS>Joy Hill, The Wagon Wheel, well I went to Valery's.

936
00:47:17.759 --> 00:47:20.324
Valery Montgomery had a nice place.

937
00:47:20.324 --> 00:47:22.034
<v ARSENEAULT>What was his called?

938
00:47:22.034 --> 00:47:23.745
<v RIGGS>It was just Vallire's.

939
00:47:23.745 --> 00:47:24.172
V-A-L-L-I-R-E-S.

940
00:47:24.172 --> 00:47:29.303
It was on Prater Road and he actually had a big place.

941
00:47:29.303 --> 00:47:31.441
And he had live entertainment.

942
00:47:31.441 --> 00:47:33.579
He like, brought in bands.

943
00:47:33.579 --> 00:47:37.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember some of the bands?

944
00:47:37.000 --> 00:47:38.521
<v RIGGS>Yeah Bobby Bland, B.B.

945
00:47:38.521 --> 00:47:38.901
King.

946
00:47:38.901 --> 00:47:39.662
That type.

947
00:47:39.662 --> 00:47:44.985
I think that the auditorium is still there unless they've just torn it down.

948
00:47:44.985 --> 00:47:52.589
Then it was some more . . . I mean maybe a couple more and they may have had some more after that,

949
00:47:52.589 --> 00:47:57.532
but they were . . . And they were run by people that lived in Mossville.

950
00:47:57.532 --> 00:48:04.376
So . . . and see Butch might have gone someplace else, but I was older than . . . wasn't there for that.

951
00:48:04.376 --> 00:48:06.278
Churches used to have stuff.

952
00:48:06.278 --> 00:48:08.939
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things would the churches . . .?

953
00:48:08.939 --> 00:48:11.601
<v RIGGS>On Friday night they would have things.

954
00:48:11.601 --> 00:48:16.924
You'd go and they would have . . . sell food and maybe play some little games.

955
00:48:16.924 --> 00:48:18.825
Bingo or something like that.

956
00:48:18.825 --> 00:48:21.487
That was in the early days . . . young.

957
00:48:21.487 --> 00:48:24.529
<v ARSENEAULT>Have you ever heard of a pew rally?

958
00:48:24.529 --> 00:48:26.810
Someone mentioned a pew rally, but

959
00:48:26.810 --> 00:48:29.471
<c.p>they couldn’t tell me what it was.

960
00:48:29.471 --> 00:48:33.654
<v RIGGS>Yeah, a pew rally . . . I know what a pew rally is.

961
00:48:33.654 --> 00:48:39.738
A pew rally is at the church and what you do is everyone had a pew.

962
00:48:39.738 --> 00:48:48.863
You have a pew, I would have a pew, which is a seat, and you ask people to come and sit in your pew.

963
00:48:48.863 --> 00:48:55.707
And if you're good you have two or three pews and you invite people to the pew rally.

964
00:48:55.707 --> 00:49:00.270
It was a way I think of raising money for the church.

965
00:49:00.270 --> 00:49:08.635
And then they . . . But I guess whatever the people on that pew gave, you would collect it and then turn it in.

966
00:49:08.635 --> 00:49:10.916
So there would be a winner.

967
00:49:10.916 --> 00:49:14.719
Somebody that had the most people in the pew rallies.

968
00:49:14.719 --> 00:49:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What about a penny march?

969
00:49:17.000 --> 00:49:19.112
<v RIGGS>Oh, yeah . . . Know what that is.

970
00:49:19.112 --> 00:49:19.464
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

971
00:49:19.464 --> 00:49:21.223
<v RIGGS>Because I was a queen.

972
00:49:21.223 --> 00:49:28.615
The penny march was you select young ladies . . . and sometimes they would do it like they . . . did it for the churches.

973
00:49:28.615 --> 00:49:34.950
You'd get somebody from Christ Sanctified, somebody from the C.M.E., somebody from the Baptist, or just individual people.

974
00:49:34.950 --> 00:49:37.413
You collect money, and it's a table.

975
00:49:37.413 --> 00:49:39.173
It's a table sitting there.

976
00:49:39.173 --> 00:49:44.101
And you march around the table and you drop a penny in a basket.

977
00:49:44.101 --> 00:49:44.453
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

978
00:49:44.453 --> 00:49:48.676
<v RIGGS>Each person . . . So most of the time it was at the school.

979
00:49:48.676 --> 00:49:58.179
And what happens is the queen would march around and people would march behind her just a circle going around and around dropping pennies in the basket.

980
00:49:58.179 --> 00:50:00.642
That went until . . . You had [no] money.

981
00:50:00.642 --> 00:50:02.402
You started off with money.

982
00:50:02.402 --> 00:50:14.369
So what you'd do you would go to the table . . . When it was time for the queen to march you go to the table and you give them say twenty dollars at that time.

983
00:50:14.369 --> 00:50:17.536
Well they'd give you twenty dollars’ worth of pennies.

984
00:50:17.536 --> 00:50:17.888
So . . .

985
00:50:17.888 --> 00:50:20.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That's a lot of pennies.

986
00:50:20.000 --> 00:50:20.512
<v RIGGS>Oh, yes.

987
00:50:20.512 --> 00:50:21.793
So queen just marching, marching . . .

988
00:50:21.793 --> 00:50:23.329
<v ARSENEAULT>And dropping pennies the whole time.

989
00:50:23.329 --> 00:50:23.585
Okay.

990
00:50:23.585 --> 00:50:29.988
<v RIGGS>She marching, marching, and then when she runs out of pennies she gets some more and people would follow her and go round with her.

991
00:50:29.988 --> 00:50:31.780
Go behind her and drop pennies in.

992
00:50:31.780 --> 00:50:33.061
Go behind, drop pennies in.

993
00:50:33.061 --> 00:50:34.854
It was just a little get together.

994
00:50:34.854 --> 00:50:37.927
They probably sold gumbo, and had fried fish, and stuff like that.

995
00:50:37.927 --> 00:50:41.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So what did you have to do to become a queen?

996
00:50:41.000 --> 00:50:44.084
<v RIGGS>Well you were selected because see you had to raise money.

997
00:50:44.084 --> 00:50:48.569
All the money that you changed, like when you got the twenty dollars’ worth of pennies,

998
00:50:48.569 --> 00:50:54.736
you put the pennies back in there so that if there was people taking this money to the bank then they would

999
00:50:54.736 --> 00:50:57.540
<c.p>count the money up at the end of the evening.

1000
00:50:57.540 --> 00:51:01.184
All the money that the first queen had they would count that up.

1001
00:51:01.184 --> 00:51:04.548
I guess they were doing it kind of as it went on.

1002
00:51:04.548 --> 00:51:05.389
I don't remember.

1003
00:51:05.389 --> 00:51:11.276
But at the end of the evening . . . It would be like two or three days or ever how they had it.

1004
00:51:11.276 --> 00:51:16.883
At the end of the evening the person that dropped the most money in the basket was the queen, okay?

1005
00:51:16.883 --> 00:51:18.285
So she was the queen.

1006
00:51:18.285 --> 00:51:20.527
The next night somebody else tried to be.

1007
00:51:20.527 --> 00:51:21.929
You’d go around and around.

1008
00:51:21.929 --> 00:51:26.695
Drop the money in and they count the money up and that night somebody else might win.

1009
00:51:26.695 --> 00:51:28.096
So everybody wants to win.

1010
00:51:28.096 --> 00:51:32.021
So the next night say you try to get more money and more money.

1011
00:51:32.021 --> 00:51:39.870
And at the end you would have a queen because the person that raised the most money over the three nights was the queen of the penny march.

1012
00:51:39.870 --> 00:51:40.711
That make sense?

1013
00:51:40.711 --> 00:51:44.356
<v ARSENEAULT>So there's a queen for the day and then there's a grand queen?

1014
00:51:44.356 --> 00:51:45.197
<v RIGGS>Grand queen, yes.

1015
00:51:45.197 --> 00:51:47.159
<v ARSENEAULT>How is the grand queen treated, or?

1016
00:51:47.159 --> 00:51:48.000
A sash?

1017
00:51:48.000 --> 00:51:48.315
<v RIGGS>Nothing.

1018
00:51:48.315 --> 00:51:49.258
That was it.

1019
00:51:49.258 --> 00:51:49.887
<v ARSENEAULT>You just . . .

1020
00:51:49.887 --> 00:51:51.145
<v RIGGS>You had a crown.

1021
00:51:51.145 --> 00:51:51.460
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1022
00:51:51.460 --> 00:51:52.718
<v RIGGS>You had a crown.

1023
00:51:52.718 --> 00:51:54.919
You start off nobody had a crown.

1024
00:51:54.919 --> 00:51:59.008
After the first night, the queen of the first night get the crown.

1025
00:51:59.008 --> 00:52:08.129
So the next night if somebody beat her then she has to go up and take the crown off and put it on the person that won that night.

1026
00:52:08.129 --> 00:52:10.960
So the last night you got a nice crown.

1027
00:52:10.960 --> 00:52:11.903
Probably a tiara.

1028
00:52:11.903 --> 00:52:13.161
I don't really remember.

1029
00:52:13.161 --> 00:52:15.363
So you get to keep that one.

1030
00:52:15.363 --> 00:52:17.565
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you still have any of them?

1031
00:52:17.565 --> 00:52:20.081
<v RIGGS>No. [laughs] No, but that was a thing.

1032
00:52:20.081 --> 00:52:21.653
That was a big thing.

1033
00:52:21.653 --> 00:52:25.742
I don't know who told you that, but that was a big thing.

1034
00:52:25.742 --> 00:52:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Ms. Lenoria Ambrose.

1035
00:52:27.000 --> 00:52:27.558
<v RIGGS>Yeah, yeah.

1036
00:52:27.558 --> 00:52:32.860
<v ARSENEAULT>She had told me about it a penny rally, but she was like, "I don't remember what it was."

1037
00:52:32.860 --> 00:52:33.140
<v RIGGS>Lenoria.

1038
00:52:33.140 --> 00:52:33.977
<v ARSENEAULT>I was like . . .

1039
00:52:33.977 --> 00:52:34.535
<v RIGGS>Lenoria, yeah.

1040
00:52:34.535 --> 00:52:34.814
Lenoria.

1041
00:52:34.814 --> 00:52:37.884
<v ARSENEAULT>She told me about pew rallies, and penny marches, and cake . . .

1042
00:52:37.884 --> 00:52:38.442
<v RIGGS>Cake walks.

1043
00:52:38.442 --> 00:52:39.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1044
00:52:39.000 --> 00:52:41.236
<v RIGGS>She told you how that go?

1045
00:52:41.236 --> 00:52:41.609
<v ARSENEAULT>Some.

1046
00:52:41.609 --> 00:52:44.590
She's like, "I think . . ." No, she tried but . . .

1047
00:52:44.590 --> 00:52:47.571
<v RIGGS>Okay, with the cake walk people made cakes.

1048
00:52:47.571 --> 00:52:48.316
Homemade cakes.

1049
00:52:48.316 --> 00:52:55.397
So what happened is they . . . You would have chairs and so you pay . . . I done forgot what it was,

1050
00:52:55.397 --> 00:52:58.751
but you pay to be in the cake walk.

1051
00:52:58.751 --> 00:53:00.614
I'll just say a quarter.

1052
00:53:00.614 --> 00:53:04.713
Then you walk around . . . They play music and you walk around.

1053
00:53:04.713 --> 00:53:09.558
You have ten chairs and you have fifteen [Riggs’ clarification: eleven] people walking.

1054
00:53:09.558 --> 00:53:17.756
So when the music is playing and you walk around, walk around, and when the music stops you sit in a chair.

1055
00:53:17.756 --> 00:53:22.228
So the person that does not get a chair, they're all eliminated.

1056
00:53:22.228 --> 00:53:25.954
Then you walk again and . . . No, you move a chair.

1057
00:53:25.954 --> 00:53:30.054
You eliminate and you move a chair and you walk again.

1058
00:53:30.054 --> 00:53:33.408
<v ARSENEAULT>So one person's going to be without a chair.

1059
00:53:33.408 --> 00:53:37.507
<v RIGGS>One person will be without a chair and you eliminate . . . Eliminate.

1060
00:53:37.507 --> 00:53:39.743
The last person gets the cake.

1061
00:53:39.743 --> 00:53:41.233
<v ARSENEAULT>I bet people were . . .

1062
00:53:41.233 --> 00:53:44.587
<v RIGGS>Yeah they'd be running and trying to get it.

1063
00:53:44.587 --> 00:53:46.823
Then it goes all over again.

1064
00:53:46.823 --> 00:53:48.686
You pay to do that.

1065
00:53:48.686 --> 00:53:50.922
<v ARSENEAULT>Did anyone ever get hurt, or?

1066
00:53:50.922 --> 00:53:53.158
<v RIGGS>No, that was a big thing.

1067
00:53:53.158 --> 00:53:56.512
People would do that and it was just fun.

1068
00:53:56.512 --> 00:53:58.748
It was like something to do.

1069
00:53:58.748 --> 00:54:02.102
That's some of the things we hung out with.

1070
00:54:02.102 --> 00:54:05.083
There was a thing that I didn't do,

1071
00:54:05.083 --> 00:54:11.418
that there was a thing that the older people did was . . . It was called a box supper.

1072
00:54:11.418 --> 00:54:14.772
The kicker was you didn't know who cooked what.

1073
00:54:14.772 --> 00:54:25.952
So the box supper was like the food was sitting on the table and you had to go around and kind of like bid for what you wanted to eat.

1074
00:54:25.952 --> 00:54:32.660
So for instance if this had chicken and so forth and so on, you had turkey and dressing.

1075
00:54:32.660 --> 00:54:35.641
So you would bid on what you want.

1076
00:54:35.641 --> 00:54:52.410
You'd write down what you . . . And at the end they would reveal who it was and then a table was set up and everybody that signed up to eat that would go over and sit down at a table and have dinner with that person.

1077
00:54:52.410 --> 00:54:55.391
Then you would know who the cook was.

1078
00:54:55.391 --> 00:54:58.000
That was kind of neat, too.

1079
00:54:58.000 --> 00:55:00.084
<v ARSENEAULT>Did anyone ever get their feelings hurt?

1080
00:55:00.084 --> 00:55:03.359
Like they didn't get there's bid on or anything like that?

1081
00:55:03.359 --> 00:55:04.252
<v RIGGS>I don't know.

1082
00:55:04.252 --> 00:55:07.229
But the things was, you didn't know who cooked what.

1083
00:55:07.229 --> 00:55:07.527
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1084
00:55:07.527 --> 00:55:13.183
<v RIGGS>You might have thought you were signing up to eat your food and it might have been his food.

1085
00:55:13.183 --> 00:55:13.481
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1086
00:55:13.481 --> 00:55:15.267
<v RIGGS>And that was something they did.

1087
00:55:15.267 --> 00:55:17.351
That was a way of raising money.

1088
00:55:17.351 --> 00:55:18.244
<v ARSENEAULT>For the church?

1089
00:55:18.244 --> 00:55:20.626
<v RIGGS>For the church, [agrees] or for the school.

1090
00:55:20.626 --> 00:55:26.282
I imagine they did some of that stuff when they were like in high school running for different things.

1091
00:55:26.282 --> 00:55:30.153
I can't say that because I didn't go to the high school here.

1092
00:55:30.153 --> 00:55:31.641
What else did we do?

1093
00:55:31.641 --> 00:55:32.534
Just little stuff.

1094
00:55:32.534 --> 00:55:34.023
<v ARSENEAULT>Let’s talk about your school.

1095
00:55:34.023 --> 00:55:34.618
Like where . . .?

1096
00:55:34.618 --> 00:55:37.000
<v RIGGS>I did not go to school here.

1097
00:55:37.000 --> 00:55:37.706
<v ARSENEAULT>So no elementary?

1098
00:55:37.706 --> 00:55:38.412
<v RIGGS>Yeah, I did.

1099
00:55:38.412 --> 00:55:38.882
I did.

1100
00:55:38.882 --> 00:55:40.059
We can talk about that.

1101
00:55:40.059 --> 00:55:41.471
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it at the old school?

1102
00:55:41.471 --> 00:55:42.882
<v RIGGS>I went to the old school.

1103
00:55:42.882 --> 00:55:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that a Rosenwald School by any chance?

1104
00:55:45.000 --> 00:55:46.098
<v RIGGS>I don't know.

1105
00:55:46.098 --> 00:55:47.195
I don't know.

1106
00:55:47.195 --> 00:55:47.561
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1107
00:55:47.561 --> 00:55:50.122
<v RIGGS>Tell me what a Rosenwald School is.

1108
00:55:50.122 --> 00:55:57.073
<v ARSENEAULT>Rosenwald was a Jewish philanthropist from Chicago [Illinois] and he started a lot of schools in rural black communities,

1109
00:55:57.073 --> 00:56:00.000
and they're these beautiful old wooden buildings . . .

1110
00:56:00.000 --> 00:56:01.345
<v RIGGS>That’s what this is.

1111
00:56:01.345 --> 00:56:02.690
<v ARSENEAULT>Usually with big windows.

1112
00:56:02.690 --> 00:56:05.379
They said that Mossville had a Rosenwald School.

1113
00:56:05.379 --> 00:56:06.388
<v RIGGS>It may be.

1114
00:56:06.388 --> 00:56:09.078
I didn't know that, but it could be.

1115
00:56:09.078 --> 00:56:11.431
It was . . . I had that in here.

1116
00:56:11.431 --> 00:56:11.767
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1117
00:56:11.767 --> 00:56:16.138
<v RIGGS>It was . . . The school was a two-classroom building with a big front porch.

1118
00:56:16.138 --> 00:56:18.155
<v ARSENEAULT>That sounds like it might be.

1119
00:56:18.155 --> 00:56:23.534
<v RIGGS>And you would walk in and as you walked into the left was a coat room,

1120
00:56:23.534 --> 00:56:28.914
to the right was a coat room, this was a room, and that was a classroom.

1121
00:56:28.914 --> 00:56:30.595
<v ARSENEAULT>So it was two classrooms?

1122
00:56:30.595 --> 00:56:30.931
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

1123
00:56:30.931 --> 00:56:36.647
And in between was a partition that went back and forth and they would open it up.

1124
00:56:36.647 --> 00:56:39.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Like on a track or something?

1125
00:56:39.000 --> 00:56:39.414
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

1126
00:56:39.414 --> 00:56:46.041
It went back into the wall and they would open it up when they had assembly.

1127
00:56:46.041 --> 00:56:49.355
They say . . . I'd think . . . I can't remember now,

1128
00:56:49.355 --> 00:56:57.639
like one side was like first to fifth and the other side was . . . It only went to the eighth grade.

1129
00:56:57.639 --> 00:56:58.053
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1130
00:56:58.053 --> 00:57:00.538
<v RIGGS>So that was the first school.

1131
00:57:00.538 --> 00:57:04.266
And had the windows, you're right, all the way

1132
00:57:04.266 --> 00:57:04.680
<c.p>around.

1133
00:57:04.680 --> 00:57:07.166
<v ARSENEAULT>What was it made out of?

1134
00:57:07.166 --> 00:57:07.580
<v RIGGS>Wood.

1135
00:57:07.580 --> 00:57:07.994
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1136
00:57:07.994 --> 00:57:08.408
<v RIGGS>Wood.

1137
00:57:08.408 --> 00:57:10.479
The floors were wood, too.

1138
00:57:10.479 --> 00:57:11.308
<v ARSENEAULT>So everything?

1139
00:57:11.308 --> 00:57:12.550
<v RIGGS>Everything was wood.

1140
00:57:12.550 --> 00:57:12.964
Wood.

1141
00:57:12.964 --> 00:57:14.207
Old, nice wood.

1142
00:57:14.207 --> 00:57:16.692
So that was the first school.

1143
00:57:16.692 --> 00:57:24.148
And they built an adjacent classroom, just one building, and they built a cafeteria on the other side.

1144
00:57:24.148 --> 00:57:29.118
So it was three classrooms now, and the fourth was the cafeteria.

1145
00:57:29.118 --> 00:57:39.888
That was there until . . . I don't know when that school actually closed because in '52 was when I went to Arkansas to go to high school.

1146
00:57:39.888 --> 00:57:49.000
At that time . . . I mean during that time if you went to high school you had to come to Lake Charles.

1147
00:57:49.000 --> 00:57:50.364
<v ARSENEAULT>Because of the . . .?

1148
00:57:50.364 --> 00:57:51.727
<v RIGGS>There was no . . .

1149
00:57:51.727 --> 00:57:53.091
<v ARSENEAULT>No high school?

1150
00:57:53.091 --> 00:57:56.273
<v RIGGS>No. It was [Second Ward?] or W.O.

1151
00:57:56.273 --> 00:58:02.182
Boston. [Second Ward?] was first school and it went and they did W.O.

1152
00:58:02.182 --> 00:58:05.818
Boston and there were people that did that.

1153
00:58:05.818 --> 00:58:09.000
I'd name some people that did that.

1154
00:58:09.000 --> 00:58:15.818
Then they bused the children to Westlake for a while and to Washington . . . Lake Charles.

1155
00:58:15.818 --> 00:58:20.818
In 1956 they built Mossville High School K [kindergarten] though twelve.

1156
00:58:20.818 --> 00:58:26.727
But basically and then I had . . . Did anybody give you teachers or anything?

1157
00:58:26.727 --> 00:58:28.091
<v ARSENEAULT>We talked about . . .

1158
00:58:28.091 --> 00:58:30.364
<v RIGGS>Or do you want those?

1159
00:58:30.364 --> 00:58:36.273
<v ARSENEAULT>Some teachers in . . . Like Ms. Rogers and people who just talked about school.

1160
00:58:36.273 --> 00:58:38.545
<v RIGGS>They were the high school.

1161
00:58:38.545 --> 00:58:40.818
I got some before then.

1162
00:58:40.818 --> 00:58:41.273
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1163
00:58:41.273 --> 00:58:47.182
So you started . . . what year did you start . . . What grade did you start?

1164
00:58:47.182 --> 00:58:49.000
Do you remember?

1165
00:58:49.000 --> 00:58:50.444
<v RIGGS>I started in first grade.

1166
00:58:50.444 --> 00:58:51.022
<v ARSENEAULT>First grade.

1167
00:58:51.022 --> 00:58:51.600
<v RIGGS>First grade.

1168
00:58:51.600 --> 00:58:53.044
<v ARSENEAULT>So they didn't have kindergarten?

1169
00:58:53.044 --> 00:58:53.622
<v RIGGS>No kindergarten.

1170
00:58:53.622 --> 00:58:54.778
Not in my time.

1171
00:58:54.778 --> 00:58:56.800
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay, so first grade was like kindergarten.

1172
00:58:56.800 --> 00:58:58.244
<v RIGGS>First grade was like kindergarten.

1173
00:58:58.244 --> 00:58:59.689
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember the year?

1174
00:58:59.689 --> 00:59:00.267
<v RIGGS>It's not . . .

1175
00:59:00.267 --> 00:59:02.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You were born in '36.

1176
00:59:02.000 --> 00:59:03.034
<v RIGGS>I was born in '36.

1177
00:59:03.034 --> 00:59:04.897
<v ARSENEAULT>So probably started when you were five or six.

1178
00:59:04.897 --> 00:59:08.000
<v RIGGS>I was five or six, but I don't know what . . . I don't really remember.

1179
00:59:08.000 --> 00:59:09.290
<v ARSENEAULT>Maybe '41, '42?

1180
00:59:09.290 --> 00:59:10.581
Something like that.

1181
00:59:10.581 --> 00:59:12.301
<v RIGGS>I would say '41.

1182
00:59:12.301 --> 00:59:17.032
I know '40 . . . I know I . . . Yeah, I would say '41.

1183
00:59:17.032 --> 00:59:21.333
Let’s see, '41, '42, '43, '44, '45, '46, '47, '48.

1184
00:59:21.333 --> 00:59:23.484
No, that was too early.

1185
00:59:23.484 --> 00:59:26.925
In '52 I was in the ninth grade.

1186
00:59:26.925 --> 00:59:29.075
<v ARSENEAULT>Started ninth grade in '52.

1187
00:59:29.075 --> 00:59:32.946
So how old were you in the ninth grade?

1188
00:59:32.946 --> 00:59:36.387
Maybe, I don't know which year they start .

1189
00:59:36.387 --> 00:59:38.538
<v RIGGS>I guess I was a . . .

1190
00:59:38.538 --> 00:59:40.688
<v ARSENEAULT>What age they start . . . Thirteen?

1191
00:59:40.688 --> 00:59:42.409
Is that too old?

1192
00:59:42.409 --> 00:59:43.699
<v RIGGS>Probably about thirteen.

1193
00:59:43.699 --> 00:59:45.849
Thirteen, fourteen, fifteen, sixteen, seventeen.

1194
00:59:45.849 --> 00:59:48.000
Yeah, that's about right.

1195
00:59:48.000 --> 00:59:48.409
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1196
00:59:48.409 --> 00:59:50.045
So fifty-two minus thirteen.

1197
00:59:50.045 --> 00:59:50.455
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

1198
00:59:50.455 --> 00:59:51.682
<v ARSENEAULT>Whatever that is.

1199
00:59:51.682 --> 00:59:55.773
I have to do . . . fifty-two . . . thirteen . . . Four carry the one.

1200
00:59:55.773 --> 00:59:59.455
Four, five six, seven, eight, nine, ten, eleven, twelve.

1201
00:59:59.455 --> 01:00:00.682
Isn't this sad?

1202
01:00:00.682 --> 01:00:03.545
I can't do this in my head.

1203
01:00:03.545 --> 01:00:09.682
<v RIGGS>I was in November, so I probably didn't go to school until I was six.

1204
01:00:09.682 --> 01:00:11.727
I was born in November . . .

1205
01:00:11.727 --> 01:00:12.955
<v ARSENEAULT>So around '40.

1206
01:00:12.955 --> 01:00:13.364
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

1207
01:00:13.364 --> 01:00:14.182
<v ARSENEAULT>I guess.

1208
01:00:14.182 --> 01:00:15.000
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

1209
01:00:15.000 --> 01:00:16.364
<v ARSENEAULT>Which [. . .?] the number.

1210
01:00:16.364 --> 01:00:18.182
<v RIGGS>What were we saying?

1211
01:00:18.182 --> 01:00:20.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So the old school.

1212
01:00:20.000 --> 01:00:24.091
So you started first grade at the old school.

1213
01:00:24.091 --> 01:00:25.909
Who was the teacher?

1214
01:00:25.909 --> 01:00:27.727
Who was your teacher?

1215
01:00:27.727 --> 01:00:30.455
<v RIGGS>My teacher was Ms. [Ezora?] Lyons.

1216
01:00:30.455 --> 01:00:34.545
The person before that, the principal, was Mr. Williams.

1217
01:00:34.545 --> 01:00:38.182
I don’t remember that was my brother's principal.

1218
01:00:38.182 --> 01:00:39.545
I don't remember.

1219
01:00:39.545 --> 01:00:44.091
Ezora Lyons and [Farrah May?] Washington was my first teachers.

1220
01:00:44.091 --> 01:00:51.818
Then when they added that other school it was Farrah May Washington, Doris [Billups?], and Ethel Mitchell.

1221
01:00:51.818 --> 01:00:58.182
They were probably there when the school closed because they went on to Mossville.

1222
01:00:58.182 --> 01:01:04.091
I don't know where they went because it was something in between there.

1223
01:01:04.091 --> 01:01:07.727
But they ended up at the Mossville School.

1224
01:01:07.727 --> 01:01:13.636
I don't know where they taught in between there, but they were there.

1225
01:01:13.636 --> 01:01:15.000
They were.

1226
01:01:15.000 --> 01:01:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So there was the principal.

1227
01:01:17.000 --> 01:01:18.600
<v RIGGS>Which was Mr. Williams.

1228
01:01:18.600 --> 01:01:20.600
I can't remember his name.

1229
01:01:20.600 --> 01:01:24.200
<v ARSENEAULT>Mr. Williams, and was he a teacher as well?

1230
01:01:24.200 --> 01:01:25.000
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

1231
01:01:25.000 --> 01:01:29.325
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he teach . . . So is it like they closed the partition?

1232
01:01:29.325 --> 01:01:30.111
<v RIGGS>Yeah, yeah.

1233
01:01:30.111 --> 01:01:31.684
They taught as well.

1234
01:01:31.684 --> 01:01:32.470
<v ARSENEAULT>I see.

1235
01:01:32.470 --> 01:01:34.436
What were the classes like?

1236
01:01:34.436 --> 01:01:36.009
<v RIGGS>The classes were combined.

1237
01:01:36.009 --> 01:01:37.581
I remember that well.

1238
01:01:37.581 --> 01:01:43.085
It was like the first, second, third grade like was all in one room.

1239
01:01:43.085 --> 01:01:45.838
They kind of taught you individually, too.

1240
01:01:45.838 --> 01:01:53.701
Like you sat and worked and maybe they would be doing math over here, but that wasn't your math class.

1241
01:01:53.701 --> 01:01:58.812
Then later on you would do math and they were doing something else.

1242
01:01:58.812 --> 01:02:01.171
That was with the combined classes.

1243
01:02:01.171 --> 01:02:07.068
I'm trying to remember what happened like say fifth, sixth . . . It's probably the same thing.

1244
01:02:07.068 --> 01:02:07.855
Same way.

1245
01:02:07.855 --> 01:02:11.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So was it all girls and boys?

1246
01:02:11.000 --> 01:02:11.659
<v RIGGS>It was mixed.

1247
01:02:11.659 --> 01:02:12.756
<v ARSENEAULT>So it was all mixed?

1248
01:02:12.756 --> 01:02:13.415
<v RIGGS>It was mixed.

1249
01:02:13.415 --> 01:02:16.049
<v ARSENEAULT>Boys like in the front or in the back or they just?

1250
01:02:16.049 --> 01:02:17.146
<v RIGGS>No, it was just mixed.

1251
01:02:17.146 --> 01:02:20.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How did you know what you were supposed to be working on?

1252
01:02:20.000 --> 01:02:20.903
<v RIGGS>How'd you know?

1253
01:02:20.903 --> 01:02:26.925
Well they told you this what the . . . They’d said, “Alright well the fifth grades we're going to do this now.

1254
01:02:26.925 --> 01:02:28.731
We’re going to do your math.”

1255
01:02:28.731 --> 01:02:34.452
And so fifth grade did math and other children did whatever she told them to do at that time.

1256
01:02:34.452 --> 01:02:36.860
And then some classes you had it together.

1257
01:02:36.860 --> 01:02:39.269
Maybe the geography was like everybody did geography.

1258
01:02:39.269 --> 01:02:41.075
<v ARSENEAULT>So they would open the partition?

1259
01:02:41.075 --> 01:02:42.581
<v RIGGS>No, no, no, no, no.

1260
01:02:42.581 --> 01:02:43.785
<v ARSENEAULT>With the whole class?

1261
01:02:43.785 --> 01:02:44.688
<v RIGGS>The whole class.

1262
01:02:44.688 --> 01:02:46.194
Whatever was in that room.

1263
01:02:46.194 --> 01:02:46.796
<v ARSENEAULT>I see.

1264
01:02:46.796 --> 01:02:48.000
<v RIGGS>In your room.

1265
01:02:48.000 --> 01:02:52.324
<v ARSENEAULT>So some things were separate and then some things were all together?

1266
01:02:52.324 --> 01:02:54.126
<v RIGGS>No, you had two classrooms.

1267
01:02:54.126 --> 01:02:54.486
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1268
01:02:54.486 --> 01:02:58.811
<v RIGGS>Grades . . . Let’s just say grades one through four was in this room.

1269
01:02:58.811 --> 01:02:59.171
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1270
01:02:59.171 --> 01:03:01.694
<v RIGGS>Five, six, seven was in this room.

1271
01:03:01.694 --> 01:03:04.577
And you never mixed until you had assembly.

1272
01:03:04.577 --> 01:03:06.018
When you had assembly,

1273
01:03:06.018 --> 01:03:13.586
that’s . . . They opened that partition and then everybody . . . You sang and had a little program or whatever you did in assembly.

1274
01:03:13.586 --> 01:03:15.748
Or the principal talked or whatever.

1275
01:03:15.748 --> 01:03:22.234
But then once that was over that was closed and then you would go back to your classes.

1276
01:03:22.234 --> 01:03:25.477
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of books would you . . . did they use?

1277
01:03:25.477 --> 01:03:26.919
<v RIGGS>We got hand-me-downs [laughs].

1278
01:03:26.919 --> 01:03:28.000
<v ARSENEAULT>From where?

1279
01:03:28.000 --> 01:03:33.268
<v RIGGS>I guess from the white school because they had names in them already.

1280
01:03:33.268 --> 01:03:36.511
We got the hand-me-downs, but they were books.

1281
01:03:36.511 --> 01:03:39.753
They were hard-back books and they were books.

1282
01:03:39.753 --> 01:03:43.805
There was spot and Jane went to the fair clippity-clap.

1283
01:03:43.805 --> 01:03:45.021
We had books.

1284
01:03:45.021 --> 01:03:50.289
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you know what kind of curriculum it was or anything like that?

1285
01:03:50.289 --> 01:03:51.100
<v RIGGS>Not really.

1286
01:03:51.100 --> 01:03:54.342
Basically you had your math and your reading.

1287
01:03:54.342 --> 01:03:54.747
Geography.

1288
01:03:54.747 --> 01:03:56.368
I'm trying to remember.

1289
01:03:56.368 --> 01:03:57.989
Guess a little science.

1290
01:03:57.989 --> 01:04:07.311
And the thing was like on the wall you would have . . . They had stuff like with your science stuff on the wall over,

1291
01:04:07.311 --> 01:04:11.363
and there and maybe your math stuff over here stuff.

1292
01:04:11.363 --> 01:04:17.442
But you had never been a single classroom so what . . . You couldn't expect anything else.

1293
01:04:17.442 --> 01:04:25.142
Until I knew when I went to high school, and then it was like you were in your classes.

1294
01:04:25.142 --> 01:04:29.600
But when you had a combined school it just never occurred.

1295
01:04:29.600 --> 01:04:39.326
I think maybe might have been a plus because if these people were learning timetables and you were sitting there you would learn them.

1296
01:04:39.326 --> 01:04:41.758
<v ARSENEAULT>Whether you wanted to or not.

1297
01:04:41.758 --> 01:04:45.000
<v RIGGS>Whether you wanted to or not [agrees].

1298
01:04:45.000 --> 01:04:47.938
<v ARSENEAULT>You were being exposed to a lot.

1299
01:04:47.938 --> 01:04:50.877
<v RIGGS>You were being exposed to other things.

1300
01:04:50.877 --> 01:04:58.012
Which made you a little bit ahead of where you were if you were in one grade.

1301
01:04:58.012 --> 01:05:05.148
But basically you didn't know the difference until you got to where there were just one classroom.

1302
01:05:05.148 --> 01:05:06.827
<v ARSENEAULT>What about the teachers?

1303
01:05:06.827 --> 01:05:08.926
How did they keep discipline?

1304
01:05:08.926 --> 01:05:11.444
What were some of the things?

1305
01:05:11.444 --> 01:05:13.123
<v RIGGS>They were very . . . strict.

1306
01:05:13.123 --> 01:05:20.679
That you knew that you had to do what you were supposed to do and you did that.

1307
01:05:20.679 --> 01:05:29.494
And if you were told to . . . If you were outside and she told you to stay there until she came back,

1308
01:05:29.494 --> 01:05:32.012
you stayed until she came back.

1309
01:05:32.012 --> 01:05:39.568
You had people that did things, but basically it wasn't any problems because they just didn't take that.

1310
01:05:39.568 --> 01:05:41.247
They didn't have to.

1311
01:05:41.247 --> 01:05:43.765
Of course they could paddle, too.

1312
01:05:43.765 --> 01:05:44.185
So . . .

1313
01:05:44.185 --> 01:05:50.062
<v ARSENEAULT>Did they have to send you to the principal to get paddled or did . . .?

1314
01:05:50.062 --> 01:05:50.481
<v RIGGS>No.

1315
01:05:50.481 --> 01:05:53.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did the teachers do it?

1316
01:05:53.000 --> 01:05:56.927
<v RIGGS>No, no. You may have gone to the principal.

1317
01:05:56.927 --> 01:06:03.473
If you went to the principal it was really something bad that you had done.

1318
01:06:03.473 --> 01:06:06.527
That you had gone to the principal.

1319
01:06:06.527 --> 01:06:06.964
No,

1320
01:06:06.964 --> 01:06:09.582
<c.p>the teachers took care of everything.

1321
01:06:09.582 --> 01:06:13.945
It was like she was the captain of that room.

1322
01:06:13.945 --> 01:06:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have a favorite teacher?

1323
01:06:17.000 --> 01:06:19.795
<v RIGGS>Probably Ms. Washington was my favorite teacher.

1324
01:06:19.795 --> 01:06:22.590
She . . . And I kept up with her.

1325
01:06:22.590 --> 01:06:26.982
I was in Houston and I still kept up with her.

1326
01:06:26.982 --> 01:06:29.378
So that was a long time.

1327
01:06:29.378 --> 01:06:40.957
She was . . . they would come out and they would . . . A lot of times they would come out and if we had something at night they would stay in Mossville.

1328
01:06:40.957 --> 01:06:45.748
They lived in Lake Charles most of them, but they would stay.

1329
01:06:45.748 --> 01:06:49.342
Like maybe they’d stay with my mother one time.

1330
01:06:49.342 --> 01:06:50.939
Maybe Butch's mother one.

1331
01:06:50.939 --> 01:06:55.331
But I can’t remember if . . . probably some of them had cars,

1332
01:06:55.331 --> 01:07:01.320
but if they didn't . . . If they stayed and it was late they may stay there.

1333
01:07:01.320 --> 01:07:06.910
They wer e like kind of . . . they would . . . More like part of the community.

1334
01:07:06.910 --> 01:07:14.896
If something was going on at one of the churches they may come that Sunday, or be there that Sunday.

1335
01:07:14.896 --> 01:07:20.086
Or if you were on program and you ask her she might come.

1336
01:07:20.086 --> 01:07:21.683
Stuff . . . They were close.

1337
01:07:21.683 --> 01:07:24.079
You had a knit with them.

1338
01:07:24.079 --> 01:07:32.065
I guess they were . . . just felt they were part of the community and part of the families that lived there.

1339
01:07:32.065 --> 01:07:33.662
So I don't know.

1340
01:07:33.662 --> 01:07:38.853
I don't know even if those or some of those people taught Jeffery.

1341
01:07:38.853 --> 01:07:44.043
Not taught him, but they were at that school when he went there.

1342
01:07:44.043 --> 01:07:49.633
But I was about to say I don't know . . . Yeah, some of them did.

1343
01:07:49.633 --> 01:07:52.428
Some of them did do the integration.

1344
01:07:52.428 --> 01:07:54.824
Some of them were still there.

1345
01:07:54.824 --> 01:08:00.414
I don't know if those two, those I named, did but some of them.

1346
01:08:00.414 --> 01:08:04.007
But basically it was a good experience for me.

1347
01:08:04.007 --> 01:08:08.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of games did y'all play growing up?

1348
01:08:08.000 --> 01:08:11.561
<v RIGGS>Hopscotch, jump rope, we did a lot of board games.

1349
01:08:11.561 --> 01:08:15.833
Even at school we had board . . . We had checkers and Siamese checkers.

1350
01:08:15.833 --> 01:08:17.970
And like what you had recess.

1351
01:08:17.970 --> 01:08:25.803
You had to go out and play, or if you didn't go out and play you could go in the cloak room,

1352
01:08:25.803 --> 01:08:30.788
that's where they were, and check them out and sit on that front porch.

1353
01:08:30.788 --> 01:08:35.061
And you could play games or play ball, or . . . Nothing really organized.

1354
01:08:35.061 --> 01:08:42.894
We didn't . . . And maybe we might at sometimes we would do exercise, but we didn't have like a physical education or exercise.

1355
01:08:42.894 --> 01:08:44.674
We went out and played.

1356
01:08:44.674 --> 01:08:46.455
Had playgrounds at our school.

1357
01:08:46.455 --> 01:08:47.879
Swings and things, that . . .

1358
01:08:47.879 --> 01:08:51.083
<v ARSENEAULT>Were the girls allowed to play with the boys?

1359
01:08:51.083 --> 01:08:52.152
<v RIGGS>Well not really.

1360
01:08:52.152 --> 01:08:52.864
Not really.

1361
01:08:52.864 --> 01:08:55.356
Usually the boys was on one side.

1362
01:08:55.356 --> 01:08:59.629
I think by choice, and the girls just on the other side.

1363
01:08:59.629 --> 01:09:02.477
But I really think it was by choice.

1364
01:09:02.477 --> 01:09:09.242
I don't think it was that you had to go over there, or you had to go over here.

1365
01:09:09.242 --> 01:09:12.447
<v ARSENEAULT>I guess when they're young kids kind of naturally . . .

1366
01:09:12.447 --> 01:09:14.227
<v RIGGS>Yeah it probably doesn’t matter.

1367
01:09:14.227 --> 01:09:20.992
As you get older it may be you like girls group over here, and then boys group over there.

1368
01:09:20.992 --> 01:09:24.909
Boys playing baseball or football and girls sitting around doing nothing.

1369
01:09:24.909 --> 01:09:25.621
Talking maybe.

1370
01:09:25.621 --> 01:09:34.523
But it wasn't a . . . I don't remember it being where when you came out boys went this way and girls went that way or even . . .

1371
01:09:34.523 --> 01:09:39.508
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you have any like favorite memories of the old school or anything like . . .?

1372
01:09:39.508 --> 01:09:42.000
I know it's far back, but.

1373
01:09:42.000 --> 01:09:55.230
<v RIGGS>Well the old . . . One of the things I remember about the old school is like a the end of the school year we always had a big play and everybody was in the play.

1374
01:09:55.230 --> 01:09:57.565
Every child was in the play.

1375
01:09:57.565 --> 01:09:59.510
We wore costumes and things.

1376
01:09:59.510 --> 01:10:01.456
We did a May fair.

1377
01:10:01.456 --> 01:10:06.904
That was in May when the spring started and we wrapped the May Pole.

1378
01:10:06.904 --> 01:10:09.628
Anybody say anything [to you about that?].

1379
01:10:09.628 --> 01:10:23.247
The May Pole was a pole that went in the ground and they put crepe paper at the top and it would string it out and boys and girls would have a string of paper.

1380
01:10:23.247 --> 01:10:35.310
And I would go . . . People this way would go under and the people that way would go over and you would wrap it and it would be like a French braid.

1381
01:10:35.310 --> 01:10:39.201
And that was . . . People came to see you do that.

1382
01:10:39.201 --> 01:10:43.870
As the kids did that well parents were sitting around watching it.

1383
01:10:43.870 --> 01:10:51.653
Actually the pole became wrapped, but it looked like the French braid it was braided, raised, and it was colorful.

1384
01:10:51.653 --> 01:10:56.322
It might have yellow, and pink, and it was called May Fair.

1385
01:10:56.322 --> 01:10:57.490
I remember that.

1386
01:10:57.490 --> 01:11:00.603
We didn't have anything like playing other schools.

1387
01:11:00.603 --> 01:11:02.159
We didn’t do that.

1388
01:11:02.159 --> 01:11:03.715
Not the elementary school.

1389
01:11:03.715 --> 01:11:05.272
You didn't do that.

1390
01:11:05.272 --> 01:11:09.552
You didn't play like volleyball, or football, or anything like that.

1391
01:11:09.552 --> 01:11:15.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have like a best friend or anything that you remember, or?

1392
01:11:15.000 --> 01:11:19.156
<v RIGGS>Well one of my best friends was . . . It was Sister Julia.

1393
01:11:19.156 --> 01:11:21.800
She was one of my best friends.

1394
01:11:21.800 --> 01:11:22.933
And Tommy Prater.

1395
01:11:22.933 --> 01:11:25.578
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things did y'all do?

1396
01:11:25.578 --> 01:11:27.089
<v RIGGS>Nothing . . . Not too much.

1397
01:11:27.089 --> 01:11:31.244
We were friends like from first grade and stuff like that.

1398
01:11:31.244 --> 01:11:38.044
One thing we did, we used to leave school and we both lived about the same amount from

1399
01:11:38.044 --> 01:11:38.422
<c.p>school.

1400
01:11:38.422 --> 01:11:41.067
And she would walk me a ways.

1401
01:11:41.067 --> 01:11:47.489
Then I'd turn around and walk her a ways, and that went on for about thirty minutes.

1402
01:11:47.489 --> 01:11:50.511
She and Tommy, which was her cousin, Prater.

1403
01:11:50.511 --> 01:11:51.644
The Audrey Prater's.

1404
01:11:51.644 --> 01:11:56.933
I don't know if you got Jean and any of the Prater's at all.

1405
01:11:56.933 --> 01:11:59.200
<v ARSENEAULT>I think I got Ms. Audrey.

1406
01:11:59.200 --> 01:11:59.956
<v RIGGS>Audrey, yeah.

1407
01:11:59.956 --> 01:12:03.356
Audrey's daughter’s Jean, and Joyce, and . . . They were friends.

1408
01:12:03.356 --> 01:12:07.889
But we were in the country so we didn't do too much.

1409
01:12:07.889 --> 01:12:12.044
On the weekends we might would go play with each other,

1410
01:12:12.044 --> 01:12:19.978
but in the evening it would be too late to go and play because it would get dark so we didn't.

1411
01:12:19.978 --> 01:12:23.000
But that was some of my friends.

1412
01:12:23.000 --> 01:12:23.371
<v ARSENEAULT>Great.

1413
01:12:23.371 --> 01:12:25.229
So much to talk about.

1414
01:12:25.229 --> 01:12:28.571
What do you remember about the electricity coming in?

1415
01:12:28.571 --> 01:12:29.686
Or the plumbing?

1416
01:12:29.686 --> 01:12:31.171
Or anything like that.

1417
01:12:31.171 --> 01:12:32.657
<v RIGGS>I remember the electricity.

1418
01:12:32.657 --> 01:12:34.514
I don't really remember electricity.

1419
01:12:34.514 --> 01:12:36.743
I just always remember having lights.

1420
01:12:36.743 --> 01:12:37.857
No, I don't.

1421
01:12:37.857 --> 01:12:38.971
No, I don't.

1422
01:12:38.971 --> 01:12:41.943
I remember when we had some lamps, too.

1423
01:12:41.943 --> 01:12:46.029
But look like it was . . . It all kind of went together.

1424
01:12:46.029 --> 01:12:50.486
I remember lamps, and then I also remember when we got lights.

1425
01:12:50.486 --> 01:12:54.571
The lights were like hanging out the ceiling with a bulb.

1426
01:12:54.571 --> 01:12:55.314
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, wow.

1427
01:12:55.314 --> 01:12:56.429
<v RIGGS>Wasn't no fixture.

1428
01:12:56.429 --> 01:12:57.171
Wasn't no . . .

1429
01:12:57.171 --> 01:12:58.286
<v ARSENEAULT>It was exposed.

1430
01:12:58.286 --> 01:13:01.257
<v RIGGS>Yeah, it was like a . . . It hung down.

1431
01:13:01.257 --> 01:13:02.743
<v ARSENEAULT>It hung like a [?] . . .

1432
01:13:02.743 --> 01:13:06.829
<v RIGGS>Yeah, it hung down and then it was on a . . . Yeah.

1433
01:13:06.829 --> 01:13:08.314
And I remember that.

1434
01:13:08.314 --> 01:13:16.114
It was quite a time before we had bathrooms because they had to do the septic tanks and the running water.

1435
01:13:16.114 --> 01:13:18.343
You had the . . . We had wells.

1436
01:13:18.343 --> 01:13:22.057
You had to have a well to have your water.

1437
01:13:22.057 --> 01:13:29.114
I remember when we got the wells and then got the inside bathroom and that was quite some time.

1438
01:13:29.114 --> 01:13:33.943
And it hadn't been too long since Mossville got their sewer line thing.

1439
01:13:33.943 --> 01:13:41.000
Because everybody had already . . . When they built the house you put your septic tank down and your gas.

1440
01:13:41.000 --> 01:13:42.455
<v ARSENEAULT>So everyone was already . . .

1441
01:13:42.455 --> 01:13:42.818
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

1442
01:13:42.818 --> 01:13:43.545
<v ARSENEAULT>Set up.

1443
01:13:43.545 --> 01:13:49.364
<v RIGGS>You had to have a gas . . . Butane gas, because it wasn't gas from the gas company.

1444
01:13:49.364 --> 01:13:50.818
<v ARSENEAULT>How does that work?

1445
01:13:50.818 --> 01:13:58.091
<v RIGGS>Well it was a tank . . . You know how . . . You know the thing that you use on a barbeque pit that . . .?

1446
01:13:58.091 --> 01:13:59.182
<v ARSENEAULT>A kerosene tank?

1447
01:13:59.182 --> 01:14:00.273
<v RIGGS>No, what's the . . .

1448
01:14:00.273 --> 01:14:01.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Propane.

1449
01:14:01.000 --> 01:14:01.302
<v RIGGS>Propane.

1450
01:14:01.302 --> 01:14:01.604
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1451
01:14:01.604 --> 01:14:06.736
<v RIGGS>So you had a big one that went in the ground, or it sat on the ground,

1452
01:14:06.736 --> 01:14:12.170
and then it ran to your house and a truck had to come out and bring your oil.

1453
01:14:12.170 --> 01:14:15.491
But you had to use that for cooking and for heating.

1454
01:14:15.491 --> 01:14:17.604
<v ARSENEAULT>So a truck came to refill it.

1455
01:14:17.604 --> 01:14:17.906
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

1456
01:14:17.906 --> 01:14:20.019
You had to pay to refill it.

1457
01:14:20.019 --> 01:14:22.132
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you had to call them in . . .

1458
01:14:22.132 --> 01:14:22.434
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

1459
01:14:22.434 --> 01:14:23.340
<v ARSENEAULT>And say, "Hey . . ."

1460
01:14:23.340 --> 01:14:23.642
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

1461
01:14:23.642 --> 01:14:26.057
You’d call them and say, "I need propane."

1462
01:14:26.057 --> 01:14:29.075
And before that I remember we had a wood stove.

1463
01:14:29.075 --> 01:14:29.981
I remember that.

1464
01:14:29.981 --> 01:14:31.189
<v ARSENEAULT>So that's not propane.

1465
01:14:31.189 --> 01:14:31.792
You just . . .

1466
01:14:31.792 --> 01:14:32.396
<v RIGGS>No, no.

1467
01:14:32.396 --> 01:14:33.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1468
01:14:33.000 --> 01:14:39.044
<v RIGGS>The wood stove was you cut wood and you put it into the stove.

1469
01:14:39.044 --> 01:14:41.202
Into a big, iron stove.

1470
01:14:41.202 --> 01:14:44.224
And that's how you cooked on top.

1471
01:14:44.224 --> 01:14:48.973
Then you had a heater which was made out of iron.

1472
01:14:48.973 --> 01:14:52.858
It sat in the . . . basically in the living room.

1473
01:14:52.858 --> 01:14:58.902
And you had to put wood . . . cut wood and put it in the heater.

1474
01:14:58.902 --> 01:15:00.197
Start a fire.

1475
01:15:00.197 --> 01:15:07.967
Then we got the propane, and so then you got a heater that you light with a match.

1476
01:15:07.967 --> 01:15:09.694
‘35, I remember [laughs].

1477
01:15:09.694 --> 01:15:22.213
But I can't tell you . . . I can't remember when we changed over from the wood to the . . . I know I was probably . . . I would think I was maybe seven,

1478
01:15:22.213 --> 01:15:25.235
eight, or nine when we changed over.

1479
01:15:25.235 --> 01:15:29.120
I don't remember, but I do remember those things.

1480
01:15:29.120 --> 01:15:37.322
I remember about the stove because the stove would get hot and you’d set the pot on the stove.

1481
01:15:37.322 --> 01:15:46.820
We used to put popcorn on that stove and it would just pop and you’d . . . from the stove because it was hot.

1482
01:15:46.820 --> 01:15:48.978
<v ARSENEAULT>Did your dad grow popcorn?

1483
01:15:48.978 --> 01:15:52.000
<v RIGGS>No. No, we didn't grow popcorn.

1484
01:15:52.000 --> 01:15:55.414
<v ARSENEAULT>Was your mom the gardener or was your dad?

1485
01:15:55.414 --> 01:15:56.172
<v RIGGS>My mom.

1486
01:15:56.172 --> 01:15:56.931
My mom.

1487
01:15:56.931 --> 01:15:57.690
My mom.

1488
01:15:57.690 --> 01:16:06.793
And before that we had an ice box where the ice man came and brought your ice and you put that into your refrigerator.

1489
01:16:06.793 --> 01:16:12.103
And then they would come every other day or whenever and bring your ice.

1490
01:16:12.103 --> 01:16:15.138
<v ARSENEAULT>You just bought it by the cube or . . .?

1491
01:16:15.138 --> 01:16:16.655
<v RIGGS>It was like twenty-five.

1492
01:16:16.655 --> 01:16:17.034
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1493
01:16:17.034 --> 01:16:17.793
<v RIGGS>I think.

1494
01:16:17.793 --> 01:16:25.000
It had to fit into the little . . . Where the freezer is now, it had to fit in there.

1495
01:16:25.000 --> 01:16:26.368
<v ARSENEAULT>And that kept everything . . . ?

1496
01:16:26.368 --> 01:16:31.500
<v RIGGS>And it'd last a couple days or so and then they would come back [agrees].

1497
01:16:31.500 --> 01:16:31.842
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1498
01:16:31.842 --> 01:16:33.553
<v RIGGS>That was the ice box.

1499
01:16:33.553 --> 01:16:38.342
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember how much it cost for like a cube of ice, or?

1500
01:16:38.342 --> 01:16:39.368
<v RIGGS>Oh probably nothing.

1501
01:16:39.368 --> 01:16:41.763
I would say less than twenty-five cents.

1502
01:16:41.763 --> 01:16:42.789
<v ARSENEAULT>Some change now.

1503
01:16:42.789 --> 01:16:43.132
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

1504
01:16:43.132 --> 01:16:44.500
Less than twenty-five cents.

1505
01:16:44.500 --> 01:16:45.868
And when that happened,

1506
01:16:45.868 --> 01:16:51.000
I don't know when that . . . when the refrigerator . . . I don't remember . . . the first refrigerator.

1507
01:16:51.000 --> 01:16:52.823
<v ARSENEAULT>It wasn't like a big deal?

1508
01:16:52.823 --> 01:16:54.949
<v RIGGS>I guess it wasn't a big deal.

1509
01:16:54.949 --> 01:16:57.380
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah you were maybe already used to it.

1510
01:16:57.380 --> 01:16:57.684
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

1511
01:16:57.684 --> 01:16:59.810
I guess it wasn't a big deal.

1512
01:16:59.810 --> 01:17:03.152
I'll tell you who had one first though, Ira Garrett. [laughs]

1513
01:17:03.152 --> 01:17:04.671
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, for all his meat!

1514
01:17:04.671 --> 01:17:05.582
<v RIGGS>At his house.

1515
01:17:05.582 --> 01:17:05.886
Yeah.

1516
01:17:05.886 --> 01:17:09.835
You can remember the people . . . like I was not here with the televisions.

1517
01:17:09.835 --> 01:17:13.785
I was in Arkansas, but you can remember who had the first television.

1518
01:17:13.785 --> 01:17:15.911
Yeah, you can remember stuff like that.

1519
01:17:15.911 --> 01:17:18.646
<v ARSENEAULT>Was Ira Garrett the first one with a television?

1520
01:17:18.646 --> 01:17:19.861
<v RIGGS>He probably was there.

1521
01:17:19.861 --> 01:17:23.810
I know one thing we had, you had to listen to the radio.

1522
01:17:23.810 --> 01:17:27.759
And they would have like the football games now, they would have fights.

1523
01:17:27.759 --> 01:17:31.405
That's during the time Joe Lewis and people like that was fighting.

1524
01:17:31.405 --> 01:17:37.177
And other people would come over to your house. . . To our house and sit and listen to the fight.

1525
01:17:37.177 --> 01:17:39.000
They didn't have a radio.

1526
01:17:39.000 --> 01:17:40.771
<v ARSENEAULT>So that was a big . . .?

1527
01:17:40.771 --> 01:17:42.896
<v RIGGS>You sit around and you listen.

1528
01:17:42.896 --> 01:17:44.312
His girls laugh about.

1529
01:17:44.312 --> 01:17:46.792
They say, "You sit around and listen.

1530
01:17:46.792 --> 01:17:48.208
What was you doing?"

1531
01:17:48.208 --> 01:17:49.625
I said, "Just looking."

1532
01:17:49.625 --> 01:17:51.396
"What was you looking at?"

1533
01:17:51.396 --> 01:17:52.458
I said, "Nothing.

1534
01:17:52.458 --> 01:17:53.521
You listening." [laughs]

1535
01:17:53.521 --> 01:17:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You're staring into space and you're listening.

1536
01:17:56.000 --> 01:18:02.021
<v RIGGS>You're staring at . . . Yeah, you listen and then you’re real close because the static would get there.

1537
01:18:02.021 --> 01:18:04.854
You're real close and you . . . And baseball games.

1538
01:18:04.854 --> 01:18:06.625
Oh yeah, baseball games too.

1539
01:18:06.625 --> 01:18:08.396
You do the baseball games.

1540
01:18:08.396 --> 01:18:10.875
And then in 50s the TV came.

1541
01:18:10.875 --> 01:18:13.000
<v ARSENEAULT>When did y'all get one?

1542
01:18:13.000 --> 01:18:16.070
<v RIGGS>We got one in '54 or '52.

1543
01:18:16.070 --> 01:18:17.386
No, '52 . . . '53.

1544
01:18:17.386 --> 01:18:22.211
Fifty-three and it was probably as big as that thing there.

1545
01:18:22.211 --> 01:18:23.088
The furniture.

1546
01:18:23.088 --> 01:18:26.158
And the T.V. was about like this.

1547
01:18:26.158 --> 01:18:28.789
It was in a big case.

1548
01:18:28.789 --> 01:18:30.544
A big, big case.

1549
01:18:30.544 --> 01:18:33.614
And the screen was about like that.

1550
01:18:33.614 --> 01:18:35.807
<v ARSENEAULT>All the wires still working?

1551
01:18:35.807 --> 01:18:41.509
<v RIGGS>Yeah, I guess all the other stuff was in the back of that.

1552
01:18:41.509 --> 01:18:44.140
It was a piece of furniture.

1553
01:18:44.140 --> 01:18:46.772
<v ARSENEAULT>You set stuff on it and . . .?

1554
01:18:46.772 --> 01:18:47.649
<v RIGGS>Oh, yeah.

1555
01:18:47.649 --> 01:18:50.281
It was a piece of furniture.

1556
01:18:50.281 --> 01:18:52.035
<v ARSENEAULT>Now it's so different.

1557
01:18:52.035 --> 01:18:58.175
So y’all would listen to the radio and y'all would watch T.V. growing up.

1558
01:18:58.175 --> 01:19:03.000
So when you . . . Why did you go to school in . . .?

1559
01:19:03.000 --> 01:19:03.662
<v RIGGS>In Arkansas?

1560
01:19:03.662 --> 01:19:03.993
<v ARSENEAULT>[Agrees].

1561
01:19:03.993 --> 01:19:09.950
<v RIGGS>Because when it's time to go to high school they were transferring the . . . They were going to Westlake,

1562
01:19:09.950 --> 01:19:12.266
then it was up in the air.

1563
01:19:12.266 --> 01:19:15.245
They didn't know what they were going to do.

1564
01:19:15.245 --> 01:19:20.540
<c.p>They decided, "Okay, we're not going to do that we're going to send them to Washington."

1565
01:19:20.540 --> 01:19:24.180
So they had been doing that for two or three years.

1566
01:19:24.180 --> 01:19:27.158
They would do this and stop and do that.

1567
01:19:27.158 --> 01:19:33.777
My mother's sister lived in Hot Springs, Arkansas and I went up and stayed with her and went to school.

1568
01:19:33.777 --> 01:19:36.755
Came home during the summers, every holiday, and everything.

1569
01:19:36.755 --> 01:19:40.396
I guess I never skipped a beat with my classmates here.

1570
01:19:40.396 --> 01:19:44.036
I just stayed and finished high school then came back home.

1571
01:19:44.036 --> 01:19:45.360
<v ARSENEAULT>What school was it?

1572
01:19:45.360 --> 01:19:46.353
<v RIGGS>Langston High School.

1573
01:19:46.353 --> 01:19:47.345
Hot Springs, Arkansas.

1574
01:19:47.345 --> 01:19:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it a black . . .?

1575
01:19:49.000 --> 01:19:51.245
<v RIGGS>It was a black school.

1576
01:19:51.245 --> 01:19:53.939
It was a black school [agrees].

1577
01:19:53.939 --> 01:19:56.633
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about that?

1578
01:19:56.633 --> 01:19:58.429
Is it still there?

1579
01:19:58.429 --> 01:19:59.327
I wonder.

1580
01:19:59.327 --> 01:20:03.367
<v RIGGS>It's there, but it's not . . . It's the old part.

1581
01:20:03.367 --> 01:20:06.061
It's now . . . It's a media center.

1582
01:20:06.061 --> 01:20:12.796
It's still there and we do reunions every two years, and next year's my reunion.

1583
01:20:12.796 --> 01:20:21.327
But, well that was my . . . going to . . . First moving to the city and then going to a big school,

1584
01:20:21.327 --> 01:20:24.469
a two story school, and changing classes.

1585
01:20:24.469 --> 01:20:25.367
<v ARSENEAULT>[More?] changes.

1586
01:20:25.367 --> 01:20:27.163
<v RIGGS>I had to adjust.

1587
01:20:27.163 --> 01:20:32.551
I guess I was a country girl, but not a country girl.

1588
01:20:32.551 --> 01:20:41.980
But I had to adjust and it didn't take long to adjust and then I was one that was in everything.

1589
01:20:41.980 --> 01:20:49.163
I was in the band, and I was a majorette, and I played . . . I did everything.

1590
01:20:49.163 --> 01:20:51.857
And I just enjoyed high school.

1591
01:20:51.857 --> 01:20:55.000
But that was why I went.

1592
01:20:55.000 --> 01:20:57.754
<v ARSENEAULT>What was your first impression like coming to this?

1593
01:20:57.754 --> 01:20:58.672
You moved to . . .

1594
01:20:58.672 --> 01:21:00.813
<v RIGGS>When I moved to . . . When I moved?

1595
01:21:00.813 --> 01:21:01.119
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1596
01:21:01.119 --> 01:21:04.179
<v RIGGS>The first thing I went up in September I guess.

1597
01:21:04.179 --> 01:21:06.321
It was . . . I guess it was exciting.

1598
01:21:06.321 --> 01:21:11.828
I had to meet friends and I guess I'm a people person so I met them real well.

1599
01:21:11.828 --> 01:21:16.724
The big shocker was the first morning I woke up it was snow on the ground.

1600
01:21:16.724 --> 01:21:21.313
And of course I thought I didn't have to go to school, but I did.

1601
01:21:21.313 --> 01:21:22.843
That was my biggest shocker.

1602
01:21:22.843 --> 01:21:24.985
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have to walk to school?

1603
01:21:24.985 --> 01:21:25.291
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

1604
01:21:25.291 --> 01:21:25.491
I walked up the hill about two blocks to the school.

1605
01:21:25.291 --> 01:21:28.657
<v ARSENEAULT>[. . . ?]

1606
01:21:28.657 --> 01:21:29.575
<v RIGGS>I was ready.

1607
01:21:29.575 --> 01:21:32.022
I had the clothes, but I had . . . Huh?

1608
01:21:32.022 --> 01:21:34.164
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that your first time seeing snow?

1609
01:21:34.164 --> 01:21:35.388
<v RIGGS>I’d never seen snow.

1610
01:21:35.388 --> 01:21:36.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1611
01:21:36.000 --> 01:21:38.129
<v RIGGS>I’d never seen that much snow.

1612
01:21:38.129 --> 01:21:40.613
And Hot Spring is in the mountains.

1613
01:21:40.613 --> 01:21:40.968
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1614
01:21:40.968 --> 01:21:42.742
<v RIGGS>We were in the valley.

1615
01:21:42.742 --> 01:21:48.774
And I looked out, well I thought I didn’t have to go to school, but I did.

1616
01:21:48.774 --> 01:21:50.548
That was my first shock.

1617
01:21:50.548 --> 01:21:53.387
But other than that it worked out well.

1618
01:21:53.387 --> 01:21:54.806
<v ARSENEAULT>What about your siblings?

1619
01:21:54.806 --> 01:21:58.000
<v RIGGS>My brothers are eight years older than I am.

1620
01:21:58.000 --> 01:21:58.355
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1621
01:21:58.355 --> 01:22:02.968
<v RIGGS>And by the time I went to Arkansas they both were in service.

1622
01:22:02.968 --> 01:22:04.742
They went to service early.

1623
01:22:04.742 --> 01:22:11.129
And so they . . . I basically . . . A lot of the times in the summer I even went to visit

1624
01:22:11.129 --> 01:22:16.097
<c.p>them because one of them had married and they were out in west Texas.

1625
01:22:16.097 --> 01:22:18.935
They actually ended up living in west Texas,

1626
01:22:18.935 --> 01:22:24.613
but . . . and they both . . . When I moved to Houston and then they both moved to Houston.

1627
01:22:24.613 --> 01:22:29.226
So they're both deceased now, but we all ended up in Houston together.

1628
01:22:29.226 --> 01:22:31.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What did they join?

1629
01:22:31.000 --> 01:22:32.026
<v RIGGS>Air force.

1630
01:22:32.026 --> 01:22:33.564
<v ARSENEAULT>They both joined?

1631
01:22:33.564 --> 01:22:36.128
<v RIGGS>Joined the air force [agrees].

1632
01:22:36.128 --> 01:22:42.282
And their first place they went to was Barksdale in Shreveport, Louisiana.

1633
01:22:42.282 --> 01:22:44.333
That's where they were.

1634
01:22:44.333 --> 01:22:47.410
One went overseas and one didn't.

1635
01:22:47.410 --> 01:22:51.513
They went to San Antonio [Texas] to there.

1636
01:22:51.513 --> 01:22:55.615
They were in St. Angelo [San Angelo, Texas].

1637
01:22:55.615 --> 01:22:59.205
That was one place they were stationed.

1638
01:22:59.205 --> 01:23:01.256
Where else were they?

1639
01:23:01.256 --> 01:23:03.308
<v ARSENEAULT>What were their names?

1640
01:23:03.308 --> 01:23:05.872
<v RIGGS>Curtis Rigmaiden and Rudolph Rigmaiden.

1641
01:23:05.872 --> 01:23:07.923
<v ARSENEAULT>Curtis was the oldest?

1642
01:23:07.923 --> 01:23:11.000
<v RIGGS>Curtis was the oldest [agrees].

1643
01:23:11.000 --> 01:23:13.154
<v ARSENEAULT>Did they join at the same time?

1644
01:23:13.154 --> 01:23:18.385
<v RIGGS>Well Curtis joined first and as soon Rudolph turned . . . I think he actually didn't tell the truth.

1645
01:23:18.385 --> 01:23:22.692
I think he wasn't as old as he said he was when he joined.

1646
01:23:22.692 --> 01:23:26.385
And believe it or not they were stationed together for a year.

1647
01:23:26.385 --> 01:23:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1648
01:23:27.000 --> 01:23:28.125
<v RIGGS>Their first year.

1649
01:23:28.125 --> 01:23:35.250
Well Rudolph caught up with Curtis at Barksdale and they were together for a year and then they separated.

1650
01:23:35.250 --> 01:23:39.375
Well they were together . . . They went to St. Angelo together, too.

1651
01:23:39.375 --> 01:23:43.125
I guess maybe they applied to go there one time.

1652
01:23:43.125 --> 01:23:48.750
But they actually kind of stayed together even after they married and they the same . . .

1653
01:23:48.750 --> 01:23:49.875
<v ARSENEAULT>They were close?

1654
01:23:49.875 --> 01:23:50.250
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

1655
01:23:50.250 --> 01:23:51.000
Same cities.

1656
01:23:51.000 --> 01:23:53.625
<v ARSENEAULT>Did they ever serve active . . . See active . . .?

1657
01:23:53.625 --> 01:23:57.000
<v RIGGS>No. My brother . . . No. My brother went to Vietnam.

1658
01:23:57.000 --> 01:24:00.000
I mean Rudolph, I'm sorry, went to Vietnam.

1659
01:24:00.000 --> 01:24:01.500
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s the younger brother?

1660
01:24:01.500 --> 01:24:01.875
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

1661
01:24:01.875 --> 01:24:03.000
Curtis didn't.

1662
01:24:03.000 --> 01:24:05.208
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he ever talk about it?

1663
01:24:05.208 --> 01:24:05.944
<v RIGGS>Not much.

1664
01:24:05.944 --> 01:24:07.416
<v ARSENEAULT>What would he say?

1665
01:24:07.416 --> 01:24:09.992
<v RIGGS>He really didn't talk about it much.

1666
01:24:09.992 --> 01:24:13.304
Did he ever talk to you all about it?

1667
01:24:13.304 --> 01:24:14.408
<v WILLIAMS>[It wasn’t Vietnam.

1668
01:24:14.408 --> 01:24:15.512
It was Korea?]

1669
01:24:15.512 --> 01:24:16.248
<v RIGGS>Oh Korea.

1670
01:24:16.248 --> 01:24:16.984
I'm sorry.

1671
01:24:16.984 --> 01:24:17.352
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1672
01:24:17.352 --> 01:24:18.088
<v RIGGS>I'm sorry.

1673
01:24:18.088 --> 01:24:21.032
<v ARSENEAULT>I got . . . It's hard to keep the dates.

1674
01:24:21.032 --> 01:24:22.136
<v RIGGS>Yeah, I understand.

1675
01:24:22.136 --> 01:24:23.240
It was Korea.

1676
01:24:23.240 --> 01:24:28.392
No, I don't know . . . I don’t think he saw action, or maybe he did.

1677
01:24:28.392 --> 01:24:29.496
I don't know.

1678
01:24:29.496 --> 01:24:35.752
But he didn’t . . . he really . . . I mean he talked about it, but not really talk about it.

1679
01:24:35.752 --> 01:24:36.488
What happened.

1680
01:24:36.488 --> 01:24:37.224
He didn't.

1681
01:24:37.224 --> 01:24:40.168
<v ARSENEAULT>What were their like . . . MOS [Military Occupational Specialty]?

1682
01:24:40.168 --> 01:24:43.848
Or I'm not sure what the air force calls it.

1683
01:24:43.848 --> 01:24:44.952
<v RIGGS>I don't remember.

1684
01:24:44.952 --> 01:24:47.528
What they ended up . . . What was their . . .

1685
01:24:47.528 --> 01:24:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Like their job?

1686
01:24:49.000 --> 01:24:50.366
<v RIGGS>Oh okay, okay.

1687
01:24:50.366 --> 01:24:54.008
I thought you meant what they were doing.

1688
01:24:54.008 --> 01:24:57.650
I'm trying to think, what did Rudolph do?

1689
01:24:57.650 --> 01:25:00.837
Did Rudolph do the mechanic thing then?

1690
01:25:00.837 --> 01:25:04.935
I don't know if he did the mechanic thing.

1691
01:25:04.935 --> 01:25:06.301
I don't remember.

1692
01:25:06.301 --> 01:25:07.211
I really . . .

1693
01:25:07.211 --> 01:25:10.398
<v WILLIAMS>He ended up in Japan as well.

1694
01:25:10.398 --> 01:25:10.854
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1695
01:25:10.854 --> 01:25:13.585
<v RIGGS>Yeah he did go to Japan.

1696
01:25:13.585 --> 01:25:15.407
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that after Korea?

1697
01:25:15.407 --> 01:25:15.862
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

1698
01:25:15.862 --> 01:25:19.959
I don't remember what he . . . I'm trying to think.

1699
01:25:19.959 --> 01:25:25.423
I really don't remember what they did there in the say, service.

1700
01:25:25.423 --> 01:25:28.154
What they did in the service.

1701
01:25:28.154 --> 01:25:29.976
I'm trying to think.

1702
01:25:29.976 --> 01:25:31.797
I don't really remember.

1703
01:25:31.797 --> 01:25:33.163
I don't remember.

1704
01:25:33.163 --> 01:25:38.626
<v ARSENEAULT>Sounds like Rudolph went more overseas, and Curtis stayed in the states.

1705
01:25:38.626 --> 01:25:39.081
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

1706
01:25:39.081 --> 01:25:40.902
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he ever go?

1707
01:25:40.902 --> 01:25:43.179
<v RIGGS>No, Curtis never went overseas.

1708
01:25:43.179 --> 01:25:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>He always stayed?

1709
01:25:45.000 --> 01:25:47.450
<v RIGGS>He's always stayed in the states.

1710
01:25:47.450 --> 01:25:49.900
And I was trying to think.

1711
01:25:49.900 --> 01:25:58.475
I want to think maybe he did something like how they . . . what is it when you . . . They were sentenced up out.

1712
01:25:58.475 --> 01:26:00.517
He was like . . . for instance,

1713
01:26:00.517 --> 01:26:07.050
if they were sending . . . Let's just say . . . I don't even know what they would be sending.

1714
01:26:07.050 --> 01:26:08.275
In the supply.

1715
01:26:08.275 --> 01:26:09.908
Something about supply maybe.

1716
01:26:09.908 --> 01:26:11.542
I don't know what.

1717
01:26:11.542 --> 01:26:15.625
It seemed like he did something like in the supply.

1718
01:26:15.625 --> 01:26:18.892
Maybe in the warehouse or something about supplies.

1719
01:26:18.892 --> 01:26:24.608
Maybe that’s . . . I'm thinking of like the address where you would write squadron so-and-so.

1720
01:26:24.608 --> 01:26:25.017
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1721
01:26:25.017 --> 01:26:28.692
<v RIGGS>And this . . . I just can't remember what they did.

1722
01:26:28.692 --> 01:26:33.183
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember . . . do they . . . The highest rank they both achieved,

1723
01:26:33.183 --> 01:26:34.000
<c.p>or?

1724
01:26:34.000 --> 01:26:36.250
<v RIGGS>I don't remember that either.

1725
01:26:36.250 --> 01:26:42.100
Rudolph stayed in longer than Curtis and I don't remember . . . Too long ago.

1726
01:26:42.100 --> 01:26:42.550
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1727
01:26:42.550 --> 01:26:45.250
Did they retire in the service?

1728
01:26:45.250 --> 01:26:45.700
<v RIGGS>No.

1729
01:26:45.700 --> 01:26:47.500
<v ARSENEAULT>They both got out?

1730
01:26:47.500 --> 01:26:49.300
<v RIGGS>They both got out.

1731
01:26:49.300 --> 01:26:52.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What did they do after?

1732
01:26:52.000 --> 01:26:55.286
<v RIGGS>They both ended up in St. Angelo, Texas.

1733
01:26:55.286 --> 01:26:58.571
Rudolph worked . . . I know he was a mechanic.

1734
01:26:58.571 --> 01:27:06.786
So I don't know if he did that in the service, but I know he did when he got out.

1735
01:27:06.786 --> 01:27:09.661
He went to school in the G.I.

1736
01:27:09.661 --> 01:27:12.536
Bill and got certified as a mechanic.

1737
01:27:12.536 --> 01:27:15.000
And Curtis went on the G.I.

1738
01:27:15.000 --> 01:27:19.929
Bill and he did shoe repair, and he was also a minister.

1739
01:27:19.929 --> 01:27:28.554
But now when they retired, I know Rudolph did the mechanic stuff and Curtis was doing the shoe [repair] stuff, too.

1740
01:27:28.554 --> 01:27:31.839
But I also think he had a job.

1741
01:27:31.839 --> 01:27:36.768
He . . . I don't remember what it was but he had a job.

1742
01:27:36.768 --> 01:27:40.875
He worked someplace and I can't remember what it was.

1743
01:27:40.875 --> 01:27:43.339
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of minister was he?

1744
01:27:43.339 --> 01:27:43.750
<v RIGGS>Baptist.

1745
01:27:43.750 --> 01:27:47.446
That's what he ended up doing at the end.

1746
01:27:47.446 --> 01:27:52.786
He went to the seminary and then he ended up . . . Came to Houston.

1747
01:27:52.786 --> 01:27:56.482
Had a church in Houston, and then got sick.

1748
01:27:56.482 --> 01:28:01.000
He was there until he got sick and passed away.

1749
01:28:01.000 --> 01:28:04.862
<v ARSENEAULT>How old was he when he passed away?

1750
01:28:04.862 --> 01:28:07.276
<v RIGGS>He was fifty-four or fifty-three.

1751
01:28:07.276 --> 01:28:08.724
He was young.

1752
01:28:08.724 --> 01:28:11.138
He had . . . What was it?

1753
01:28:11.138 --> 01:28:11.621
Leukemia?

1754
01:28:11.621 --> 01:28:12.586
Or something.

1755
01:28:12.586 --> 01:28:14.034
He was young.

1756
01:28:14.034 --> 01:28:20.793
When you think about it he was very [ . . .?] in the 50s he was young.

1757
01:28:20.793 --> 01:28:27.552
Rudolph lived to eighty something and worked until he was probably close to eighty.

1758
01:28:27.552 --> 01:28:29.966
<v ARSENEAULT>Didn't let it stop him.

1759
01:28:29.966 --> 01:28:31.414
Kept on going.

1760
01:28:31.414 --> 01:28:31.897
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

1761
01:28:31.897 --> 01:28:33.345
And he boxed.

1762
01:28:33.345 --> 01:28:35.759
He boxed in the service.

1763
01:28:35.759 --> 01:28:36.724
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, wow.

1764
01:28:36.724 --> 01:28:38.655
<v RIGGS>He was a boxer.

1765
01:28:38.655 --> 01:28:39.621
But he . . .

1766
01:28:39.621 --> 01:28:41.552
<v ARSENEAULT>He stayed healthy maybe?

1767
01:28:41.552 --> 01:28:42.034
Or . . .

1768
01:28:42.034 --> 01:28:43.000
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

1769
01:28:43.000 --> 01:28:45.880
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he . . . did they . . . Did both play baseball, too?

1770
01:28:45.880 --> 01:28:46.520
<v RIGGS>Rudolph did.

1771
01:28:46.520 --> 01:28:47.160
Curtis didn't.

1772
01:28:47.160 --> 01:28:48.120
On that league.

1773
01:28:48.120 --> 01:28:48.760
Curtis, yeah.

1774
01:28:48.760 --> 01:28:51.640
I think Curtis was the announcer. [laughs] Rudolph played.

1775
01:28:51.640 --> 01:28:53.560
I think Curtis was the announcer.

1776
01:28:53.560 --> 01:28:55.160
<v ARSENEAULT>Was Curtis was the minister?

1777
01:28:55.160 --> 01:28:55.480
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

1778
01:28:55.480 --> 01:28:56.760
The minister was the . . .

1779
01:28:56.760 --> 01:28:59.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So he had the minister voice.

1780
01:28:59.000 --> 01:28:59.462
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

1781
01:28:59.462 --> 01:29:03.154
So he was the announcer . . . Who's that batting.

1782
01:29:03.154 --> 01:29:05.000
Yeah, he did that.

1783
01:29:05.000 --> 01:29:06.385
He did that.

1784
01:29:06.385 --> 01:29:07.308
The minister.

1785
01:29:07.308 --> 01:29:08.231
He did.

1786
01:29:08.231 --> 01:29:09.154
<v ARSENEAULT>Special memories.

1787
01:29:09.154 --> 01:29:13.308
Did anyone else in your family join the service?

1788
01:29:13.308 --> 01:29:17.000
Like a dad, or uncle, or grandfather, or?

1789
01:29:17.000 --> 01:29:18.385
<v RIGGS>My uncle, B.C.

1790
01:29:18.385 --> 01:29:22.538
Garrett Junior, and I didn't pick that stuff up.

1791
01:29:22.538 --> 01:29:26.692
He was the year of . . . With the Buffalo Soldiers.

1792
01:29:26.692 --> 01:29:27.615
<v ARSENEAULT>What's that?

1793
01:29:27.615 --> 01:29:29.000
Do tell.

1794
01:29:29.000 --> 01:29:34.000
<v RIGGS>The Buffalo Soldiers was the black cavalry that went west.

1795
01:29:34.000 --> 01:29:39.000
They were sent west and they dealt with the Indians.

1796
01:29:39.000 --> 01:29:43.500
I had . . . well I didn't . . . I pulled it up.

1797
01:29:43.500 --> 01:29:57.500
I had his papers where he went in and I had some things that his . . . A card from the president where when he went to his first assignment.

1798
01:29:57.500 --> 01:30:04.500
Well, the president wrote his father and said he had arrived at Fort Rucker.

1799
01:30:04.500 --> 01:30:09.000
And my mother had that, so I had that.

1800
01:30:09.000 --> 01:30:15.000
But anyway, the Buffalo Soldiers actually went ahead and fought the Indians.

1801
01:30:15.000 --> 01:30:18.000
<v ARSENEAULT>They kind of cleared the way?

1802
01:30:18.000 --> 01:30:19.000
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

1803
01:30:19.000 --> 01:30:21.009
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow he must have been . . .

1804
01:30:21.009 --> 01:30:26.231
<v RIGGS>And that was in . . . Well I know he went to Fort Huachuca, Arizona.

1805
01:30:26.231 --> 01:30:29.444
But I don't know what all he did.

1806
01:30:29.444 --> 01:30:30.650
But he did.

1807
01:30:30.650 --> 01:30:38.282
Then how long he was in there . . . But he was in the army and he was in the cavalry.

1808
01:30:38.282 --> 01:30:42.299
Now they're just doing a thing with the Buffalo Soldiers.

1809
01:30:42.299 --> 01:30:47.120
The Buffalo Soldiers was the black cavalry in whatever year that was.

1810
01:30:47.120 --> 01:30:51.137
And he was a minister and he lived in Mossville.

1811
01:30:51.137 --> 01:30:53.547
His family’s still here, Berkeley Junior.

1812
01:30:53.547 --> 01:30:57.564
<v ARSENEAULT>That was the man who started . . . Was the first teacher?

1813
01:30:57.564 --> 01:30:59.171
<v RIGGS>That was his son.

1814
01:30:59.171 --> 01:31:03.188
<v ARSENEAULT>That was . . . Do you remember how many kid he had?

1815
01:31:03.188 --> 01:31:03.590
<v RIGGS>Who?

1816
01:31:03.590 --> 01:31:05.197
Berkeley Junior or Senior?

1817
01:31:05.197 --> 01:31:06.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Senior.

1818
01:31:06.000 --> 01:31:06.348
<v RIGGS>Senior.

1819
01:31:06.348 --> 01:31:09.485
That was the one that had the two wives.

1820
01:31:09.485 --> 01:31:13.667
Four children with the first wife, five children with the second wife.

1821
01:31:13.667 --> 01:31:14.015
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1822
01:31:14.015 --> 01:31:18.197
And Berkeley Junior was in the first group or the second group?

1823
01:31:18.197 --> 01:31:19.242
<v RIGGS>The second group.

1824
01:31:19.242 --> 01:31:24.470
<v ARSENEAULT>So he was maybe the first child of the second group or something like that?

1825
01:31:24.470 --> 01:31:26.561
<v RIGGS>No, he was a third child . . .

1826
01:31:26.561 --> 01:31:26.909
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1827
01:31:26.909 --> 01:31:28.303
<v RIGGS>With the second group.

1828
01:31:28.303 --> 01:31:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1829
01:31:29.000 --> 01:31:33.829
<v RIGGS>But he . . . I don’t . . . I didn't pull . . . I have some stuff on that,

1830
01:31:33.829 --> 01:31:36.429
but I didn't bring it and stuff.

1831
01:31:36.429 --> 01:31:38.286
With him being in the . . .

1832
01:31:38.286 --> 01:31:42.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you know what Indian tribes he was fighting?

1833
01:31:42.000 --> 01:31:42.431
<v RIGGS>[Disagrees].

1834
01:31:42.431 --> 01:31:45.014
I don't know none of that.

1835
01:31:45.014 --> 01:31:54.057
The only reason I know that is that stuff I found that card in my . . . I knew that he was there,

1836
01:31:54.057 --> 01:32:01.808
but I found that card and I knew it was Fort Huachuca, and I've heard of Fort Huachuca.

1837
01:32:01.808 --> 01:32:06.975
And then maybe about ten years ago . . . Well not . . . maybe before then,

1838
01:32:06.975 --> 01:32:10.851
they started . . . I think congress recognized the Buffalo Soldiers.

1839
01:32:10.851 --> 01:32:20.324
And actually now they’ve just . . . They've been finding them and bringing them to Washington and giving them a medal of some kind.

1840
01:32:20.324 --> 01:32:22.046
I don't know what.

1841
01:32:22.046 --> 01:32:25.060
And Houston has a Buffalo Soldier museum.

1842
01:32:25.060 --> 01:32:26.352
It's pretty big.

1843
01:32:26.352 --> 01:32:33.242
But I can't tell you anything about what they . . . What he did because I don't know.

1844
01:32:33.242 --> 01:32:34.964
But he was young.

1845
01:32:34.964 --> 01:32:35.826
<v ARSENEAULT>How interesting.

1846
01:32:35.826 --> 01:32:38.409
I've never heard of that before.

1847
01:32:38.409 --> 01:32:40.132
<v RIGGS>Of the Buffalo Soldiers?

1848
01:32:40.132 --> 01:32:40.562
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1849
01:32:40.562 --> 01:32:41.854
That’s real neat.

1850
01:32:41.854 --> 01:32:43.577
<v RIGGS>That's what they were.

1851
01:32:43.577 --> 01:32:44.007
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1852
01:32:44.007 --> 01:32:49.605
And I guess people from Mossville, because I know a lot of the

1853
01:32:49.605 --> 01:32:54.772
<c.p>wild horses and everything, sounds like a lot of people rode horses.

1854
01:32:54.772 --> 01:32:55.633
<v RIGGS>They . . . Yeah.

1855
01:32:55.633 --> 01:32:57.356
That happened like lately.

1856
01:32:57.356 --> 01:32:58.217
After me.

1857
01:32:58.217 --> 01:32:59.509
The horse thing.

1858
01:32:59.509 --> 01:33:00.801
People had horses.

1859
01:33:00.801 --> 01:33:02.093
They had horses.

1860
01:33:02.093 --> 01:33:11.996
But like the horse thing now people are doing that . . . But B.C.'s children . . . I don't know if you've done . . . I'm sure you haven't.

1861
01:33:11.996 --> 01:33:20.178
I don't think my cousin has come to do anything, but he has a daughter that lives in Sulphur,

1862
01:33:20.178 --> 01:33:27.929
and one in D.C., and people in New Orleans, [Louisiana] but that's my mother's side of the family.

1863
01:33:27.929 --> 01:33:28.790
That's us.

1864
01:33:28.790 --> 01:33:30.943
Not that many of us.

1865
01:33:30.943 --> 01:33:36.110
I'm the oldest one left of the siblings, of my mother's siblings.

1866
01:33:36.110 --> 01:33:38.263
I'm the only child left.

1867
01:33:38.263 --> 01:33:39.986
Everybody else is grandchildren.

1868
01:33:39.986 --> 01:33:40.416
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1869
01:33:40.416 --> 01:33:43.000
How does that feel like?

1870
01:33:43.000 --> 01:33:44.270
<v RIGGS>It feels good.

1871
01:33:44.270 --> 01:33:45.964
I really feels good.

1872
01:33:45.964 --> 01:33:49.350
It's like all the nieces and nephews, yeah.

1873
01:33:49.350 --> 01:33:51.891
<v ARSENEAULT>So we are probably . . . Let's see,

1874
01:33:51.891 --> 01:33:59.511
do you remember anything about them talking about the Great Depression or victory gardens or anything like that?

1875
01:33:59.511 --> 01:34:01.628
<v RIGGS>No. I really, really don't.

1876
01:34:01.628 --> 01:34:06.708
I remember . . . I vaguely remember . . . I started to say the food stamps.

1877
01:34:06.708 --> 01:34:07.978
The rationing stamps.

1878
01:34:07.978 --> 01:34:14.328
I don't remember . . . I remember that they had to have a stamp to get gas.

1879
01:34:14.328 --> 01:34:19.409
You had to have a stamp to get shoes and get sugar.

1880
01:34:19.409 --> 01:34:24.066
I guess stuff was . . . It was the Depression things were rationed.

1881
01:34:24.066 --> 01:34:27.876
But I don't remember . . . I remember the little book.

1882
01:34:27.876 --> 01:34:35.073
I remember it was a book and that you opened it and would tear I guess whatever.

1883
01:34:35.073 --> 01:34:36.343
I remember my

1884
01:34:36.343 --> 01:34:39.307
<c.p>mother keeping it in a safe place.

1885
01:34:39.307 --> 01:34:41.000
<v ARSENEAULT>In the safe?

1886
01:34:41.000 --> 01:34:41.326
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

1887
01:34:41.326 --> 01:34:42.631
In a safe place.

1888
01:34:42.631 --> 01:34:50.788
And like for instance if you had five children I think each . . . I think you had two pair of shoes a year with that stamp.

1889
01:34:50.788 --> 01:34:58.945
So if you wore your shoes out you couldn't get any more shoes unless you bought a stamp from somebody else or something like that.

1890
01:34:58.945 --> 01:34:59.924
I remember that.

1891
01:34:59.924 --> 01:35:02.534
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it like a certain kind of shoe?

1892
01:35:02.534 --> 01:35:03.186
<v RIGGS>No, no.

1893
01:35:03.186 --> 01:35:05.144
<v ARSENEAULT>Did they all look the same?

1894
01:35:05.144 --> 01:35:07.428
<v RIGGS>No you just had to have stamps.

1895
01:35:07.428 --> 01:35:09.712
It wasn't a certain kind of shoe.

1896
01:35:09.712 --> 01:35:11.669
It was like things were rationed.

1897
01:35:11.669 --> 01:35:15.258
So you couldn't buy but two pair of shoes a year.

1898
01:35:15.258 --> 01:35:15.911
<v ARSENEAULT>I see.

1899
01:35:15.911 --> 01:35:16.564
<v RIGGS>No one.

1900
01:35:16.564 --> 01:35:22.110
But then maybe some people didn't have the money to buy their two pair or one pair.

1901
01:35:22.110 --> 01:35:29.941
I remember my mother telling me this for the Depression, and it’s funny, my dad came to the Port of Lake Charles and worked.

1902
01:35:29.941 --> 01:35:33.530
And they paid him in a gold piece and he was

1903
01:35:33.530 --> 01:35:33.856
<c.p>mad.

1904
01:35:33.856 --> 01:35:36.140
I think a five dollar gold piece.

1905
01:35:36.140 --> 01:35:39.076
He did not want that five dollar gold piece.

1906
01:35:39.076 --> 01:35:40.381
He didn't want it.

1907
01:35:40.381 --> 01:35:41.686
He wanted five dollars.

1908
01:35:41.686 --> 01:35:47.559
So my mother like had to like go to the bank with him and get his five dollars.

1909
01:35:47.559 --> 01:35:49.517
He didn't want that gold piece.

1910
01:35:49.517 --> 01:35:56.042
So I guess money was not in circulation or whatever and they paid him with a five dollar gold piece.

1911
01:35:56.042 --> 01:35:58.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Probably worth a lot now!

1912
01:35:58.000 --> 01:36:01.424
<v RIGGS>He didn't want it, and we laughed about that.

1913
01:36:01.424 --> 01:36:02.946
He didn't want it.

1914
01:36:02.946 --> 01:36:07.511
<v ARSENEAULT>Did they talk about the Depression, like how hard it was, or?

1915
01:36:07.511 --> 01:36:15.880
<v RIGGS>Well yeah I guess . . . Not, not really, but that was one of the things I remember about what they said about that.

1916
01:36:15.880 --> 01:36:16.261
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1917
01:36:16.261 --> 01:36:17.402
That stuck out.

1918
01:36:17.402 --> 01:36:17.783
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

1919
01:36:17.783 --> 01:36:19.685
<v ARSENEAULT>Did your mom make clothes?

1920
01:36:19.685 --> 01:36:21.207
<v RIGGS>No. She didn't sew.

1921
01:36:21.207 --> 01:36:21.967
She crocheted.

1922
01:36:21.967 --> 01:36:23.870
Oh she was a crotchetier.

1923
01:36:23.870 --> 01:36:27.674
She crocheted a lot, and knit, but she didn't sew.

1924
01:36:27.674 --> 01:36:28.815
She didn’t sew.

1925
01:36:28.815 --> 01:36:30.337
She wasn't a seamstress.

1926
01:36:30.337 --> 01:36:33.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did y'all get the clothes?

1927
01:36:33.000 --> 01:36:33.913
<v RIGGS>We bought them.

1928
01:36:33.913 --> 01:36:34.217
<v ARSENEAULT>Ready-made?

1929
01:36:34.217 --> 01:36:35.435
<v RIGGS>We basically bought them.

1930
01:36:35.435 --> 01:36:41.217
And then it was a lady that sewed in Mossville, and like I know she would make me dresses.

1931
01:36:41.217 --> 01:36:47.000
Sometimes like just before school she might make me three or four dresses, or two or three, or whatever.

1932
01:36:47.000 --> 01:36:47.304
Then . . .

1933
01:36:47.304 --> 01:36:50.348
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you get to pick out the fabric or anything?

1934
01:36:50.348 --> 01:36:50.652
<v RIGGS>Yes.

1935
01:36:50.652 --> 01:36:54.000
We'd go get the fabric and we'd get the dresses made.

1936
01:36:54.000 --> 01:36:57.043
Then basically was Sears, and then we'd order from catalogues.

1937
01:36:57.043 --> 01:36:58.565
Anybody talk about a catalog?

1938
01:36:58.565 --> 01:36:58.870
<v ARSENEAULT>Maybe.

1939
01:36:58.870 --> 01:36:59.783
<v RIGGS>Yeah, a catalog.

1940
01:36:59.783 --> 01:37:01.609
Well now they have catalogues, too.

1941
01:37:01.609 --> 01:37:03.130
But Sears had a catalog . . .

1942
01:37:03.130 --> 01:37:06.174
<v ARSENEAULT>They talked about using them as toilet paper I think.

1943
01:37:06.174 --> 01:37:08.000
In an outhouse or something.

1944
01:37:08.000 --> 01:37:13.000
<v RIGGS>No. Yeah, people probably did, but that's how a lot of people shopped

1945
01:37:13.000 --> 01:37:17.231
<c.p>because they didn’t . . . Couldn't get some place else so you ordered.

1946
01:37:17.231 --> 01:37:26.462
But I always think maybe we ordered some stuff from Sears, and wasn't a Penny's then, but basically I remember coming to Lake Charles.

1947
01:37:26.462 --> 01:37:28.385
I remember coming to Sears.

1948
01:37:28.385 --> 01:37:30.308
What else was here then?

1949
01:37:30.308 --> 01:37:30.692
Muller’s.

1950
01:37:30.692 --> 01:37:32.231
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it all segregated?

1951
01:37:32.231 --> 01:37:32.615
<v RIGGS>Huh?

1952
01:37:32.615 --> 01:37:36.462
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it all segregated when y'all would come to shop?

1953
01:37:36.462 --> 01:37:38.000
Was it only . . .?

1954
01:37:38.000 --> 01:37:38.847
<v RIGGS>The restaurant.

1955
01:37:38.847 --> 01:37:40.119
Just the restaurant.

1956
01:37:40.119 --> 01:37:41.814
No. Basically the restaurant.

1957
01:37:41.814 --> 01:37:46.475
You, you know . . . The restaurant was segregated, but not really shopping.

1958
01:37:46.475 --> 01:37:49.441
Not . . . We didn't really have much here.

1959
01:37:49.441 --> 01:37:53.678
When I was going to McNeese we did the bus.

1960
01:37:53.678 --> 01:38:00.458
We just got on the front seat of the bus and next day that was over.

1961
01:38:00.458 --> 01:38:01.305
And then . . .

1962
01:38:01.305 --> 01:38:03.424
<v ARSENEAULT>So you did . . . You protested?

1963
01:38:03.424 --> 01:38:03.847
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

1964
01:38:03.847 --> 01:38:08.085
On the bus and the sitting at the lunch counter.

1965
01:38:08.085 --> 01:38:09.780
That . . . Nothing happened really.

1966
01:38:09.780 --> 01:38:16.136
When we . . . With the bus, got on the bus and sat on the front seat.

1967
01:38:16.136 --> 01:38:27.153
And then by the time we got out to McNeese they were there to take us off, but we got off before we got there. [laughs]

1968
01:38:27.153 --> 01:38:28.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Smart.

1969
01:38:28.000 --> 01:38:29.600
<v RIGGS>So nothing happened, so.

1970
01:38:29.600 --> 01:38:32.400
<v ARSENEAULT>Who was there to take you off?

1971
01:38:32.400 --> 01:38:32.800
<v RIGGS>NAACP.

1972
01:38:32.800 --> 01:38:33.200
No!

1973
01:38:33.200 --> 01:38:40.400
The police was there to take the people off that got on that was on the front seat.

1974
01:38:40.400 --> 01:38:44.000
But the NAACP got us off a block before.

1975
01:38:44.000 --> 01:38:45.600
That was the bus.

1976
01:38:45.600 --> 01:38:50.000
Nothing really big here when I was here during that time.

1977
01:38:50.000 --> 01:38:52.000
<v ARSENEAULT>No protests or anything?

1978
01:38:52.000 --> 01:38:54.990
<v RIGGS>Yeah, they had protests, but nothing happened.

1979
01:38:54.990 --> 01:38:56.271
No big ones.

1980
01:38:56.271 --> 01:38:57.552
Just sit down.

1981
01:38:57.552 --> 01:38:58.406
<v ARSENEAULT>The peaceful.

1982
01:38:58.406 --> 01:39:00.542
<v RIGGS>At . . . Now not Mossville now.

1983
01:39:00.542 --> 01:39:03.531
We were talking about just Lake Charles.

1984
01:39:03.531 --> 01:39:06.948
But I don't . . . I really don't know anybody.

1985
01:39:06.948 --> 01:39:16.771
I did because I was over here going to McNeese so I was in the group that . . . I didn't do the lunch counter,

1986
01:39:16.771 --> 01:39:20.188
but I was here when they did it.

1987
01:39:20.188 --> 01:39:21.469
Then the bus.

1988
01:39:21.469 --> 01:39:23.177
But was no reaction.

1989
01:39:23.177 --> 01:39:24.031
No nothing.

1990
01:39:24.031 --> 01:39:25.740
Nobody went to jail.

1991
01:39:25.740 --> 01:39:31.719
No . . . I don't know what anybody else experienced at . . . They may have after that.

1992
01:39:31.719 --> 01:39:43.250
I'm sure that something . . . like with Westlake and Sulphur and with . . . I'm sure that . . . And see some of the kids in Mossville also went to Sulphur High.

1993
01:39:43.250 --> 01:39:45.385
They were really pushed around.

1994
01:39:45.385 --> 01:39:51.365
They went from one place to another place to . . . And they went to Sulphur.

1995
01:39:51.365 --> 01:39:57.344
So I don't know what happened in the 50s with the high school in

1996
01:39:57.344 --> 01:39:57.771
<c.p>Mossville.

1997
01:39:57.771 --> 01:40:02.042
Like I say, in '56 they had their [fair?] school.

1998
01:40:02.042 --> 01:40:03.750
So that ended that.

1999
01:40:03.750 --> 01:40:06.312
<v ARSENEAULT>So you came back from Arkansas . . .

2000
01:40:06.312 --> 01:40:07.167
<v RIGGS>In '55.

2001
01:40:07.167 --> 01:40:11.010
<v ARSENEAULT>And started in . . . You started next year at McNeese?

2002
01:40:11.010 --> 01:40:12.292
Or you started . . .?

2003
01:40:12.292 --> 01:40:14.000
<v RIGGS>That same year.

2004
01:40:14.000 --> 01:40:14.811
<v ARSENEAULT>Same year.

2005
01:40:14.811 --> 01:40:16.027
<v RIGGS>In September. [Agrees].

2006
01:40:16.027 --> 01:40:16.432
September.

2007
01:40:16.432 --> 01:40:20.081
But then after that people were going to McNeese.

2008
01:40:20.081 --> 01:40:29.405
That was the year it turned over from a junior to a four year college because it was a junior when I applied.

2009
01:40:29.405 --> 01:40:34.270
But as far as Mossville, wasn't nothing in Mossville to integrate, see.

2010
01:40:34.270 --> 01:40:43.595
So . . . But I don't know now what happened when the children from . . . Now the children from Mossville also did go to Westlake High,

2011
01:40:43.595 --> 01:40:46.432
and they also went to Sulphur High.

2012
01:40:46.432 --> 01:40:50.486
So they did have a period of integration from Mossville.

2013
01:40:50.486 --> 01:40:57.378
Because before when we were talking about those other schools that bussing, that was to black schools.

2014
01:40:57.378 --> 01:41:03.865
So they did have . . . Now some people should be able to tell you about that integration.

2015
01:41:03.865 --> 01:41:04.270
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

2016
01:41:04.270 --> 01:41:07.919
<v RIGGS>I don't think . . . Now Butch finished from Mossville High.

2017
01:41:07.919 --> 01:41:11.973
We laughed about it when I got out the car.

2018
01:41:11.973 --> 01:41:15.216
I said, "Now how many years is it?"

2019
01:41:15.216 --> 01:41:16.838
And he said, "Nine."

2020
01:41:16.838 --> 01:41:18.054
Between our ages.

2021
01:41:18.054 --> 01:41:19.676
His sister . . . Now, nine.

2022
01:41:19.676 --> 01:41:21.703
He said it was nine.

2023
01:41:21.703 --> 01:41:23.730
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay, because he's the youngest?

2024
01:41:23.730 --> 01:41:25.351
<v RIGGS>Because he came . . . Yes.

2025
01:41:25.351 --> 01:41:29.000
He was . . . he came after . . . We was ten . . .

2026
01:41:29.000 --> 01:41:29.400
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

2027
01:41:29.400 --> 01:41:31.800
<v RIGGS>When she had this little brother.

2028
01:41:31.800 --> 01:41:33.400
<v ARSENEAULT>He was a surprise.

2029
01:41:33.400 --> 01:41:33.800
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

2030
01:41:33.800 --> 01:41:37.400
<v ARSENEAULT>I bet y'all teased him so much growing up.

2031
01:41:37.400 --> 01:41:39.800
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].Yeah he was tagging it along.

2032
01:41:39.800 --> 01:41:40.200
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

2033
01:41:40.200 --> 01:41:41.000
So funny.

2034
01:41:41.000 --> 01:41:43.800
So McNeese, what was your major there?

2035
01:41:43.800 --> 01:41:45.800
<v RIGGS>Physical education at the time.

2036
01:41:45.800 --> 01:41:47.400
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you finish out?

2037
01:41:47.400 --> 01:41:47.800
<v RIGGS>No.

2038
01:41:47.800 --> 01:41:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What happened?

2039
01:41:49.000 --> 01:41:49.492
<v RIGGS>Didn't.

2040
01:41:49.492 --> 01:41:52.935
I . . . That was my results at McNeese.

2041
01:41:52.935 --> 01:41:56.871
I didn't go back, and I should have.

2042
01:41:56.871 --> 01:41:59.331
I did not go back.

2043
01:41:59.331 --> 01:42:03.758
I had junior status and just never went back.

2044
01:42:03.758 --> 01:42:11.629
Married, moved to Houston, and started back and didn't start [TSU?], and I just never finished.

2045
01:42:11.629 --> 01:42:13.105
<v ARSENEAULT>So physical education.

2046
01:42:13.105 --> 01:42:17.040
You were going to be a P.E. teacher?

2047
01:42:17.040 --> 01:42:17.532
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

2048
01:42:17.532 --> 01:42:21.468
<v ARSENEAULT>So what was McNeese like at the time?

2049
01:42:21.468 --> 01:42:22.944
Like what was . . .?

2050
01:42:22.944 --> 01:42:26.879
Well maybe first impressions or something like that.

2051
01:42:26.879 --> 01:42:28.355
<v RIGGS>McNeese was good.

2052
01:42:28.355 --> 01:42:37.210
Now that was a . . . It was hard because we were the first blacks there, but it went well.

2053
01:42:37.210 --> 01:42:38.194
Nothing happened.

2054
01:42:38.194 --> 01:42:41.637
After the first semester everything was just . . .

2055
01:42:41.637 --> 01:42:42.129
<v ARSENEAULT>Calm?

2056
01:42:42.129 --> 01:42:43.113
<v RIGGS>Calm. [Agrees].

2057
01:42:43.113 --> 01:42:48.524
And by I think it was seven people or six people.

2058
01:42:48.524 --> 01:42:49.508
I can't

2059
01:42:49.508 --> 01:42:50.984
<c.p>remember how many.

2060
01:42:50.984 --> 01:43:00.331
You probably didn't see another person except at lunch and by the second year it was more people there.

2061
01:43:00.331 --> 01:43:02.298
And it was good.

2062
01:43:02.298 --> 01:43:04.758
It was a good experience.

2063
01:43:04.758 --> 01:43:11.645
<v ARSENEAULT>What were . . . Did you have many memorable teachers, or classes, or anything like that?

2064
01:43:11.645 --> 01:43:18.040
<v RIGGS>Yeah, I had one that I liked that was . . . that taught Louisiana history.

2065
01:43:18.040 --> 01:43:20.500
Dr. [Suarez?], I liked him.

2066
01:43:20.500 --> 01:43:21.976
He was good.

2067
01:43:21.976 --> 01:43:25.419
I remember Ms. [Kit?] she was good.

2068
01:43:25.419 --> 01:43:38.702
Basically we did . . . spent a lot of time in the . . . I got to think about what they were called . . . The churches had organizations . . . The Baptist Student Center.

2069
01:43:38.702 --> 01:43:47.065
And that's what we did because basically a lot of the things at McNeese was not open.

2070
01:43:47.065 --> 01:43:51.000
When I say they were not open . . .

2071
01:43:51.000 --> 01:43:52.381
<v ARSENEAULT>Open to black people?

2072
01:43:52.381 --> 01:43:52.727
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

2073
01:43:52.727 --> 01:43:54.108
<v ARSENEAULT>They were white only?

2074
01:43:54.108 --> 01:43:54.453
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

2075
01:43:54.453 --> 01:43:55.489
They were closed.

2076
01:43:55.489 --> 01:43:58.597
Like I went to . . . It had a sign up.

2077
01:43:58.597 --> 01:44:02.050
It said, "All P.E. majors come to the girl's gym."

2078
01:44:02.050 --> 01:44:05.849
So it was a black boy and me so we went.

2079
01:44:05.849 --> 01:44:14.827
And when we were sitting here the fellow got up and said, "Well this is an invitation only meeting, so you go to have your invitation.”

2080
01:44:14.827 --> 01:44:19.662
So I just turned around I said, "Y'all need to get your invitations out."

2081
01:44:19.662 --> 01:44:23.115
So nobody had an invitation, so we didn't leave either.

2082
01:44:23.115 --> 01:44:24.151
But it was . . .

2083
01:44:24.151 --> 01:44:27.259
<v ARSENEAULT>He was trying to bully y'all to get out.

2084
01:44:27.259 --> 01:44:31.748
<v RIGGS>Yeah he was . . . Make us get out because we didn't have an invitation.

2085
01:44:31.748 --> 01:44:35.892
I guess they thought we were going to get up and leave.

2086
01:44:35.892 --> 01:44:37.273
And we didn’t leave.

2087
01:44:37.273 --> 01:44:39.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What happened after that?

2088
01:44:39.000 --> 01:44:40.916
<v RIGGS>They had the meeting.

2089
01:44:40.916 --> 01:44:46.666
I never really had anything where someone was ugly or called names.

2090
01:44:46.666 --> 01:44:57.685
I had a B in the physical education at the second year and so you were with the other people that was there,

2091
01:44:57.685 --> 01:45:02.955
and had a lot of football players that was in that.

2092
01:45:02.955 --> 01:45:08.705
So some of them became very . . . Not friends, but they were friendly.

2093
01:45:08.705 --> 01:45:12.058
So I never really had any problems.

2094
01:45:12.058 --> 01:45:16.370
Most of the people did not have any problems.

2095
01:45:16.370 --> 01:45:18.766
McNeese integrated just kind of . . .

2096
01:45:18.766 --> 01:45:22.120
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about Dr. King?

2097
01:45:22.120 --> 01:45:26.432
Did y'all listen to any of his stuff, or?

2098
01:45:26.432 --> 01:45:26.911
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

2099
01:45:26.911 --> 01:45:32.181
I was at McNeese when he did the march on Washington.

2100
01:45:32.181 --> 01:45:35.056
And did I ever see him?

2101
01:45:35.056 --> 01:45:41.284
Yeah I’ve seen him at . . . I saw him at a rally in Houston.

2102
01:45:41.284 --> 01:45:46.075
Never did any marches, but basically his theory was good.

2103
01:45:46.075 --> 01:45:51.345
I did visit the grave site and stuff in Atlanta [Georgia].

2104
01:45:51.345 --> 01:45:54.220
Went to Atlanta and visited there.

2105
01:45:54.220 --> 01:46:01.886
But I just think he did a great job in what he did and lived for.

2106
01:46:01.886 --> 01:46:06.198
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember where you were when he was . . .?

2107
01:46:06.198 --> 01:46:06.677
<v RIGGS>Yes.

2108
01:46:06.677 --> 01:46:10.510
I was in Houston when he was assassinated.

2109
01:46:10.510 --> 01:46:12.426
I was in Houston.

2110
01:46:12.426 --> 01:46:17.217
In fact, we were getting ready to go to Arkansas.

2111
01:46:17.217 --> 01:46:19.613
It was close to Easter.

2112
01:46:19.613 --> 01:46:23.446
We were getting ready to go to Arkansas.

2113
01:46:23.446 --> 01:46:26.799
And people, "Are you going to travel?"

2114
01:46:26.799 --> 01:46:29.674
Said, "Yeah, I'm going to travel."

2115
01:46:29.674 --> 01:46:33.507
We were in . . . I was living in Houston.

2116
01:46:33.507 --> 01:46:36.861
<v ARSENEAULT>Were they kind of nervous or something?

2117
01:46:36.861 --> 01:46:39.256
<v RIGGS>No. Well it didn't . . . No,

2118
01:46:39.256 --> 01:46:45.964
it didn't really . . . It didn't upset . . . It upset me for him to get killed.

2119
01:46:45.964 --> 01:46:51.234
I wasn't afraid that something was going to happen to me.

2120
01:46:51.234 --> 01:46:57.942
I knew that they were just killing him because of his cause and that.

2121
01:46:57.942 --> 01:47:03.691
I didn't think they were going around just opening up on people.

2122
01:47:03.691 --> 01:47:13.273
So it didn't . . . Wasn't that kind of thing that frightened me or made me not want to go to nothing.

2123
01:47:13.273 --> 01:47:18.543
I would have gone to rallies if they had had one.

2124
01:47:18.543 --> 01:47:22.376
I wasn't the type that would not go.

2125
01:47:22.376 --> 01:47:27.167
But none of the marches was I afraid of participating.

2126
01:47:27.167 --> 01:47:31.000
I would have if I needed to.

2127
01:47:31.000 --> 01:47:33.176
<v ARSENEAULT>What about President Kennedy?

2128
01:47:33.176 --> 01:47:35.352
Do you remember his?

2129
01:47:35.352 --> 01:47:36.440
<v RIGGS>I remember.

2130
01:47:36.440 --> 01:47:38.616
Actually I was working.

2131
01:47:38.616 --> 01:47:42.424
I was at work when it happened.

2132
01:47:42.424 --> 01:47:49.496
I was working at a YMCA here in Lake Charles when it happened.

2133
01:47:49.496 --> 01:47:51.128
That was devastating.

2134
01:47:51.128 --> 01:47:59.288
And it was devastating not only because . . . It was devastating because it was the president.

2135
01:47:59.288 --> 01:48:10.168
And it wasn't because of black, or white or . . . it was just like you just didn't believe that was . . . happened.

2136
01:48:10.168 --> 01:48:17.784
That someone would kill the president for any reason, regardless of who he was.

2137
01:48:17.784 --> 01:48:19.960
That was very devastating.

2138
01:48:19.960 --> 01:48:29.208
Both of those deaths were bad, but I don't know, that just really got me with him.

2139
01:48:29.208 --> 01:48:31.928
I just couldn’t believe it.

2140
01:48:31.928 --> 01:48:35.736
It was something you just really couldn't.

2141
01:48:35.736 --> 01:48:39.000
<v ARSENEAULT>He's going to wake up.

2142
01:48:39.000 --> 01:48:39.446
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

2143
01:48:39.446 --> 01:48:43.011
That you couldn't believe that this had happened.

2144
01:48:43.011 --> 01:48:50.141
That the United States was . . . somebody was doing something to the president of the United States.

2145
01:48:50.141 --> 01:48:53.261
That's how I felt about that one.

2146
01:48:53.261 --> 01:48:59.946
And actually I was watching the news when [Ruby?] shot . . . I saw that on T.V.

2147
01:48:59.946 --> 01:49:01.728
That was on T.V.

2148
01:49:01.728 --> 01:49:08.413
When they were bringing him out and they shot . . . I was looking at that, so.

2149
01:49:08.413 --> 01:49:09.750
<v ARSENEAULT>Little crazy time.

2150
01:49:09.750 --> 01:49:10.641
<v RIGGS>Yeah, crazy.

2151
01:49:10.641 --> 01:49:11.533
That’s right.

2152
01:49:11.533 --> 01:49:11.978
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

2153
01:49:11.978 --> 01:49:16.435
I hate to call it that, but it was wild.

2154
01:49:16.435 --> 01:49:18.217
Everyone was getting assassinated.

2155
01:49:18.217 --> 01:49:22.228
<v RIGGS>And people . . . It wasn't a black or white thing.

2156
01:49:22.228 --> 01:49:23.565
People mourned together.

2157
01:49:23.565 --> 01:49:26.685
Even with Dr. King people did that.

2158
01:49:26.685 --> 01:49:31.587
It was just a human thing when you think about it.

2159
01:49:31.587 --> 01:49:33.370
That's how I felt.

2160
01:49:33.370 --> 01:49:38.272
<v ARSENEAULT>On a lighter note, we talked about some of the entertainment.

2161
01:49:38.272 --> 01:49:44.511
You said Ms. Martill's but that was kind of when you were in Arkansas.

2162
01:49:44.511 --> 01:49:46.293
What about the movies?

2163
01:49:46.293 --> 01:49:50.750
Where was . . . Did y'all go to the movies in Mossville?

2164
01:49:50.750 --> 01:49:56.989
<v RIGGS>Yeah, we went to the movies in . . . Well yeah, the movie was in Westlake.

2165
01:49:56.989 --> 01:49:58.326
<v ARSENEAULT>Remember the theater?

2166
01:49:58.326 --> 01:50:01.000
The name of the theater?

2167
01:50:01.000 --> 01:50:01.729
<v RIGGS>Probably West . . .

2168
01:50:01.729 --> 01:50:02.458
<v ARSENEAULT>Just Westlake?

2169
01:50:02.458 --> 01:50:05.740
<v RIGGS>I don't remember, but I can just see it.

2170
01:50:05.740 --> 01:50:07.562
<v ARSENEAULT>Just Westlake Theater or something?

2171
01:50:07.562 --> 01:50:09.750
<v RIGGS>No, I don't remember that one.

2172
01:50:09.750 --> 01:50:12.302
I remember the one in Hot Springs.

2173
01:50:12.302 --> 01:50:15.219
I don't remember what it was in Westlake.

2174
01:50:15.219 --> 01:50:18.500
They also had a drive-in on the new 90.

2175
01:50:18.500 --> 01:50:22.146
Over on the new 90 that we went to young.

2176
01:50:22.146 --> 01:50:25.427
The movies . . . They had the movies in Lake Charles.

2177
01:50:25.427 --> 01:50:29.438
I can't even think of the name of the ones here.

2178
01:50:29.438 --> 01:50:31.625
One of them was The Dixie.

2179
01:50:31.625 --> 01:50:37.458
I think that might have been a black movie that was over on Railroad Avenue here.

2180
01:50:37.458 --> 01:50:38.188
<v ARSENEAULT>The Dixie?

2181
01:50:38.188 --> 01:50:38.552
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

2182
01:50:38.552 --> 01:50:40.740
I think it was The Dixie.

2183
01:50:40.740 --> 01:50:48.031
Now you know at the Westlake movie it was integrated so the blacks went upstairs and the blacks went downstairs.

2184
01:50:48.031 --> 01:50:52.042
That was . . . But I just think it was the Westlake Theater.

2185
01:50:52.042 --> 01:50:53.865
I don't remember a name.

2186
01:50:53.865 --> 01:50:56.781
And at the drive-in you just went in.

2187
01:50:56.781 --> 01:50:58.240
<v ARSENEAULT>You just drove in.

2188
01:50:58.240 --> 01:50:58.969
Doesn't matter . . .

2189
01:50:58.969 --> 01:51:00.427
<v RIGGS>Just drove in [agrees].

2190
01:51:00.427 --> 01:51:01.521
It didn't matter.

2191
01:51:01.521 --> 01:51:03.708
But I can't remember the name.

2192
01:51:03.708 --> 01:51:11.000
There was some movies, other movies here that I'm trying to remember the names, but I can't remember them.

2193
01:51:11.000 --> 01:51:14.671
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember any movies you saw?

2194
01:51:14.671 --> 01:51:15.195
<v RIGGS>Huh?

2195
01:51:15.195 --> 01:51:17.293
<v ARSENEAULT>Like at the drive-in.

2196
01:51:17.293 --> 01:51:20.963
You remember any movies that you saw?

2197
01:51:20.963 --> 01:51:22.537
Any particular ones?

2198
01:51:22.537 --> 01:51:23.585
<v RIGGS>Oh please.

2199
01:51:23.585 --> 01:51:28.829
Way back . . . I don't even remember back that far back.

2200
01:51:28.829 --> 01:51:32.500
<v ARSENEAULT>I'm sure they were black and white.

2201
01:51:32.500 --> 01:51:33.549
<v RIGGS>Oh, yeah.

2202
01:51:33.549 --> 01:51:41.415
Most of the . . . They had movies that was a love stories and stuff like that.

2203
01:51:41.415 --> 01:51:44.561
Most of the movies were westerns.

2204
01:51:44.561 --> 01:51:45.610
Westerns and . . .

2205
01:51:45.610 --> 01:51:49.280
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember your first color movie?

2206
01:51:49.280 --> 01:51:54.000
<v RIGGS>No. I don't remember my first color movie.

2207
01:51:54.000 --> 01:51:57.265
<v ARSENEAULT>If you think of it you can tell us.

2208
01:51:57.265 --> 01:52:01.257
Was there anywhere you could listen to zydeco music in Mossville?

2209
01:52:01.257 --> 01:52:04.885
<v RIGGS>I'm not a zydeco girl, but I know about zydeco.

2210
01:52:04.885 --> 01:52:09.602
What about . . . Do I listen to it or did I listen to it?

2211
01:52:09.602 --> 01:52:13.956
<v ARSENEAULT>Did anybody in the community play in a zydeco band or anything?

2212
01:52:13.956 --> 01:52:21.212
<v RIGGS>I knew somebody, but they were from Westlake and it was [Cliff Shenear?] and his sister and I were friends.

2213
01:52:21.212 --> 01:52:24.115
He actually became big and recorded and stuff.

2214
01:52:24.115 --> 01:52:32.823
But when I was growing up and in here, in Mossville, well see we didn't have many people in Mossville that talked Creole either.

2215
01:52:32.823 --> 01:52:33.912
Wasn't that many.

2216
01:52:33.912 --> 01:52:35.000
I mean . . .

2217
01:52:35.000 --> 01:52:36.447
<v ARSENEAULT>Did anyone speak it?

2218
01:52:36.447 --> 01:52:36.809
<v RIGGS>No.

2219
01:52:36.809 --> 01:52:37.894
<v ARSENEAULT>In the community?

2220
01:52:37.894 --> 01:52:40.426
<v RIGGS>No, not when I was growing up.

2221
01:52:40.426 --> 01:52:41.872
<v ARSENEAULT>You don't really remember?

2222
01:52:41.872 --> 01:52:45.489
<v RIGGS>If it was somebody married someone and then they did.

2223
01:52:45.489 --> 01:52:47.298
But no. That I knew.

2224
01:52:47.298 --> 01:52:52.000
So and I knew about zydeco, but not like it is now.

2225
01:52:52.000 --> 01:52:53.333
<v ARSENEAULT>So I was just . . .

2226
01:52:53.333 --> 01:52:56.000
<v RIGGS>And I learned to zydeco as an adult.

2227
01:52:56.000 --> 01:52:56.333
<v ARSENEAULT>Really?

2228
01:52:56.333 --> 01:52:56.667
<v RIGGS>[Agrees]

2229
01:52:56.667 --> 01:52:58.667
<v ARSENEAULT>What does it mean to zydeco?

2230
01:52:58.667 --> 01:53:00.000
<v RIGGS>What does it mean?

2231
01:53:00.000 --> 01:53:01.333
Zydeco is a two-step.

2232
01:53:01.333 --> 01:53:04.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So it's a dance and a music?

2233
01:53:04.000 --> 01:53:04.418
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

2234
01:53:04.418 --> 01:53:06.507
It's a dance and music.

2235
01:53:06.507 --> 01:53:11.103
It's a music, and it's a music that you dance to.

2236
01:53:11.103 --> 01:53:11.521
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

2237
01:53:11.521 --> 01:53:16.952
<v RIGGS>So I don't know if Butch and them even . . . They may know now.

2238
01:53:16.952 --> 01:53:20.712
<v ARSENEAULT>But growing up there wasn't like a big thing?

2239
01:53:20.712 --> 01:53:21.130
<v RIGGS>No.

2240
01:53:21.130 --> 01:53:21.548
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

2241
01:53:21.548 --> 01:53:21.966
<v RIGGS>No.

2242
01:53:21.966 --> 01:53:24.890
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of dancing would y'all do?

2243
01:53:24.890 --> 01:53:30.322
<v RIGGS>Just swing and jitterbug, or just that's what it was at that time.

2244
01:53:30.322 --> 01:53:31.158
Just swung.

2245
01:53:31.158 --> 01:53:34.082
Swung and danced fast and dance slow.

2246
01:53:34.082 --> 01:53:35.336
Wasn't any names.

2247
01:53:35.336 --> 01:53:36.171
<v ARSENEAULT>You just . . .

2248
01:53:36.171 --> 01:53:39.096
<v RIGGS>Yeah, you just got out and dancing.

2249
01:53:39.096 --> 01:53:42.438
You danced by . . . with people like at school.

2250
01:53:42.438 --> 01:53:47.034
You danced with the girls and got the dance, you see.

2251
01:53:47.034 --> 01:53:50.377
And then you had to teach the boys.

2252
01:53:50.377 --> 01:53:56.226
Basically the sisters would teach their brothers and then you would dance with them.

2253
01:53:56.226 --> 01:54:05.000
But you probably didn't dance with them until about seventh, eighth grade or farther because boys they didn’t dance much.

2254
01:54:05.000 --> 01:54:06.536
<v ARSENEAULT>They played baseball instead.

2255
01:54:06.536 --> 01:54:08.073
<v RIGGS>They played baseball [agrees].

2256
01:54:08.073 --> 01:54:09.225
They played baseball.

2257
01:54:09.225 --> 01:54:13.450
Probably basically would . . . most boys, I don't know that in girls,

2258
01:54:13.450 --> 01:54:22.285
they probably don't start . . . boys probably don't dance at all until probably their first prom or the . . . Unless they do it at home.

2259
01:54:22.285 --> 01:54:28.046
Now like we at home, we danced and played music, and danced and . . . Our family.

2260
01:54:28.046 --> 01:54:29.967
That was something we did.

2261
01:54:29.967 --> 01:54:33.040
But I don't even know when Jeffery danced.

2262
01:54:33.040 --> 01:54:38.033
If he danced before he went to high school, or in middle school.

2263
01:54:38.033 --> 01:54:41.106
You know what I mean, away from home.

2264
01:54:41.106 --> 01:54:43.026
But we danced at home.

2265
01:54:43.026 --> 01:54:44.563
<v ARSENEAULT>What was the Jitterbug?

2266
01:54:44.563 --> 01:54:44.947
<v RIGGS>Huh?

2267
01:54:44.947 --> 01:54:46.099
<v ARSENEAULT>What's the Jitterbug?

2268
01:54:46.099 --> 01:54:50.325
<v RIGGS>The Jitterbug is a fast dance just like I just said.

2269
01:54:50.325 --> 01:54:57.238
You dance fast and together, and then the fellow will swing you out and turn you a little.

2270
01:54:57.238 --> 01:55:01.079
And you just dance and then you get back together.

2271
01:55:01.079 --> 01:55:03.000
It's a fast dance.

2272
01:55:03.000 --> 01:55:07.373
<v ARSENEAULT>Is there any particular music that you have to listen to?

2273
01:55:07.373 --> 01:55:08.566
<v RIGGS>No, not really.

2274
01:55:08.566 --> 01:55:11.349
Well t's a beat . . . It's fast music.

2275
01:55:11.349 --> 01:55:14.133
<v ARSENEAULT>So it's got to be like a . . .?

2276
01:55:14.133 --> 01:55:14.530
<v RIGGS>Yes.

2277
01:55:14.530 --> 01:55:18.904
It's got to have a beat and be a fast music.

2278
01:55:18.904 --> 01:55:28.843
And if you were like at a formal or something it’d be a slow . . . Before my time the slow dance was called the slow drag.

2279
01:55:28.843 --> 01:55:30.831
Like you said the Jitterbug.

2280
01:55:30.831 --> 01:55:35.205
But during my time in high school time we did stuff.

2281
01:55:35.205 --> 01:55:37.193
They had dances with names.

2282
01:55:37.193 --> 01:55:40.373
Like you did maybe the monkey . . . certain things.

2283
01:55:40.373 --> 01:55:42.361
Then you did something else.

2284
01:55:42.361 --> 01:55:45.145
That was like a United States thing.

2285
01:55:45.145 --> 01:55:47.530
Somebody would . . . They did it everywhere.

2286
01:55:47.530 --> 01:55:48.723
<v ARSENEAULT>Like a trend.

2287
01:55:48.723 --> 01:55:49.120
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

2288
01:55:49.120 --> 01:55:54.289
And then you would watch the Soul Train on T.V. or Dick Clark.

2289
01:55:54.289 --> 01:55:59.458
In high school I listened to Dick Clark and danced to that, too.

2290
01:55:59.458 --> 01:56:03.036
So you just danced to what the music was.

2291
01:56:03.036 --> 01:56:05.422
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have a favorite musician?

2292
01:56:05.422 --> 01:56:09.000
Or a favorite song or anything like that?

2293
01:56:09.000 --> 01:56:10.010
<v RIGGS>Not really.

2294
01:56:10.010 --> 01:56:12.029
I did and didn't.

2295
01:56:12.029 --> 01:56:16.573
I liked to dance even like in high school.

2296
01:56:16.573 --> 01:56:22.126
I liked to dance whether it was slow, fast, just dance.

2297
01:56:22.126 --> 01:56:24.146
But nothing in particular.

2298
01:56:24.146 --> 01:56:25.155
Maybe Jitterbug.

2299
01:56:25.155 --> 01:56:26.165
Something fast.

2300
01:56:26.165 --> 01:56:29.699
Maybe that would be my favorite dance.

2301
01:56:29.699 --> 01:56:33.738
<v ARSENEAULT>But no favorite musician or anything like that?

2302
01:56:33.738 --> 01:56:36.767
<v RIGGS>Well in high school maybe, yes.

2303
01:56:36.767 --> 01:56:41.311
Let's see, I don't even know who that was.

2304
01:56:41.311 --> 01:56:49.388
I'm just trying to think who would have been if I would have had a favorite.

2305
01:56:49.388 --> 01:56:52.417
<v ARSENEAULT>We can come back to it.

2306
01:56:52.417 --> 01:56:52.922
<v RIGGS>Okay.

2307
01:56:52.922 --> 01:56:53.427
Okay.

2308
01:56:53.427 --> 01:56:56.456
<v ARSENEAULT>What about like courting or dating?

2309
01:56:56.456 --> 01:56:58.981
What did y'all call it?

2310
01:56:58.981 --> 01:56:59.990
<v RIGGS>Same thing.

2311
01:56:59.990 --> 01:57:01.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

2312
01:57:01.000 --> 01:57:02.306
<v RIGGS>Dating or courting.

2313
01:57:02.306 --> 01:57:07.529
Now we didn't date and go on dates until high school probably.

2314
01:57:07.529 --> 01:57:12.318
Where the fellow came over and took you to the movie.

2315
01:57:12.318 --> 01:57:21.459
You might have met him at the movie, but they didn't come and get you and take you to the movie.

2316
01:57:21.459 --> 01:57:28.859
The group of girls go to the movie and then you meet your boyfriend at the movie.

2317
01:57:28.859 --> 01:57:34.953
And then like when you got in high school then maybe you would date.

2318
01:57:34.953 --> 01:57:42.353
Or you would go to a social, or go to a school dance, or something like that.

2319
01:57:42.353 --> 01:57:44.529
I'm just trying to think.

2320
01:57:44.529 --> 01:57:55.847
And see you didn't really have to go on a date because if the dance was after the school . . . the football game you were already there.

2321
01:57:55.847 --> 01:57:58.894
Yeah so it really wasn't no date.

2322
01:57:58.894 --> 01:58:04.553
You didn't . . . Basically you first date was probably the prom I would think.

2323
01:58:04.553 --> 01:58:08.471
That was probably my first date, was the prom.

2324
01:58:08.471 --> 01:58:11.518
<v ARSENEAULT>Were parents strict or anything like that?

2325
01:58:11.518 --> 01:58:15.000
I guess it'd depend on the parent.

2326
01:58:15.000 --> 01:58:19.074
<v RIGGS>Well they just all kind of had the same rule.

2327
01:58:19.074 --> 01:58:21.926
The rule was you did this a

2328
01:58:21.926 --> 01:58:24.778
<c.p>certain age and this a certain age.

2329
01:58:24.778 --> 01:58:26.000
Some people did.

2330
01:58:26.000 --> 01:58:27.222
Some people didn't.

2331
01:58:27.222 --> 01:58:29.667
Some parents had rules some didn't.

2332
01:58:29.667 --> 01:58:34.556
<v ARSENEAULT>How were y'all expected to behave in front of adults growing up?

2333
01:58:34.556 --> 01:58:39.037
<v RIGGS>You were expected to be a child in front of adults.

2334
01:58:39.037 --> 01:58:41.481
You were expected to respect people.

2335
01:58:41.481 --> 01:58:43.519
Respect what was going on.

2336
01:58:43.519 --> 01:58:48.815
Basically if adults gathered together you would be happy because you'd go play.

2337
01:58:48.815 --> 01:58:50.852
Maybe they brought their children.

2338
01:58:50.852 --> 01:58:57.370
You'd go on somewhere else and you didn't sit around adults and be in the conversation.

2339
01:58:57.370 --> 01:58:59.000
Stuff like that.

2340
01:58:59.000 --> 01:59:02.528
<v ARSENEAULT>How would your parents discipline you and your brothers?

2341
01:59:02.528 --> 01:59:06.057
<v RIGGS>Well they would talk and . . . Well my dad didn't.

2342
01:59:06.057 --> 01:59:13.898
I think my dad spanked me once with a newspaper, but my mom did the discipline, but not a lot.

2343
01:59:13.898 --> 01:59:15.074
Not a lot.

2344
01:59:15.074 --> 01:59:18.210
You had to do something real, real bad.

2345
01:59:18.210 --> 01:59:24.091
Something she had told you to do over and over and you didn’t do it.

2346
01:59:24.091 --> 01:59:26.051
Then you may get it.

2347
01:59:26.051 --> 01:59:32.324
But our thing was we would always all three get it because she would say, "Okay,

2348
01:59:32.324 --> 01:59:39.381
you said you didn't do it, and you said you didn't do it, and you didn't do it.

2349
01:59:39.381 --> 01:59:44.477
So I'll get all three of you and I'll have the right one."

2350
01:59:44.477 --> 01:59:46.438
<v ARSENEAULT>A one in three chance.

2351
01:59:46.438 --> 01:59:48.790
<v RIGGS>A one in the three chances.

2352
01:59:48.790 --> 01:59:49.182
Yeah.

2353
01:59:49.182 --> 01:59:51.534
You'd go, "You better tell it."

2354
01:59:51.534 --> 01:59:53.494
That was our group [laughs].

2355
01:59:53.494 --> 01:59:54.278
<v ARSENEAULT>That's funny.

2356
01:59:54.278 --> 01:59:55.455
<v RIGGS>But not bad.

2357
01:59:55.455 --> 01:59:57.023
Not . . . They weren't strict.

2358
01:59:57.023 --> 01:59:59.767
I'm just trying to think what else.

2359
01:59:59.767 --> 02:00:04.864
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember like an age where you knew you were grown up?

2360
02:00:04.864 --> 02:00:08.000
Like when you finally knew you were . . .?

2361
02:00:08.000 --> 02:00:11.574
<v RIGGS>I guess probably my senior year in high school.

2362
02:00:11.574 --> 02:00:18.722
I think that's where it was because you think about, "Oh, this is going to be over soon."

2363
02:00:18.722 --> 02:00:21.502
And what am I going to do?

2364
02:00:21.502 --> 02:00:24.679
I think that's where it was for me.

2365
02:00:24.679 --> 02:00:27.458
Because see, I was away from home.

2366
02:00:27.458 --> 02:00:31.032
I knew that I was going to come home.

2367
02:00:31.032 --> 02:00:33.812
I never stopped going back to Arkansas.

2368
02:00:33.812 --> 02:00:35.401
I still do that.

2369
02:00:35.401 --> 02:00:40.960
But I knew that I was going to leave there and not live there.

2370
02:00:40.960 --> 02:00:44.534
I didn't think I was anymore, and I didn't.

2371
02:00:44.534 --> 02:00:47.711
So I ended up back home and here.

2372
02:00:47.711 --> 02:00:52.079
But I really do think that's when it was for me.

2373
02:00:52.079 --> 02:00:54.462
My senior year in high school.

2374
02:00:54.462 --> 02:00:58.433
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have any chores you were expected to do?

2375
02:00:58.433 --> 02:01:02.007
Like your parents expected you to do certain things?

2376
02:01:02.007 --> 02:01:02.801
<v RIGGS>Oh yeah.

2377
02:01:02.801 --> 02:01:05.184
We had to do certain things.

2378
02:01:05.184 --> 02:01:05.978
You did.

2379
02:01:05.978 --> 02:01:07.964
Everybody had things to do.

2380
02:01:07.964 --> 02:01:11.935
I think my first thing was like sweep the porch.

2381
02:01:11.935 --> 02:01:13.921
Get the . . . Sweep the porch.

2382
02:01:13.921 --> 02:01:15.112
Then do dishes.

2383
02:01:15.112 --> 02:01:23.848
And once I . . . With them being a little bit older we didn't do as much rotation, like you do dishes this week.

2384
02:01:23.848 --> 02:01:25.040
My brothers did.

2385
02:01:25.040 --> 02:01:34.570
And then when I got up to doing dishes they were about gone, so I had to do all the dishes at that time.

2386
02:01:34.570 --> 02:01:36.556
But we did have chores.

2387
02:01:36.556 --> 02:01:36.953
Yeah.

2388
02:01:36.953 --> 02:01:39.336
Had things you had to do.

2389
02:01:39.336 --> 02:01:40.924
Take out the trash.

2390
02:01:40.924 --> 02:01:43.704
Oh, we hung clothes on the line.

2391
02:01:43.704 --> 02:01:48.072
We washed them and hung them on the line so you

2392
02:01:48.072 --> 02:01:53.235
<c.p>had to either hang up the clothes, bring in the clothes, fold clothes.

2393
02:01:53.235 --> 02:01:54.823
Yeah, we had chores.

2394
02:01:54.823 --> 02:01:56.014
Even the boys.

2395
02:01:56.014 --> 02:01:58.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did your mom cook?

2396
02:01:58.000 --> 02:01:58.333
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

2397
02:01:58.333 --> 02:02:00.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you ever help her out cooking?

2398
02:02:00.667 --> 02:02:01.000
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

2399
02:02:01.000 --> 02:02:04.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things did she teach you?

2400
02:02:04.000 --> 02:02:06.615
<v RIGGS>Well most of how to cook things.

2401
02:02:06.615 --> 02:02:13.712
How to . . . once I was . . . Well I was I guess old enough to go in the kitchen and cook.

2402
02:02:13.712 --> 02:02:17.821
I would be in there and she'd let me do things.

2403
02:02:17.821 --> 02:02:21.183
For instance, like peel potatoes and snap beans . . . stuff.

2404
02:02:21.183 --> 02:02:27.533
And then breakfast was the first thing I think I really learned to cook, and then rice.

2405
02:02:27.533 --> 02:02:33.136
One thing you learned to do was, being from Louisiana, you learned to make coffee.

2406
02:02:33.136 --> 02:02:36.125
That was a thing you had to do.

2407
02:02:36.125 --> 02:02:38.739
You had to learn to make coffee.

2408
02:02:38.739 --> 02:02:45.463
You had to . . . because they had the coffee pot that you poured the water into and it dripped.

2409
02:02:45.463 --> 02:02:45.837
<v ARSENEAULT>Percolator?

2410
02:02:45.837 --> 02:02:48.078
<v RIGGS>No, it was a drip drip.

2411
02:02:48.078 --> 02:02:49.572
They had the percolator.

2412
02:02:49.572 --> 02:02:57.043
But you would boil the water and you had a coffee pot with the grounds in it and a reservoir.

2413
02:02:57.043 --> 02:03:02.646
Once the water boiled you poured the . . . over the coffee and it dripped it again.

2414
02:03:02.646 --> 02:03:05.634
But the kicker was the coffee was greasy.

2415
02:03:05.634 --> 02:03:08.623
It had . . . Coffee had a little grease so . . .

2416
02:03:08.623 --> 02:03:09.370
<v ARSENEAULT>The oils?

2417
02:03:09.370 --> 02:03:10.117
<v RIGGS>The oil.

2418
02:03:10.117 --> 02:03:16.467
You had to wash that coffee pot and make sure that the cups didn't have the grease.

2419
02:03:16.467 --> 02:03:21.696
I guess because it went through and the coffee went through a little filter.

2420
02:03:21.696 --> 02:03:23.191
Not a good filter.

2421
02:03:23.191 --> 02:03:31.782
Because they . . . my mother would say, "You didn't wash the coffee pot because I can taste the oil,” or “I feel the oil.”

2422
02:03:31.782 --> 02:03:36.638
So you had to learn to wash that coffee pot and make coffee.

2423
02:03:36.638 --> 02:03:40.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember where you got your coffee?

2424
02:03:40.000 --> 02:03:45.375
<v RIGGS>Well she bought it at the grocery store and she also bought some standard coffee.

2425
02:03:45.375 --> 02:03:50.750
Was a person that sold coffee and I think it came from New Orleans somewhere.

2426
02:03:50.750 --> 02:03:53.975
They would come through and she would buy coffee.

2427
02:03:53.975 --> 02:03:55.408
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it already ground?

2428
02:03:55.408 --> 02:03:58.633
<v RIGGS>They had . . . the first . . . Some of it was not.

2429
02:03:58.633 --> 02:04:02.933
Some that she bought . . . Sometimes she'd buy the beans and roast them.

2430
02:04:02.933 --> 02:04:04.008
<v ARSENEAULT>Do it herself?

2431
02:04:04.008 --> 02:04:04.367
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

2432
02:04:04.367 --> 02:04:05.800
And then grind them.

2433
02:04:05.800 --> 02:04:07.592
<v ARSENEAULT>How would she grind them?

2434
02:04:07.592 --> 02:04:15.475
<v RIGGS>A little grinder that sat on the side of the table that hooked to the table and you would grind the coffee.

2435
02:04:15.475 --> 02:04:20.133
I mean you put the coffee in this little thing and turn it.

2436
02:04:20.133 --> 02:04:23.000
Kind of like the electric one now.

2437
02:04:23.000 --> 02:04:25.171
<v ARSENEAULT>But it was . . . This one was manual?

2438
02:04:25.171 --> 02:04:25.481
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

2439
02:04:25.481 --> 02:04:26.411
This was manual.

2440
02:04:26.411 --> 02:04:28.891
<v ARSENEAULT>Did she ever grind anything else in there?

2441
02:04:28.891 --> 02:04:29.822
<v RIGGS>Not in that.

2442
02:04:29.822 --> 02:04:31.682
That was a little coffee grinder.

2443
02:04:31.682 --> 02:04:33.233
<v ARSENEAULT>That was just for coffee?

2444
02:04:33.233 --> 02:04:34.473
<v RIGGS>There was another thing.

2445
02:04:34.473 --> 02:04:36.953
The grinder that you did to grind meat.

2446
02:04:36.953 --> 02:04:41.295
That went on the side of the table when you making dressing and stuff.

2447
02:04:41.295 --> 02:04:42.535
It had a grinder.

2448
02:04:42.535 --> 02:04:45.016
Yeah she used to do the coffee beans.

2449
02:04:45.016 --> 02:04:50.287
And when I go in the store now, like sometimes you’re in the big store and you'll

2450
02:04:50.287 --> 02:04:56.178
<c.p>see the coffee beans where you buy them and you take them home and roast them then grind them.

2451
02:04:56.178 --> 02:04:58.039
<v ARSENEAULT>Nothing better than fresh roasted coffee.

2452
02:04:58.039 --> 02:04:58.659
<v RIGGS>It's not.

2453
02:04:58.659 --> 02:04:59.899
It smells so good.

2454
02:04:59.899 --> 02:05:00.829
<v ARSENEAULT>It's the best.

2455
02:05:00.829 --> 02:05:03.000
Where did y'all get the meat?

2456
02:05:03.000 --> 02:05:05.365
<v RIGGS>At the . . . Now I remember stores.

2457
02:05:05.365 --> 02:05:14.038
At the stores and in Mossville during the . . . Just I think probably in the summer and just in the winter or whenever.

2458
02:05:14.038 --> 02:05:16.798
I don't know when you did this.

2459
02:05:16.798 --> 02:05:19.558
People would butcher, they called it cows.

2460
02:05:19.558 --> 02:05:22.317
And then they would sell the meat.

2461
02:05:22.317 --> 02:05:27.837
So like you would buy meat, but see you didn't have a deep freezer.

2462
02:05:27.837 --> 02:05:32.173
You only bought enough that your refrigerator . . . But I remember that.

2463
02:05:32.173 --> 02:05:33.750
They would do that.

2464
02:05:33.750 --> 02:05:35.721
And they would do hogs.

2465
02:05:35.721 --> 02:05:43.606
But with the hog, we didn't have a smoke house though, but with the hogs they would smoke the meat.

2466
02:05:43.606 --> 02:05:48.337
They had a house out back and they would smoke the meat.

2467
02:05:48.337 --> 02:05:50.308
The bacon and the ham.

2468
02:05:50.308 --> 02:05:52.279
<v ARSENEAULT>I bet that smelled good.

2469
02:05:52.279 --> 02:05:55.433
<v RIGGS>And that was . . . It would hang up. [Agrees].

2470
02:05:55.433 --> 02:05:57.010
It was hanging up.

2471
02:05:57.010 --> 02:05:57.798
And sausage.

2472
02:05:57.798 --> 02:05:59.375
They made their own.

2473
02:05:59.375 --> 02:06:02.529
<v ARSENEAULT>Did your mom have a famous recipe or . . .?

2474
02:06:02.529 --> 02:06:07.260
<v RIGGS>Well my mother did a cheesecake, and she did a pound cake.

2475
02:06:07.260 --> 02:06:08.837
And she liked them.

2476
02:06:08.837 --> 02:06:13.173
I guess she liked both of those, so those she did.

2477
02:06:13.173 --> 02:06:15.144
She made chicken and dumplings.

2478
02:06:15.144 --> 02:06:17.904
People liked her chicken and dumplings, so.

2479
02:06:17.904 --> 02:06:25.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did your dad have a favorite meal that she cooked or anything that he would request, or?

2480
02:06:25.000 --> 02:06:27.655
<v RIGGS>Not that I really, really know.

2481
02:06:27.655 --> 02:06:32.964
I mean I didn't know of anything that he really, really liked.

2482
02:06:32.964 --> 02:06:38.273
I can't say that it was fried chicken or . . . I don't remember.

2483
02:06:38.273 --> 02:06:40.927
<v ARSENEAULT>Did your mom fry her chicken?

2484
02:06:40.927 --> 02:06:41.370
<v RIGGS>Yes.

2485
02:06:41.370 --> 02:06:42.697
She fried chicken.

2486
02:06:42.697 --> 02:06:44.024
She fried chicken.

2487
02:06:44.024 --> 02:06:45.794
She would fry chicken.

2488
02:06:45.794 --> 02:06:48.891
And basically she made biscuits . . . Made bread.

2489
02:06:48.891 --> 02:06:51.545
She actually made a light bread.

2490
02:06:51.545 --> 02:06:55.085
I remember her doing that as a child.

2491
02:06:55.085 --> 02:06:57.739
She would do . . . make light bread.

2492
02:06:57.739 --> 02:07:00.394
<v ARSENEAULT>She just cooked in the oven?

2493
02:07:00.394 --> 02:07:00.836
<v RIGGS>Huh?

2494
02:07:00.836 --> 02:07:03.933
<v ARSENEAULT>She just made it in the oven?

2495
02:07:03.933 --> 02:07:04.376
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

2496
02:07:04.376 --> 02:07:06.145
<v ARSENEAULT>Fresh bread from scratch?

2497
02:07:06.145 --> 02:07:07.473
<v RIGGS>Yeah, from scratch.

2498
02:07:07.473 --> 02:07:12.782
Make it in the oven and then you had to slice it.

2499
02:07:12.782 --> 02:07:15.879
It was like a little pound cake.

2500
02:07:15.879 --> 02:07:22.958
I remember her making bread, or . . . I'm trying to think of something else that she liked.

2501
02:07:22.958 --> 02:07:26.939
She liked . . . Oh she liked to make pecan candy.

2502
02:07:26.939 --> 02:07:30.479
Just before Christmas that was what she liked.

2503
02:07:30.479 --> 02:07:35.788
She would do pecan candy and different kind of candies and cookies.

2504
02:07:35.788 --> 02:07:38.000
<v ARSENEAULT>She like the sweets?

2505
02:07:38.000 --> 02:07:38.327
<v RIGGS>Cookies.

2506
02:07:38.327 --> 02:07:39.635
We had pecan trees.

2507
02:07:39.635 --> 02:07:41.923
That was our job, to peel pecans.

2508
02:07:41.923 --> 02:07:43.558
<v ARSENEAULT>They were in the yard?

2509
02:07:43.558 --> 02:07:45.192
<v RIGGS>They were in the yard.

2510
02:07:45.192 --> 02:07:49.769
You couldn't say that you didn’t have any because you could go get them.

2511
02:07:49.769 --> 02:07:50.750
<v ARSENEAULT>They're expensive now.

2512
02:07:50.750 --> 02:07:51.731
<v RIGGS>Oh, aren’t they?

2513
02:07:51.731 --> 02:07:52.385
Aren’t they?

2514
02:07:52.385 --> 02:07:55.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I wish I had a pecan tree.

2515
02:07:55.000 --> 02:07:57.921
<v RIGGS>They had a lot of pecan trees out there.

2516
02:07:57.921 --> 02:07:59.219
There was a lot.

2517
02:07:59.219 --> 02:08:01.815
And I guess my grandfather had planted them.

2518
02:08:01.815 --> 02:08:04.086
We had a lot of pecan trees.

2519
02:08:04.086 --> 02:08:04.411
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

2520
02:08:04.411 --> 02:08:06.682
Are they still there on the property?

2521
02:08:06.682 --> 02:08:07.980
<v RIGGS>I'm sure they are.

2522
02:08:07.980 --> 02:08:11.225
<v ARSENEAULT>I guess you'll find out today when we go visit.

2523
02:08:11.225 --> 02:08:12.523
<v RIGGS>I'm sure they are.

2524
02:08:12.523 --> 02:08:15.119
<v ARSENEAULT>Go pick a few take them to Texas.

2525
02:08:15.119 --> 02:08:16.417
<v RIGGS>We’ll pick a few.

2526
02:08:16.417 --> 02:08:23.232
See our house was built where . . . Well not where his house was, but on the same property where his house was.

2527
02:08:23.232 --> 02:08:29.722
And it was like he had planted . . . because when I grew up there was still some there like plum trees,

2528
02:08:29.722 --> 02:08:31.344
or fig trees, pear trees.

2529
02:08:31.344 --> 02:08:32.642
And so I'm sure.

2530
02:08:32.642 --> 02:08:35.238
But I happen to know it's grown over.

2531
02:08:35.238 --> 02:08:38.483
So we hadn't been on there, but . . . And the pecans.

2532
02:08:38.483 --> 02:08:39.457
Had plenty pecan.

2533
02:08:39.457 --> 02:08:40.430
In fact my . . .

2534
02:08:40.430 --> 02:08:44.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have a favorite thing to eat growing up?

2535
02:08:44.000 --> 02:08:45.252
<v RIGGS>Probably the pecans.

2536
02:08:45.252 --> 02:08:51.093
My brothers . . . We used to get the pecans young and we would sell them.

2537
02:08:51.093 --> 02:08:58.186
We'd go out and get the pecans and we'd sit on the side of the road and

2538
02:08:58.186 --> 02:09:00.272
<c.p>people stop and buy them.

2539
02:09:00.272 --> 02:09:02.776
<v ARSENEAULT>How much would they fetch usually?

2540
02:09:02.776 --> 02:09:04.445
<v RIGGS>Probably not too much.

2541
02:09:04.445 --> 02:09:06.114
I don't even remember.

2542
02:09:06.114 --> 02:09:13.207
Probably less than a dollar in a little . . . And lot of them was in a little can.

2543
02:09:13.207 --> 02:09:18.214
A little thing that we would . . . They would measure them out in.

2544
02:09:18.214 --> 02:09:19.883
They would sell them.

2545
02:09:19.883 --> 02:09:21.134
We'd sell pecans.

2546
02:09:21.134 --> 02:09:26.976
And looked like it was a lot, but I know it wasn't a lot.

2547
02:09:26.976 --> 02:09:29.062
When we would sell pecans.

2548
02:09:29.062 --> 02:09:31.566
<v ARSENEAULT>Bet you thought you were rich.

2549
02:09:31.566 --> 02:09:36.990
<v RIGGS>[laughs] We would go to Uncle Ira's store and spend it right away.

2550
02:09:36.990 --> 02:09:38.241
<v ARSENEAULT>Buy some candies.

2551
02:09:38.241 --> 02:09:39.076
<v RIGGS>Selling pecans.

2552
02:09:39.076 --> 02:09:45.752
I think of Mossville and I think . . . I can't think that I was in the country.

2553
02:09:45.752 --> 02:09:52.428
I never felt like I was in the . . . People say I was raised in the country.

2554
02:09:52.428 --> 02:09:55.766
I know I was raised in the country,

2555
02:09:55.766 --> 02:10:04.945
but to me it wasn't raised . . . I wasn't raised where there was truck farms and there was cotton, and there was farming.

2556
02:10:04.945 --> 02:10:07.031
To me that's the country.

2557
02:10:07.031 --> 02:10:11.621
I never thought of my . . . I knew it was the country.

2558
02:10:11.621 --> 02:10:14.541
I never thought of us as country.

2559
02:10:14.541 --> 02:10:18.714
I guess I thought of us as a little town.

2560
02:10:18.714 --> 02:10:21.634
I don't know what other people think,

2561
02:10:21.634 --> 02:10:32.066
but I never thought of that as . . . I called the country if you lived down a dirt road and its two or three house there.

2562
02:10:32.066 --> 02:10:34.986
That would be the country to me.

2563
02:10:34.986 --> 02:10:42.079
But let’s see, in Mossville we were on the main road and it was houses all along.

2564
02:10:42.079 --> 02:10:43.748
So that's how I

2565
02:10:43.748 --> 02:10:45.000
<c.p>saw it.

2566
02:10:45.000 --> 02:10:47.343
<v ARSENEAULT>You said there was a sugar mill there?

2567
02:10:47.343 --> 02:10:49.100
<v RIGGS>I don't know where that was.

2568
02:10:49.100 --> 02:10:49.979
<v ARSENEAULT>At one point.

2569
02:10:49.979 --> 02:10:50.271
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

2570
02:10:50.271 --> 02:10:51.443
It was a place.

2571
02:10:51.443 --> 02:10:53.493
Yeah, I don't know where it was.

2572
02:10:53.493 --> 02:10:58.471
I think from . . . I think that it was up near that Methodist church off of Prater Road.

2573
02:10:58.471 --> 02:10:58.764
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

2574
02:10:58.764 --> 02:11:01.107
<v RIGGS>I think it was over in there somewhere.

2575
02:11:01.107 --> 02:11:03.157
But they would . . . Someone grew the sugarcane.

2576
02:11:03.157 --> 02:11:04.914
I don't know who it was.

2577
02:11:04.914 --> 02:11:07.550
And see, they grind it to make the syrup.

2578
02:11:07.550 --> 02:11:09.600
That's what I know they would do.

2579
02:11:09.600 --> 02:11:12.529
And it might have been just a group of people.

2580
02:11:12.529 --> 02:11:16.336
I don't know if it was a big thing or a little thing.

2581
02:11:16.336 --> 02:11:18.971
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it there like, when you were growing up?

2582
02:11:18.971 --> 02:11:19.264
<v RIGGS>[Disagrees].

2583
02:11:19.264 --> 02:11:20.143
I don't remember.

2584
02:11:20.143 --> 02:11:26.000
That's why when I was born . . . I don't ever remember seeing . . . That's why I don't know where it was.

2585
02:11:26.000 --> 02:11:29.613
<v ARSENEAULT>It's just they kind of talked about . . .

2586
02:11:29.613 --> 02:11:30.130
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

2587
02:11:30.130 --> 02:11:32.194
About it being there.

2588
02:11:32.194 --> 02:11:34.259
<v ARSENEAULT>What about any sawmills?

2589
02:11:34.259 --> 02:11:37.356
<v RIGGS>No. Not that I know of.

2590
02:11:37.356 --> 02:11:38.389
No sawmills.

2591
02:11:38.389 --> 02:11:39.421
<v ARSENEAULT>Other industries?

2592
02:11:39.421 --> 02:11:43.035
Do you remember the plants coming in?

2593
02:11:43.035 --> 02:11:46.648
<v RIGGS>No, I was trying to remember that.

2594
02:11:46.648 --> 02:11:52.326
I don't remember when . . . I don't remember the plants coming in.

2595
02:11:52.326 --> 02:12:03.167
I do remember . . . I know they had a Firestone plant, and it was on the other side of the new 90.

2596
02:12:03.167 --> 02:12:11.942
And a Cities Service plant, now I know they were there maybe the late 40s early 50s.

2597
02:12:11.942 --> 02:12:17.104
And the Conoco plant was there probably in the 40s.

2598
02:12:17.104 --> 02:12:21.234
And PPG, I don't know when they came.

2599
02:12:21.234 --> 02:12:28.977
That's the one that just you come up over the bridge is to the right.

2600
02:12:28.977 --> 02:12:32.590
I don't remember when that one came.

2601
02:12:32.590 --> 02:12:38.785
But most of them came when I was a teenager, or before.

2602
02:12:38.785 --> 02:12:41.366
I remember people working there.

2603
02:12:41.366 --> 02:12:43.947
My relatives and other people.

2604
02:12:43.947 --> 02:12:53.755
That's one of the things I think that the people in Mossville seem to have had a better life.

2605
02:12:53.755 --> 02:12:55.819
They had nice homes.

2606
02:12:55.819 --> 02:12:57.368
They had cars.

2607
02:12:57.368 --> 02:13:01.498
I used to see them around the corner.

2608
02:13:01.498 --> 02:13:03.046
And they worked.

2609
02:13:03.046 --> 02:13:06.144
They had some kind of job.

2610
02:13:06.144 --> 02:13:07.692
People had jobs.

2611
02:13:07.692 --> 02:13:16.468
I think the plant was instrumental in a lot of them having a better quality of life.

2612
02:13:16.468 --> 02:13:22.662
And people came to Lake Charles and worked and did different things.

2613
02:13:22.662 --> 02:13:26.792
Worked for people or worked at Sears, basically.

2614
02:13:26.792 --> 02:13:35.567
It probably was some poverty, but not bad, bad poverty and maybe you didn't see some stuff.

2615
02:13:35.567 --> 02:13:41.762
But I don't remember even going to school and children coming to

2616
02:13:41.762 --> 02:13:51.569
<c.p>school and somebody would say well, "They don't have clothes" or, "They didn't have food" or something like that.

2617
02:13:51.569 --> 02:13:55.183
I don't ever remember anything like that.

2618
02:13:55.183 --> 02:13:58.280
Or anybody being down and out.

2619
02:13:58.280 --> 02:14:02.410
And maybe they were, I just didn't know.

2620
02:14:02.410 --> 02:14:07.572
And if it was, it wasn't a lot of people.

2621
02:14:07.572 --> 02:14:18.412
Sit and think about it, a lot of people I can think about where they worked . . . some of the men really.

2622
02:14:18.412 --> 02:14:20.993
As I was growing up.

2623
02:14:20.993 --> 02:14:24.090
<v ARSENEAULT>What were some of the places?

2624
02:14:24.090 --> 02:14:24.606
<v RIGGS>Huh?

2625
02:14:24.606 --> 02:14:27.704
<v ARSENEAULT>What were some of the places?

2626
02:14:27.704 --> 02:14:36.479
<v RIGGS>A lot of them worked at . . . like at the Firestone plant and at the Cities Service plants.

2627
02:14:36.479 --> 02:14:42.157
My brothers worked the Cities Service . . . At the Cities Service plant.

2628
02:14:42.157 --> 02:14:45.255
At that PPG plant and Conoco.

2629
02:14:45.255 --> 02:14:53.514
There was a Dunham and Price cement plant on the new place, and people worked there.

2630
02:14:53.514 --> 02:15:01.773
There were people that came here and they worked for like the [Grays?] in Lake Charles.

2631
02:15:01.773 --> 02:15:05.387
The people that lived on Shell Beach.

2632
02:15:05.387 --> 02:15:09.000
They worked for some of them.

2633
02:15:09.000 --> 02:15:12.200
<v ARSENEAULT>The Gray's was that like the wealthy family?

2634
02:15:12.200 --> 02:15:12.600
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

2635
02:15:12.600 --> 02:15:14.200
Yeah and the Burden's.

2636
02:15:14.200 --> 02:15:22.600
As I was doing this and thinking, I was just thinking that most of the people I knew had a job.

2637
02:15:22.600 --> 02:15:23.400
All worked.

2638
02:15:23.400 --> 02:15:26.200
A lot of the women didn't work.

2639
02:15:26.200 --> 02:15:31.400
A lot of the women didn't work, but most of the men worked.

2640
02:15:31.400 --> 02:15:33.800
<v ARSENEAULT>What were like the women's duties?

2641
02:15:33.800 --> 02:15:39.000
<v RIGGS>I think . . . I guess they were housewives or stayed at home, or . . .

2642
02:15:39.000 --> 02:15:40.447
<v ARSENEAULT>Cooking and cleaning?

2643
02:15:40.447 --> 02:15:42.377
<v RIGGS>Had their children. [Agrees].

2644
02:15:42.377 --> 02:15:43.825
They didn’t work.

2645
02:15:43.825 --> 02:15:46.237
Most of them didn't work.

2646
02:15:46.237 --> 02:15:51.061
<v ARSENEAULT>It's kind of more recent, but we're talking about industry.

2647
02:15:51.061 --> 02:15:53.474
What about like the pollution?

2648
02:15:53.474 --> 02:15:58.298
What do you remember when Mossville water was . . . got polluted?

2649
02:15:58.298 --> 02:16:08.912
<v RIGGS>Well I guess I remember when it wasn't, but then I also remember when it was because the plants . . . It's been there.

2650
02:16:08.912 --> 02:16:18.561
It wasn't as many, but I guess it . . . When you stop [clears throat] pardon me, and you think about it,

2651
02:16:18.561 --> 02:16:28.693
and I think of the number of people that I know that is passed away that lived here and had cancer.

2652
02:16:28.693 --> 02:16:35.930
My mother had cancer, her sister had cancer, and they lived here until the 50s.

2653
02:16:35.930 --> 02:16:46.061
I'm not saying that’s . . . That they got it from there, but then I know other relatives and other people that died.

2654
02:16:46.061 --> 02:16:52.333
So I would say that it . . . I keep going back to the 50s,

2655
02:16:52.333 --> 02:17:02.947
but that's . . . I would say that was probably when it happened and it was probably happening all along with things being released.

2656
02:17:02.947 --> 02:17:07.772
Because sometimes you'd say, "Oh, the plant has released something."

2657
02:17:07.772 --> 02:17:11.632
You smell something, smell like a rotten egg.

2658
02:17:11.632 --> 02:17:18.868
That was . . . but you never . . . I never really thought of it then, or growing up.

2659
02:17:18.868 --> 02:17:21.763
I guess nobody else it either.

2660
02:17:21.763 --> 02:17:23.693
But I don't know.

2661
02:17:23.693 --> 02:17:26.588
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there ever any explosions, or?

2662
02:17:26.588 --> 02:17:27.070
<v RIGGS>No.

2663
02:17:27.070 --> 02:17:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Anything like that?

2664
02:17:29.000 --> 02:17:30.926
<v RIGGS>I don't remember any explosion.

2665
02:17:30.926 --> 02:17:37.473
Maybe some small fires at Conoco which is the one . . . You know where the Conoco Plant is?

2666
02:17:37.473 --> 02:17:39.014
I keep saying Conoco.

2667
02:17:39.014 --> 02:17:39.399
It's . . .

2668
02:17:39.399 --> 02:17:41.324
<v ARSENEAULT>I don’t . . . There’s so many.

2669
02:17:41.324 --> 02:17:42.865
They all look like . . .

2670
02:17:42.865 --> 02:17:43.250
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

2671
02:17:43.250 --> 02:17:46.331
It's coming out of Westlake to the left.

2672
02:17:46.331 --> 02:17:46.716
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

2673
02:17:46.716 --> 02:17:52.878
<v RIGGS>I remember some fires, but I don't remember any explosions, and it could have been some.

2674
02:17:52.878 --> 02:17:57.885
I don't remember any, but look like I would remember there being some.

2675
02:17:57.885 --> 02:18:00.196
Something happened at the Firestone plant.

2676
02:18:00.196 --> 02:18:01.351
It wasn't explosion.

2677
02:18:01.351 --> 02:18:04.818
I think it was something like . . . They made tires.

2678
02:18:04.818 --> 02:18:11.365
And I think it was something like a vat and . . . What do you make tires out of?

2679
02:18:11.365 --> 02:18:11.750
Rubber?

2680
02:18:11.750 --> 02:18:12.520
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, yeah.

2681
02:18:12.520 --> 02:18:18.682
<v RIGGS>The rubber came out and maybe some people got burnt, but it wasn't . . . I remember that.

2682
02:18:18.682 --> 02:18:24.845
But it wasn't no big thing I don't think, or no people getting killed or anything.

2683
02:18:24.845 --> 02:18:29.466
And it may have been an explosion, but I don't remember one.

2684
02:18:29.466 --> 02:18:31.007
<v ARSENEAULT>Just wanted to ask.

2685
02:18:31.007 --> 02:18:34.473
Just . . . I know there's so many plants right there.

2686
02:18:34.473 --> 02:18:38.324
I’m just . . . Wonder what it was like living so close.

2687
02:18:38.324 --> 02:18:41.405
<v RIGGS>I don't think people ever thought about that.

2688
02:18:41.405 --> 02:18:46.797
I mean never even thought of . . . There were some people who lived very close.

2689
02:18:46.797 --> 02:18:48.338
Closer than other people.

2690
02:18:48.338 --> 02:18:51.419
I don't really think they thought of it.

2691
02:18:51.419 --> 02:18:54.500
I guess it was a way of life.

2692
02:18:54.500 --> 02:19:01.047
It may have been that this was your livelihood, so you didn't think it would hurt you.

2693
02:19:01.047 --> 02:19:03.743
My daddy never worked at the plant.

2694
02:19:03.743 --> 02:19:06.824
Like I said, my daddy became a carpenter.

2695
02:19:06.824 --> 02:19:08.750
He built houses and stuff.

2696
02:19:08.750 --> 02:19:10.291
But he always worked.

2697
02:19:10.291 --> 02:19:12.986
But he never went to the plant.

2698
02:19:12.986 --> 02:19:16.838
I don't know why he never went to the plant.

2699
02:19:16.838 --> 02:19:19.534
But he never worked at the plant.

2700
02:19:19.534 --> 02:19:20.689
Any of them.

2701
02:19:20.689 --> 02:19:23.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he build y'alls house?

2702
02:19:23.000 --> 02:19:23.305
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

2703
02:19:23.305 --> 02:19:24.527
He built our house.

2704
02:19:24.527 --> 02:19:25.749
<v ARSENEAULT>That's kind of nice.

2705
02:19:25.749 --> 02:19:27.581
Something breaks he can fix it.

2706
02:19:27.581 --> 02:19:27.886
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

2707
02:19:27.886 --> 02:19:34.299
It was like my mother and daddy got a divorce, and I have him laughing, and he moved to Lake Charles.

2708
02:19:34.299 --> 02:19:38.575
I had him laughing I said . . . he had . . . His house was a nice house.

2709
02:19:38.575 --> 02:19:40.407
He had a very nice house.

2710
02:19:40.407 --> 02:19:44.072
I said, "My daddy's house was like the ‘House of [Seven] Gables’."

2711
02:19:44.072 --> 02:19:49.263
He had a . . . I mean it was a big house, and they had a lot of rooms.

2712
02:19:49.263 --> 02:19:53.234
The rooms was big, and it went all the way back the lot.

2713
02:19:53.234 --> 02:19:55.066
The garage was on the back.

2714
02:19:55.066 --> 02:19:57.814
I said it was like going to a hotel.

2715
02:19:57.814 --> 02:20:01.784
You drive up the front and go all the way to the back.

2716
02:20:01.784 --> 02:20:03.617
But it was a nice house.

2717
02:20:03.617 --> 02:20:04.533
But I think

2718
02:20:04.533 --> 02:20:10.030
<c.p>. . . When I think about it later being adult he probably built the house and then he added on.

2719
02:20:10.030 --> 02:20:14.000
I guess as he could afford it he added onto that house.

2720
02:20:14.000 --> 02:20:16.057
<v ARSENEAULT>Because he could because he knew . . .

2721
02:20:16.057 --> 02:20:19.486
<v RIGGS>That house went down with the . . . What's the last hurricane?

2722
02:20:19.486 --> 02:20:20.171
<v ARSENEAULT>Not Rita?

2723
02:20:20.171 --> 02:20:22.229
<v RIGGS>Katrina . . . Not . . . Katrina that hit here?

2724
02:20:22.229 --> 02:20:23.600
Whatever was hit here.

2725
02:20:23.600 --> 02:20:25.657
They had to tear it down.

2726
02:20:25.657 --> 02:20:26.686
It went then.

2727
02:20:26.686 --> 02:20:29.771
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember any hurricanes in Mossville growing up?

2728
02:20:29.771 --> 02:20:30.114
<v RIGGS>Yes.

2729
02:20:30.114 --> 02:20:31.486
I remember Hurricane Audrey.

2730
02:20:31.486 --> 02:20:34.229
Not . . . Well Hurricane Audrey tore up Cameron [Louisiana].

2731
02:20:34.229 --> 02:20:34.914
<v ARSENEAULT>So close.

2732
02:20:34.914 --> 02:20:36.286
<v RIGGS>And I remember that.

2733
02:20:36.286 --> 02:20:36.629
Huh?

2734
02:20:36.629 --> 02:20:38.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That's so close.

2735
02:20:38.000 --> 02:20:38.368
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

2736
02:20:38.368 --> 02:20:41.311
It did Cameron and we had to evacuate.

2737
02:20:41.311 --> 02:20:43.150
That school was built then.

2738
02:20:43.150 --> 02:20:44.989
We went to the school.

2739
02:20:44.989 --> 02:20:46.829
Others we didn't really leave.

2740
02:20:46.829 --> 02:20:49.036
And nothing ever happened in Mossville.

2741
02:20:49.036 --> 02:20:56.761
We never had any . . . Me growing up, we never had anything bad or anything happen to our house during a hurricane.

2742
02:20:56.761 --> 02:21:04.118
I actually started remembering hurricanes after . . . maybe it was because they didn't have the tracking stuff and you didn't know.

2743
02:21:04.118 --> 02:21:11.107
You just knew a bad storm was coming, and you didn't know that it was a hurricane or what.

2744
02:21:11.107 --> 02:21:14.786
They hadn't named them I don't think during that time.

2745
02:21:14.786 --> 02:21:20.671
You just knew the weather was bad and then it did this and it had tornados.

2746
02:21:20.671 --> 02:21:30.236
So I don't . . . The first hurricane . . . When I started really knowing about hurricanes and stuff was in the 50s with Audrey and when those hurricanes hit.

2747
02:21:30.236 --> 02:21:31.707
<v ARSENEAULT>What was that like?

2748
02:21:31.707 --> 02:21:33.546
Everyone meeting at the school?

2749
02:21:33.546 --> 02:21:37.225
<v RIGGS>It was chaotic for a while, but people met there.

2750
02:21:37.225 --> 02:21:40.168
We spent the night because it started flooding.

2751
02:21:40.168 --> 02:21:44.582
But the Red Cross had set it up so it wasn't bad.

2752
02:21:44.582 --> 02:21:49.364
So they had people like in the gym and in the lunch room.

2753
02:21:49.364 --> 02:21:52.307
And they gave . . . Families had like little classrooms.

2754
02:21:52.307 --> 02:21:56.721
If you had a big family put you in a little classroom.

2755
02:21:56.721 --> 02:21:58.193
But it wasn't bad.

2756
02:21:58.193 --> 02:21:58.929
We [laughed?].

2757
02:21:58.929 --> 02:21:59.664
We ate.

2758
02:21:59.664 --> 02:22:00.400
They cooked.

2759
02:22:00.400 --> 02:22:07.021
They had the food and stuff there, and I think we went back the next morning or something.

2760
02:22:07.021 --> 02:22:08.125
Jeffery was small.

2761
02:22:08.125 --> 02:22:09.596
He was a baby.

2762
02:22:09.596 --> 02:22:11.436
<v ARSENEAULT>Were you living at Houston?

2763
02:22:11.436 --> 02:22:12.539
<v RIGGS>I was here.

2764
02:22:12.539 --> 02:22:14.746
With that one I was here.

2765
02:22:14.746 --> 02:22:15.850
<v ARSENEAULT>When did you . . .?

2766
02:22:15.850 --> 02:22:17.689
<v RIGGS>When'd I go to Houston?

2767
02:22:17.689 --> 02:22:18.057
<v ARSENEAULT>[Agrees].

2768
02:22:18.057 --> 02:22:20.264
<v RIGGS>I went to Houston in '65.

2769
02:22:20.264 --> 02:22:21.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

2770
02:22:21.000 --> 02:22:25.299
<v RIGGS>But that hurricane . . . he was . . . Yeah he was a baby.

2771
02:22:25.299 --> 02:22:28.308
He was little when that hurricane hit.

2772
02:22:28.308 --> 02:22:37.335
But we went to that, and that was the only time I've ever evacuated for . . . And that was because of flooding.

2773
02:22:37.335 --> 02:22:39.054
<v ARSENEAULT>There's so many questions.

2774
02:22:39.054 --> 02:22:40.774
What about medical treatment?

2775
02:22:40.774 --> 02:22:42.923
<v RIGGS>Did we have medical treatment?

2776
02:22:42.923 --> 02:22:45.502
<v ARSENEAULT>Where'd y'all go to the doctor?

2777
02:22:45.502 --> 02:22:48.511
<v RIGGS>We went to the doctor in Sulphur.

2778
02:22:48.511 --> 02:22:51.950
Yeah, we went to the doctors in Sulphur.

2779
02:22:51.950 --> 02:22:57.109
Then growing up I don't think we ever went to Doctor Ross.

2780
02:22:57.109 --> 02:22:58.828
Was a black doctor.

2781
02:22:58.828 --> 02:23:02.697
Yeah, I don't think we ever went . . . I didn't.

2782
02:23:02.697 --> 02:23:05.276
But we went to Doctor [Seals?].

2783
02:23:05.276 --> 02:23:07.855
I remember Doctor Seals in Sulphur.

2784
02:23:07.855 --> 02:23:09.575
<c.p>And hospital, St. Patty’s.

2785
02:23:09.575 --> 02:23:11.724
<v ARSENEAULT>So you mentioned a midwife.

2786
02:23:11.724 --> 02:23:13.873
Aunt . . . What was her name?

2787
02:23:13.873 --> 02:23:14.303
<v RIGGS>[Jannie?].

2788
02:23:14.303 --> 02:23:14.733
<v ARSENEAULT>Jannie?

2789
02:23:14.733 --> 02:23:20.321
<v RIGGS>I don't know nothing about Aunt Jannie, but Aunt Jannie was the midwife.

2790
02:23:20.321 --> 02:23:29.778
And well I guess in Mossville area I don't know where else she went, but she probably delivered a lot of us.

2791
02:23:29.778 --> 02:23:33.217
I don't know if she delivered my brothers.

2792
02:23:33.217 --> 02:23:36.656
I don't know why I don't know that.

2793
02:23:36.656 --> 02:23:37.946
I don't know.

2794
02:23:37.946 --> 02:23:39.235
I don't know.

2795
02:23:39.235 --> 02:23:40.955
I'm going to look.

2796
02:23:40.955 --> 02:23:43.964
I think I have their birth certificates.

2797
02:23:43.964 --> 02:23:47.403
I'm going to look because she signed them.

2798
02:23:47.403 --> 02:23:49.552
She signed the birth certificate.

2799
02:23:49.552 --> 02:23:52.131
<v ARSENEAULT>Were you born in the home?

2800
02:23:52.131 --> 02:23:52.991
<v RIGGS>At home.

2801
02:23:52.991 --> 02:23:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Everyone was born in the home?

2802
02:23:56.000 --> 02:23:56.270
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

2803
02:23:56.270 --> 02:23:58.162
<v ARSENEAULT>Did your mom ever talk about it?

2804
02:23:58.162 --> 02:23:58.432
<v RIGGS>[Disagrees]

2805
02:23:58.432 --> 02:24:00.595
<v ARSENEAULT>I guess they don't talk about those things.

2806
02:24:00.595 --> 02:24:01.676
<v RIGGS>No. No, she didn’t.

2807
02:24:01.676 --> 02:24:06.000
No, except the part I told you about the boys going and stopping at the . . .

2808
02:24:06.000 --> 02:24:06.883
<v ARSENEAULT>Sugarcane mill.

2809
02:24:06.883 --> 02:24:09.089
<v RIGGS>At the sugarcane thing [agrees].

2810
02:24:09.089 --> 02:24:10.855
So no, not really.

2811
02:24:10.855 --> 02:24:12.620
She didn't really talk.

2812
02:24:12.620 --> 02:24:21.006
I mean she just didn't . . . And maybe they didn't talk about stuff like that when I was growing up.

2813
02:24:21.006 --> 02:24:22.771
Maybe we did talk.

2814
02:24:22.771 --> 02:24:28.067
It was like . . . we were not . . . when I talked to other people,

2815
02:24:28.067 --> 02:24:34.687
and with the rapport they had with their mother, or father, or stuff . . . we talked.

2816
02:24:34.687 --> 02:24:36.011
We did talk.

2817
02:24:36.011 --> 02:24:39.983
We did the girls talk and the . . . We talked.

2818
02:24:39.983 --> 02:24:42.631
Not just that, but we talked.

2819
02:24:42.631 --> 02:24:47.045
But that was where we did . . . That was the doctor.

2820
02:24:47.045 --> 02:24:54.989
I guess until they got old and I left and they probably went to someone else . . . Doctor Sears.

2821
02:24:54.989 --> 02:24:55.872
Nothing big.

2822
02:24:55.872 --> 02:25:00.726
Nobody . . . I think my daddy had surgery when I was young.

2823
02:25:00.726 --> 02:25:03.816
He might of had appendix or something,

2824
02:25:03.816 --> 02:25:10.877
but nobody really . . . I don't think my mother went to a . . . growing up, to the hospital.

2825
02:25:10.877 --> 02:25:15.291
She went to the doctor, but not to the hospital.

2826
02:25:15.291 --> 02:25:17.056
I don't remember that.

2827
02:25:17.056 --> 02:25:21.911
I remember Daddy going and having that surgery at St. Patty.

2828
02:25:21.911 --> 02:25:23.235
<v ARSENEAULT>Any home remedies?

2829
02:25:23.235 --> 02:25:25.000
Anything like that?

2830
02:25:25.000 --> 02:25:25.917
<v RIGGS>Not really.

2831
02:25:25.917 --> 02:25:30.044
<v ARSENEAULT>What'd y'all do for like a stomach ache, or?

2832
02:25:30.044 --> 02:25:31.878
<v RIGGS>I don't even remember.

2833
02:25:31.878 --> 02:25:43.801
Probably had some . . . I know once a year we used to . . . In the winter time she would like . . . We would take castor oil and something else.

2834
02:25:43.801 --> 02:25:45.635
Everybody talks about that.

2835
02:25:45.635 --> 02:25:49.762
I guess that'd get you ready for the colds.

2836
02:25:49.762 --> 02:25:58.017
But I guess basically whatever you were given because see, when I came you had drugstores in stores.

2837
02:25:58.017 --> 02:25:59.392
I guess aspirins.

2838
02:25:59.392 --> 02:26:09.939
I know they had Alka-Seltzer when I was growing up because I remember I put one in m y mouth and almost choked.

2839
02:26:09.939 --> 02:26:11.315
I remember that.

2840
02:26:11.315 --> 02:26:21.403
So I guess for stomach ache they took Alka-Seltzer’s, but as far as I guess just regular stuff that you would do . . .

2841
02:26:21.403 --> 02:26:23.238
<v ARSENEAULT>For a headache anything?

2842
02:26:23.238 --> 02:26:25.530
<v RIGGS>Yeah, you'd probably take aspirins.

2843
02:26:25.530 --> 02:26:28.740
That's the only thing I remember doing.

2844
02:26:28.740 --> 02:26:29.657
<v ARSENEAULT>Mosquito bites?

2845
02:26:29.657 --> 02:26:30.116
<v RIGGS>Huh?

2846
02:26:30.116 --> 02:26:31.033
Mosquito bites?

2847
02:26:31.033 --> 02:26:31.950
<v ARSENEAULT>Mosquito bites.

2848
02:26:31.950 --> 02:26:33.785
<v RIGGS>I don't remember that.

2849
02:26:33.785 --> 02:26:38.370
I know for a wasp bite they would put tobacco.

2850
02:26:38.370 --> 02:26:42.956
Wet tobacco and put on a wasp . . . On a sting.

2851
02:26:42.956 --> 02:26:44.790
They would do that.

2852
02:26:44.790 --> 02:26:48.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was that supposed to do?

2853
02:26:48.000 --> 02:26:50.988
<v RIGGS>It would stop it from swelling or something.

2854
02:26:50.988 --> 02:26:53.229
I don't know what it did.

2855
02:26:53.229 --> 02:26:57.711
But they would put tobacco on a wasp sting, or whatever stings.

2856
02:26:57.711 --> 02:26:59.578
They would something like that.

2857
02:26:59.578 --> 02:27:02.940
Now I have heard . . . I don't remember doing it,

2858
02:27:02.940 --> 02:27:10.036
but I have heard that people said like if you cut yourself or you're bleeding, you put spider web.

2859
02:27:10.036 --> 02:27:12.277
That was something that people did.

2860
02:27:12.277 --> 02:27:14.145
I don't remember doing that.

2861
02:27:14.145 --> 02:27:18.253
I don't remember my mama putting a spider web on it.

2862
02:27:18.253 --> 02:27:21.988
But I'm trying to think of any other remedies that . . .

2863
02:27:21.988 --> 02:27:22.361
<v ARSENEAULT>Fever?

2864
02:27:22.361 --> 02:27:25.349
Did y'all do anything special for a fever?

2865
02:27:25.349 --> 02:27:26.843
<v RIGGS>I don't remember that.

2866
02:27:26.843 --> 02:27:31.325
With a cold we had cough syrup and stuff like that, so.

2867
02:27:31.325 --> 02:27:36.928
I know they would sometimes make a tea if you had the flu or something.

2868
02:27:36.928 --> 02:27:40.663
You might make a tea and give you a tea.

2869
02:27:40.663 --> 02:27:41.783
But not many.

2870
02:27:41.783 --> 02:27:44.024
She didn't do many remedies, no.

2871
02:27:44.024 --> 02:27:46.265
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of tea was it?

2872
02:27:46.265 --> 02:27:52.614
<v RIGGS>I don't know if it was a tea bag or it might have been a lemon tea.

2873
02:27:52.614 --> 02:27:55.602
Maybe lemon and honey or something like that.

2874
02:27:55.602 --> 02:27:57.470
Rather than a tea bag.

2875
02:27:57.470 --> 02:28:00.831
I know she had tea because we had tea.

2876
02:28:00.831 --> 02:28:06.060
Like when you were sick and coughing it probably was a lemon and honey.

2877
02:28:06.060 --> 02:28:07.181
Something like that.

2878
02:28:07.181 --> 02:28:11.663
<v ARSENEAULT>Did . . . Were there any like, treaters in the area that y'all would . . .?

2879
02:28:11.663 --> 02:28:12.036
<v RIGGS>Traders.

2880
02:28:12.036 --> 02:28:12.410
<v ARSENEAULT>Traders?

2881
02:28:12.410 --> 02:28:15.024
<v RIGGS>People that came along and sold stuff.

2882
02:28:15.024 --> 02:28:16.145
That's the trader.

2883
02:28:16.145 --> 02:28:17.639
What is a trader?

2884
02:28:17.639 --> 02:28:21.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Or like someone who does . . . Is a healer.

2885
02:28:21.000 --> 02:28:23.585
<v RIGGS>Oh no. No we didn't do that.

2886
02:28:23.585 --> 02:28:24.323
The trader?

2887
02:28:24.323 --> 02:28:26.538
I never heard that word before.

2888
02:28:26.538 --> 02:28:27.277
<v ARSENEAULT>Or treater?

2889
02:28:27.277 --> 02:28:29.862
Or [traitoire?] I think in French it’s . . .

2890
02:28:29.862 --> 02:28:30.600
<v RIGGS>I know.

2891
02:28:30.600 --> 02:28:34.662
Yeah, no. No, no, no. They might have prayed for you.

2892
02:28:34.662 --> 02:28:36.508
No, but not the healer.

2893
02:28:36.508 --> 02:28:37.985
I've heard of that.

2894
02:28:37.985 --> 02:28:38.723
The healer.

2895
02:28:38.723 --> 02:28:39.092
Yeah.

2896
02:28:39.092 --> 02:28:40.200
<v ARSENEAULT>So no one . . .?

2897
02:28:40.200 --> 02:28:42.046
<v RIGGS>No. We didn't do that.

2898
02:28:42.046 --> 02:28:43.154
<v ARSENEAULT>Any root teas . . .

2899
02:28:43.154 --> 02:28:43.523
<v RIGGS>No.

2900
02:28:43.523 --> 02:28:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . Or anything like that?

2901
02:28:45.000 --> 02:28:46.477
Just kind of standard.

2902
02:28:46.477 --> 02:28:47.954
<v RIGGS>[Agrees] No root tea.

2903
02:28:47.954 --> 02:28:48.692
No mamou.

2904
02:28:48.692 --> 02:28:49.800
No root tea.

2905
02:28:49.800 --> 02:28:51.277
We didn’t do that.

2906
02:28:51.277 --> 02:28:54.969
I've heard the mamou tea and the stuff like that.

2907
02:28:54.969 --> 02:28:56.815
We didn't do mamou tea.

2908
02:28:56.815 --> 02:29:00.138
<v ARSENEAULT>Did they . . . Did anyone sell it in the community?

2909
02:29:00.138 --> 02:29:01.985
Was there anyone that was . . .?

2910
02:29:01.985 --> 02:29:03.831
<v RIGGS>Not that I knew of.

2911
02:29:03.831 --> 02:29:10.846
But I've heard people talk about that for different ailments and for different . . . That they did the mamou tea.

2912
02:29:10.846 --> 02:29:11.954
They did mamou.

2913
02:29:11.954 --> 02:29:17.492
In fact, there were some people that go to my church and they're from Haiti.

2914
02:29:17.492 --> 02:29:22.662
And we had a potluck and this lady made tea and it was mamou.

2915
02:29:22.662 --> 02:29:25.985
I tasted it and I said, "What is this?"

2916
02:29:25.985 --> 02:29:30.785
And she went to tell me what it was and it was mamou.

2917
02:29:30.785 --> 02:29:33.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What does it taste like?

2918
02:29:33.000 --> 02:29:36.209
<v RIGGS>Kind of like a . . . maybe like a ginger tea.

2919
02:29:36.209 --> 02:29:38.705
You ever had a tea with ginger?

2920
02:29:38.705 --> 02:29:39.419
<v ARSENEAULT>It’s strong.

2921
02:29:39.419 --> 02:29:41.202
<v RIGGS>It has a little sweetness.

2922
02:29:41.202 --> 02:29:45.481
And I think that my step-mother was Creole and she did mamou.

2923
02:29:45.481 --> 02:29:48.333
And that's how I knew how it tastes.

2924
02:29:48.333 --> 02:29:49.760
But it was good.

2925
02:29:49.760 --> 02:29:52.969
I mean it was like any tea that you

2926
02:29:52.969 --> 02:29:57.605
<c.p>would drink, but it was made out of the mamou root she said.

2927
02:29:57.605 --> 02:29:58.674
Never had that.

2928
02:29:58.674 --> 02:30:00.814
Even here I’d never had it.

2929
02:30:00.814 --> 02:30:02.597
She said, "You're from Louisiana?"

2930
02:30:02.597 --> 02:30:05.093
I said, "I never had mamou tea."

2931
02:30:05.093 --> 02:30:06.876
<v ARSENEAULT>I haven't had it either.

2932
02:30:06.876 --> 02:30:09.016
I've been wanting to try it.

2933
02:30:09.016 --> 02:30:09.729
Let's see.

2934
02:30:09.729 --> 02:30:11.155
We talked about that.

2935
02:30:11.155 --> 02:30:12.938
<v RIGGS>Because it's a root, right?

2936
02:30:12.938 --> 02:30:14.364
Mamou is a root.

2937
02:30:14.364 --> 02:30:14.721
Yeah.

2938
02:30:14.721 --> 02:30:15.434
<v ARSENEAULT>That and . . .

2939
02:30:15.434 --> 02:30:19.000
<v RIGGS>You get it off of a tree don't you?

2940
02:30:19.000 --> 02:30:20.419
<v ARSENEAULT>That's what it sounds like.

2941
02:30:20.419 --> 02:30:20.703
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

2942
02:30:20.703 --> 02:30:21.270
<v ARSENEAULT>And sassafras.

2943
02:30:21.270 --> 02:30:21.838
<v RIGGS>Sassafras, yeah.

2944
02:30:21.838 --> 02:30:22.973
<v ARSENEAULT>But that's the filé.

2945
02:30:22.973 --> 02:30:24.108
That's what you make.

2946
02:30:24.108 --> 02:30:25.811
<v RIGGS>Yeah, sassafras is a file [agrees].

2947
02:30:25.811 --> 02:30:26.095
<v ARSENEAULT>Filé.

2948
02:30:26.095 --> 02:30:28.081
That's like . . . I think that's Creole cooking.

2949
02:30:28.081 --> 02:30:28.365
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

2950
02:30:28.365 --> 02:30:28.932
<v ARSENEAULT>Or Cajun.

2951
02:30:28.932 --> 02:30:29.784
I'm not sure.

2952
02:30:29.784 --> 02:30:31.770
<v RIGGS>Yeah I use filé in the gumbo.

2953
02:30:31.770 --> 02:30:34.041
That's the only thing I use it in.

2954
02:30:34.041 --> 02:30:36.595
I don't know what else you put it in.

2955
02:30:36.595 --> 02:30:40.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I know it’s . . . It thickens it up and it tastes good.

2956
02:30:40.000 --> 02:30:41.694
<v RIGGS>[And it too much?]

2957
02:30:41.694 --> 02:30:48.468
<v ARSENEAULT>I like file, but I wouldn't . . . I couldn't imagine drinking a tea out of it though.

2958
02:30:48.468 --> 02:30:50.586
I guess some people do.

2959
02:30:50.586 --> 02:30:51.009
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

2960
02:30:51.009 --> 02:30:51.432
Yeah.

2961
02:30:51.432 --> 02:30:52.279
<v ARSENEAULT>Drink it.

2962
02:30:52.279 --> 02:30:53.126
<v RIGGS>Maybe so.

2963
02:30:53.126 --> 02:30:54.396
<v ARSENEAULT>The sassafras tea.

2964
02:30:54.396 --> 02:30:57.360
What did I want to ask you?

2965
02:30:57.360 --> 02:30:59.477
Where did your family go

2966
02:30:59.477 --> 02:31:00.324
<c.p>to church?

2967
02:31:00.324 --> 02:31:03.712
I think you may have already said that.

2968
02:31:03.712 --> 02:31:07.099
<v RIGGS>We went to the St. Paul United Methodist.

2969
02:31:07.099 --> 02:31:08.369
The Methodist church.

2970
02:31:08.369 --> 02:31:11.333
My mother . . . My family and the B.C.

2971
02:31:11.333 --> 02:31:15.144
Junior part of my family went to Mount Zion.

2972
02:31:15.144 --> 02:31:19.802
My mother's family is buried at the cemetery at Mount Zion.

2973
02:31:19.802 --> 02:31:24.883
My daddy's family is buried at the cemetery at the Sanctified Church.

2974
02:31:24.883 --> 02:31:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where is that cemetery?

2975
02:31:27.000 --> 02:31:29.027
<v RIGGS>It's behind the church it’s . . .

2976
02:31:29.027 --> 02:31:29.838
<v ARSENEAULT>In Mossville?

2977
02:31:29.838 --> 02:31:30.243
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

2978
02:31:30.243 --> 02:31:32.270
The church is not there.

2979
02:31:32.270 --> 02:31:33.081
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

2980
02:31:33.081 --> 02:31:36.324
<v RIGGS>That's the cemetery they’re cleaning today I think.

2981
02:31:36.324 --> 02:31:36.730
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

2982
02:31:36.730 --> 02:31:37.946
<v RIGGS>That's the cemetery.

2983
02:31:37.946 --> 02:31:39.973
It’s back in the back.

2984
02:31:39.973 --> 02:31:42.000
<v ARSENEAULT>By the railroad tracks?

2985
02:31:42.000 --> 02:31:42.275
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

2986
02:31:42.275 --> 02:31:43.101
<v ARSENEAULT>Across the road.

2987
02:31:43.101 --> 02:31:43.376
<v RIGGS>Before.

2988
02:31:43.376 --> 02:31:45.303
Before you get to the railroad track.

2989
02:31:45.303 --> 02:31:47.229
<v ARSENEAULT>And then you go over the brook.

2990
02:31:47.229 --> 02:31:47.505
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

2991
02:31:47.505 --> 02:31:49.156
You go over the brook, yeah.

2992
02:31:49.156 --> 02:31:50.532
<v ARSENEAULT>And it's in the back?

2993
02:31:50.532 --> 02:31:50.807
<v RIGGS>[Agrees]

2994
02:31:50.807 --> 02:31:52.459
<v ARSENEAULT>And it's kind of grown over?

2995
02:31:52.459 --> 02:31:52.734
<v RIGGS>[Agrees]

2996
02:31:52.734 --> 02:31:54.385
<v ARSENEAULT>I know what you’re talking about.

2997
02:31:54.385 --> 02:31:57.413
<v RIGGS>My granddaughter said that we left my daddy in the woods.

2998
02:31:57.413 --> 02:31:58.239
She was little.

2999
02:31:58.239 --> 02:31:58.514
She

3000
02:31:58.514 --> 02:31:59.339
<c.p>was very small.

3001
02:31:59.339 --> 02:32:00.165
That bothered her.

3002
02:32:00.165 --> 02:32:02.367
She said, "We left him in the woods."

3003
02:32:02.367 --> 02:32:03.468
<v ARSENEAULT>It is like that.

3004
02:32:03.468 --> 02:32:05.119
<v RIGGS>Yeah it was in the woods.

3005
02:32:05.119 --> 02:32:05.945
It was woods.

3006
02:32:05.945 --> 02:32:06.495
<v ARSENEAULT>She’s right.

3007
02:32:06.495 --> 02:32:07.872
<v RIGGS>It was in the woods.

3008
02:32:07.872 --> 02:32:08.972
That's the cemetery, yeah.

3009
02:32:08.972 --> 02:32:11.174
<v ARSENEAULT>Who were some of the like, church leaders?

3010
02:32:11.174 --> 02:32:12.000
At the . . .?

3011
02:32:12.000 --> 02:32:14.761
<v RIGGS>At my church was Carl Rigmaiden.

3012
02:32:14.761 --> 02:32:17.521
Actually ended up with that church.

3013
02:32:17.521 --> 02:32:21.662
When he passed away he was the pastor there.

3014
02:32:21.662 --> 02:32:22.582
Ira Garrett.

3015
02:32:22.582 --> 02:32:26.263
We didn't have . . . Methodists don't have many people.

3016
02:32:26.263 --> 02:32:28.103
Who else was there?

3017
02:32:28.103 --> 02:32:29.944
I guess Carl Junior.

3018
02:32:29.944 --> 02:32:31.784
Long time ago though.

3019
02:32:31.784 --> 02:32:33.624
You taking me back.

3020
02:32:33.624 --> 02:32:35.005
Jack Moss maybe.

3021
02:32:35.005 --> 02:32:36.845
Yeah, Jack was there.

3022
02:32:36.845 --> 02:32:38.225
James Rigmaiden. [Carrie?].

3023
02:32:38.225 --> 02:32:39.606
Carrie was young.

3024
02:32:39.606 --> 02:32:40.066
Carrie.

3025
02:32:40.066 --> 02:32:43.286
The Hartman's I'm trying to figure out.

3026
02:32:43.286 --> 02:32:45.587
I can't remember the names.

3027
02:32:45.587 --> 02:32:49.268
Mr. Hartman’s family is just coming to me.

3028
02:32:49.268 --> 02:32:52.488
I can't remember which one it was.

3029
02:32:52.488 --> 02:32:54.789
<v ARSENEAULT>You said y'all rotated churches?

3030
02:32:54.789 --> 02:33:00.770
<v RIGGS>See the reason you rotated churches was because you didn't have a minister.

3031
02:33:00.770 --> 02:33:03.991
The minister came maybe twice a month.

3032
02:33:03.991 --> 02:33:09.972
So if he came to you on the first Sunday he didn't come

3033
02:33:09.972 --> 02:33:12.272
<c.p>again until the third Sunday.

3034
02:33:12.272 --> 02:33:22.394
So on the second Sunday if the Baptist church had a preacher that had a eleven o’ . . . didn't have a one service,

3035
02:33:22.394 --> 02:33:26.535
that had service people would go to that church.

3036
02:33:26.535 --> 02:33:30.676
Then the next Sunday you'd go to another church.

3037
02:33:30.676 --> 02:33:32.977
Now everybody didn't do that.

3038
02:33:32.977 --> 02:33:34.357
Some people did.

3039
02:33:34.357 --> 02:33:37.117
And you would rotate like that.

3040
02:33:37.117 --> 02:33:43.099
I mean you didn't belong to the church, you just visited that day.

3041
02:33:43.099 --> 02:33:43.559
<v ARSENEAULT>Interesting.

3042
02:33:43.559 --> 02:33:45.859
I've never heard of anything . . .

3043
02:33:45.859 --> 02:33:46.319
<v RIGGS>Really?

3044
02:33:46.319 --> 02:33:47.239
Like that?

3045
02:33:47.239 --> 02:33:48.620
<v ARSENEAULT>Similar to that.

3046
02:33:48.620 --> 02:33:50.000
That’s fascinating.

3047
02:33:50.000 --> 02:33:50.408
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

3048
02:33:50.408 --> 02:33:56.115
<v ARSENEAULT>Did your . . . Did the minister have like a role in like civic affairs, or?

3049
02:33:56.115 --> 02:34:00.192
<v RIGGS>The ministers at my church did not live in Mossville.

3050
02:34:00.192 --> 02:34:06.715
They lived in . . . Well some of them lived in Lake Charles and some lived other places.

3051
02:34:06.715 --> 02:34:12.015
And what they did on those odd Sundays, they went to other cities.

3052
02:34:12.015 --> 02:34:15.685
You see, they had maybe three or four churches.

3053
02:34:15.685 --> 02:34:24.654
So, and if it was something going on in the community and the church was involved, normally he would attend or come.

3054
02:34:24.654 --> 02:34:32.400
For instance if someone at the Baptist church died and we had some people that go to our church,

3055
02:34:32.400 --> 02:34:41.369
that relative was at that Baptist church, our minister might come to the funeral because of someone . . . You know what I'm saying?

3056
02:34:41.369 --> 02:34:43.408
They would do like that.

3057
02:34:43.408 --> 02:34:44.631
But basically they

3058
02:34:44.631 --> 02:34:47.077
<c.p>didn't live . . . Carl lived in Mossville.

3059
02:34:47.077 --> 02:34:49.115
He was a Mossville man.

3060
02:34:49.115 --> 02:34:50.338
He lived there.

3061
02:34:50.338 --> 02:34:55.638
And you had people that was ministers like Josh Rigmaiden had a church.

3062
02:34:55.638 --> 02:34:59.715
But his church was over here in Brownsville right here.

3063
02:34:59.715 --> 02:35:02.977
Jake Rigmaiden had a church in Texas someplace.

3064
02:35:02.977 --> 02:35:05.831
And I'm just trying . . . Thinking of ministers.

3065
02:35:05.831 --> 02:35:10.315
Berkley Garrett Junior, my uncle, had a church in Houston River.

3066
02:35:10.315 --> 02:35:11.946
Now he was steady.

3067
02:35:11.946 --> 02:35:13.985
He was there every Sunday.

3068
02:35:13.985 --> 02:35:16.023
He retired from that church.

3069
02:35:16.023 --> 02:35:21.731
Mount Zion . . . And as it got older Mount Zion, their pastors come and go.

3070
02:35:21.731 --> 02:35:25.808
I know most of their pastors don't live in Mossville.

3071
02:35:25.808 --> 02:35:27.438
Some of them do.

3072
02:35:27.438 --> 02:35:29.069
Some of them don't.

3073
02:35:29.069 --> 02:35:36.000
So if they didn’t live, then they weren't involved in the civic part of the church.

3074
02:35:36.000 --> 02:35:38.876
<v ARSENEAULT>So sounds like there were a lot of traveling.

3075
02:35:38.876 --> 02:35:40.794
<v RIGGS>Yeah some of them were traveling.

3076
02:35:40.794 --> 02:35:42.392
During that time ministers travelled.

3077
02:35:42.392 --> 02:35:43.990
They were called traveling ministers.

3078
02:35:43.990 --> 02:35:44.309
<v ARSENEAULT>Ministers?

3079
02:35:44.309 --> 02:35:44.629
<v RIGGS>[agrees]

3080
02:35:44.629 --> 02:35:47.505
<v ARSENEAULT>Did y'all have tent revivals or anything like that?

3081
02:35:47.505 --> 02:35:48.144
<v RIGGS>They did.

3082
02:35:48.144 --> 02:35:58.052
They did, but basically it was people coming through town or a minister that would come through town and he would probably ask somebody could he set up his tent on

3083
02:35:58.052 --> 02:35:58.691
<c.p>their property.

3084
02:35:58.691 --> 02:35:59.969
Then people would go.

3085
02:35:59.969 --> 02:36:05.082
They would . . . He would be there two or three nights and they would go to church.

3086
02:36:05.082 --> 02:36:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there music or anything?

3087
02:36:07.000 --> 02:36:07.422
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

3088
02:36:07.422 --> 02:36:08.687
They'd have music.

3089
02:36:08.687 --> 02:36:11.639
A lot of times they would sing.

3090
02:36:11.639 --> 02:36:14.169
I mean have a . . . not recorded.

3091
02:36:14.169 --> 02:36:20.072
They had somebody maybe at the tent thing, they had somebody playing the guitar.

3092
02:36:20.072 --> 02:36:21.337
Something like that.

3093
02:36:21.337 --> 02:36:24.711
They didn't have an organ or a piano.

3094
02:36:24.711 --> 02:36:26.398
Yeah they would sing.

3095
02:36:26.398 --> 02:36:26.819
Yeah.

3096
02:36:26.819 --> 02:36:28.084
They would sing.

3097
02:36:28.084 --> 02:36:32.301
They would not have a choir because they were travelling.

3098
02:36:32.301 --> 02:36:35.675
Yeah they didn't have a . . . It was early.

3099
02:36:35.675 --> 02:36:39.470
Maybe in the early 40s and stuff like that.

3100
02:36:39.470 --> 02:36:47.482
Now I don't if somebody comes through and do the tent thing, but yeah we have had tent things.

3101
02:36:47.482 --> 02:36:51.277
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there ever any healings or anything like that?

3102
02:36:51.277 --> 02:36:53.386
<v RIGGS>I didn't go to those.

3103
02:36:53.386 --> 02:36:56.337
I mean I don't remember any healing.

3104
02:36:56.337 --> 02:36:58.024
Let me say that.

3105
02:36:58.024 --> 02:37:00.133
I don't remember any healing.

3106
02:37:00.133 --> 02:37:04.771
Now the Sanctified church that I was telling you, that church.

3107
02:37:04.771 --> 02:37:06.036
The Holiness church.

3108
02:37:06.036 --> 02:37:06.880
They do.

3109
02:37:06.880 --> 02:37:09.831
They prayed and they healed and whatever.

3110
02:37:09.831 --> 02:37:14.892
But now that was a church that my daddy's father went to.

3111
02:37:14.892 --> 02:37:18.687
But I was young so I really don't know.

3112
02:37:18.687 --> 02:37:26.277
I guess they did because . . . They did do healing and praying, and the hands on, and the oil.

3113
02:37:26.277 --> 02:37:32.602
That was called like the Holiness church is what they were called at that time.

3114
02:37:32.602 --> 02:37:37.663
Now I don't know what it's called, but that's . . . They did that.

3115
02:37:37.663 --> 02:37:43.145
So when you was visiting if they were healing then you were there.

3116
02:37:43.145 --> 02:37:45.675
<v ARSENEAULT>You're going to get prayed for.

3117
02:37:45.675 --> 02:37:46.096
<v RIGGS>[Agrees]

3118
02:37:46.096 --> 02:37:50.313
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have a favorite hymn or anything like that?

3119
02:37:50.313 --> 02:37:52.000
Any favorite songs.

3120
02:37:52.000 --> 02:37:53.569
<v RIGGS>Yeah, I like “Amazing Grace.”

3121
02:37:53.569 --> 02:37:55.451
It's one of my favorite hymns.

3122
02:37:55.451 --> 02:37:56.706
“How Great Thou Art.”

3123
02:37:56.706 --> 02:37:59.216
I have a few hymns that I like.

3124
02:37:59.216 --> 02:38:04.863
<v ARSENEAULT>I always like thinking about old churches and like the idea of a choir and stuff like that.

3125
02:38:04.863 --> 02:38:08.314
I think I've pretty much . . . We're at . . . Almost at two hours.

3126
02:38:08.314 --> 02:38:08.941
<v RIGGS>Are we?

3127
02:38:08.941 --> 02:38:11.137
<v ARSENEAULT>I hate to keep you so long.

3128
02:38:11.137 --> 02:38:12.392
Yeah, almost two hours.

3129
02:38:12.392 --> 02:38:15.843
Usually we don't go over an hour because people get tired.

3130
02:38:15.843 --> 02:38:17.412
<v RIGGS>Am I talking too much?

3131
02:38:17.412 --> 02:38:22.118
<v ARSENEAULT>But I’m keeping you here so we can get all the information out of you.

3132
02:38:22.118 --> 02:38:23.373
But we're almost done.

3133
02:38:23.373 --> 02:38:24.000
<v RIGGS>Okay.

3134
02:38:24.000 --> 02:38:29.104
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of stories would the elders tell you about Mossville,

3135
02:38:29.104 --> 02:38:31.888
<c.p>or ghosts, or anything like that?

3136
02:38:31.888 --> 02:38:33.280
Any ghost stories?

3137
02:38:33.280 --> 02:38:36.528
<v RIGGS>I don't really remember any ghost stories.

3138
02:38:36.528 --> 02:38:42.560
I'm just trying to think of what kind of stories about . . . I don't . . .

3139
02:38:42.560 --> 02:38:49.984
<v ARSENEAULT>Like the post office or anything like . . . Did they ever talk about the old post office?

3140
02:38:49.984 --> 02:38:52.304
<v RIGGS>Now this I don't know.

3141
02:38:52.304 --> 02:38:54.160
I can't document this.

3142
02:38:54.160 --> 02:38:56.944
At some time . . . At one time.

3143
02:38:56.944 --> 02:39:08.080
I don't know when it was, or how they did it, my grandfather B.C. [Berkley C. Garrett] was supposed of had a will call.

3144
02:39:08.080 --> 02:39:18.288
Now I don't know if he picked the mail up and brought it to Mossville and the people came and got it.

3145
02:39:18.288 --> 02:39:22.000
I don't think it was a sanction.

3146
02:39:22.000 --> 02:39:24.869
<v ARSENEAULT>He just did it as like a favor?

3147
02:39:24.869 --> 02:39:25.228
<v RIGGS>[Agrees].

3148
02:39:25.228 --> 02:39:29.890
I don't know if they got it off the thing from the train.

3149
02:39:29.890 --> 02:39:34.910
But I even tried to see if I could find that, but I couldn't.

3150
02:39:34.910 --> 02:39:37.421
But I was told that that happened.

3151
02:39:37.421 --> 02:39:43.876
But no, as far as a post office, Mossville never had a post office that I know of.

3152
02:39:43.876 --> 02:39:49.614
I'm just trying to think of what . . . I mean I don't know what kind of stories.

3153
02:39:49.614 --> 02:39:51.048
I don't know what . . .

3154
02:39:51.048 --> 02:39:56.786
<v ARSENEAULT>Someone told me Evergreen Road . . . They used to tell them Evergreen Road was haunted growing up.

3155
02:39:56.786 --> 02:40:00.731
<v RIGGS>Well see I don't . . . Evergreen Road is that Road Saprack Road.

3156
02:40:00.731 --> 02:40:04.676
That's the road . . . I couldn't think of the name of it.

3157
02:40:04.676 --> 02:40:05.752
I don't know.

3158
02:40:05.752 --> 02:40:08.621
It may be, but I didn't know that.

3159
02:40:08.621 --> 02:40:09.697
It may be.

3160
02:40:09.697 --> 02:40:13.641
Now there was a place out there and it's still there.

3161
02:40:13.641 --> 02:40:20.455
I know I guess it's gone now, but . . . It was an encampment that was owned by the Baptist church.

3162
02:40:20.455 --> 02:40:21.531
Black Baptist churches.

3163
02:40:21.531 --> 02:40:29.779
I've heard that that was haunted, but I never . . . I don't even know where it was, but it existed when I was there.

3164
02:40:29.779 --> 02:40:34.441
I knew where it was supposed to be, but I never went there.

3165
02:40:34.441 --> 02:40:36.593
But I've never heard of anything.

3166
02:40:36.593 --> 02:40:37.310
Well maybe.

3167
02:40:37.310 --> 02:40:41.255
Maybe . . . They always say passing a cemetery or at a cemetery . . .

3168
02:40:41.255 --> 02:40:43.766
<v ARSENEAULT>Hold your breath or something like that?

3169
02:40:43.766 --> 02:40:44.124
<v RIGGS>Yeah.

3170
02:40:44.124 --> 02:40:48.428
But see the Sanctified cemetery was closer to me than Mount Zion.

3171
02:40:48.428 --> 02:40:50.579
That's a little bit further back.

3172
02:40:50.579 --> 02:40:53.807
So I've never really heard anything of people saying.

3173
02:40:53.807 --> 02:40:54.524
Maybe so.

3174
02:40:54.524 --> 02:40:58.828
Maybe some people see . . . Those people that lived there probably say that.

3175
02:40:58.828 --> 02:41:03.131
But I didn't go up there at night so I wouldn't know.

3176
02:41:03.131 --> 02:41:06.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Any other stories they would tell, or?

3177
02:41:06.000 --> 02:41:08.250
<v RIGGS>I don't know of any other.

3178
02:41:08.250 --> 02:41:09.750
Unless you tell them.

3179
02:41:09.750 --> 02:41:13.500
You say it I might remember, but I don't remember.

3180
02:41:13.500 --> 02:41:15.000
<v ARSENEAULT>A haunted sycamore tree?

3181
02:41:15.000 --> 02:41:17.625
A man with no face on Evergreen?

3182
02:41:17.625 --> 02:41:18.750
<v RIGGS>Oh, that’s Evergreen?

3183
02:41:18.750 --> 02:41:19.125
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

3184
02:41:19.125 --> 02:41:19.500
<v RIGGS>Okay.

3185
